      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>tattrs to the abitar.</head>
        <p>tattrs to the abitar.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CI Tag Eorros of The Examinee" invites coma spondencs on all subjects of public interest. Lomas shou:d be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information. not abuse.</p>
        <p>IRRESPON DENTS. LwrrEas must in all cases be accompanied by the name and address of the writer. COIIMVINICATIONEI received early in the week— except urgent matters—can always be given the best attention.</p>
        <p>a clear judgment as to the weight of religious thought. I maintain it is not playing the game to talk as if the Churches generally were against military training, when it is only this very small minority which is said to be opposed to the movement. In conclusion. I should like to refer members of the Labour party to the speech made last April by Senator Pearce, the Defence Minister in the Australian Labour Government, respon- sible for the National Service Act. Senator Pearce stated that "on the general policy of defence, Liberal and Labour parties mere agreed." I wish also to draw the attention of members of the Liberal party to the statement recently made by Sir R. Munro Ferguson, late Liberal M.P. for Leith Boroughs, "that he viewed with full sympathy the methods adopted in Australia for naval and military defences," and, finally. I would commend to all my fellow. citizens the admission of Sir lan Hamilton, made last month after his review of the Australian Forces. that " in spite of contrary views which he had published in the past, the compulsory principle is succeeding in practice." GEORGE CROSFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WOKEN AND EQUAL WAGES FOR EQUAL WORK.</head>
        <p>WOKEN AND EQUAL WAGES FOR EQUAL WORK.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your issue of 28th February, under the heading "Women's Questions," an article dealing with "Equal wages for equal work" is to be found. I must congratulate you on your belief that women do take an interest in °the/ things beside clothes and food, i.e., how to pre- pare it for others especially, but feel that I must relieve your mind as to the direful things that a condition of "Equal wages for equal work" might produce. The Postmaster-General seems to have stated that under such conditions women: alone would be employed in the Post Offices, but this is not the case in the countries where the condition prevails. Then you state—" As a rule, a woman need only work to keep herself." I beg to ipestion that statement. If you will inquire into the conditions of the women who work I aink in , the majority of cases you will bud that some such a one as the following is dependent on her; I. A widow's (her) children. 2. An invalid parent or husband. 3. A delicate sister or brother. 4. The need for supplementing a parnnes wages to keep the house going. 5. A child's education. Or a score of other things might be mentioned. In regard to the last-mentioned, it was found that in a school in Whitechapel 80 per cent. of the children were being kept, fed and clothed by women. Women may be "indispensable in only a limited number of cases," but what about the women's work which has been taken out of the I homes into the factory?—the manufacture of cloth, clothes, jams, pickles, soap, candles, and a quantity of other interesting things, which have been taken from the home and into the factory, where men calmly tell women they ought not to want to go. And then, finally, you give Tour ease away by stating that "if equal wages were paid ft equal work" in a great majority of oases em ployers would prefer men to women workers." That is the real argument for equal wages. If the man's wages were really sufficient for the needs of the family the great majority of women would infinitely prefer to stay at home. The alternative to going out to work now is, very frequently, to stay at home and starve. D. B. PEMBERTON.</p>
        <p>emwes exa-4:,-4ate</p>
        <p>AUSTRALIA AND RATIONAL SERVICE. Sir,—Mr. Barry's reply to my letter is simply a retreat from the impossible position in which he placed himself by his inaccurate speech at the Co-operative Hall. He has evidently pur- posely confused the issue by mixing up amend- ments about details with abandonment of the whole principle of compulsory military train- ing. No one denies that there is a certain amount of agitation in Australia with a view to getting certain details of the Australian Defence Act amended, but what Act of any consequence in this country has not required amendment in some shape or form after it,has been tried in practice? Mr. Barry states that fully twenty candidates at the last election op- posed compulsory military training, and im- mediately afterwards he refers to a candidate (evidently, from the context, one of these twenty) who advocates the amendment of the Act. This is just an instance of the confusion of thought and the slipperiness of argument in which my opponent wallows. As aforesaid, amendment of details and abandonment of prin- ciple are two totally different things, and no amount of juggling can make them the same. I repeat what I said in my former letter, that, at the General Election of May 31st, 1913, there were only three candidates who definitely opposed the principle of compulsory military, training—three Revolutionary Socialists, each of whom polled so few votes that he forfeited his deposit. The partisan character of Mr. Barry's case is shcrvrn by the callous way in which he avoids any reference to the result of the very impor- tant recent by-election in Adelaide. There were, only two candidates—one supporting the system of National Service, and the other, described as a rep resentative of the "Freedom Lettgue," Opposing it. The former scored over 10,000 votes to his opponent's less than 2,000. In Aus- tralia, unless a candidate obtains one-fifth of the total number of votes polled by the success- ful candidate he loses the deposit (X 25 Which he has to put up when nominated. On this occa- sion, therefore, the opponent to Australia's military system lost both the election and the deposit, and incidentally helped to Rhow very clearly how Mr. Barry misled his audience when he said that National Service was not populaz in Australia. Mr. Barry's failure even to refer to so significant an election is what I mean when I say that the truth is suppressed. The Responsibility of the Labour Party.—Mr. Barry still tries to make out that the Labour party is not responsible for the Defence Act. It is true that, as usual, it is not quite clear what he does mean, for, in the opening sentences of his letter, he acknowledges that the Australiaa Labour party passed National Service into law • but in the very next sentence he writes that the Bill was not their work. Presumably he 'nears that the Labour party did not outline the Bill in the first instance. In doing so, hirwever, he calmly ignores my statement of fact—which l challenge him to deny—that on April 9.4 th, 1907, Mr. W. M. Hughes. M.P., then Attorney-General • in the Labour Ministry of the Australian Com- monwealth, outlined the Labour party's views on universal military training, and during the subsequent three years was one of the moatl active propagandists in winning the approval of the Australian people to the principle of cum, pulsory military training. If any of your readers would like to have a copy o• f Mr. Hughes' speech and will apply to me, I shall be pleased to let him or her have one. _</p>
        <p>Ministers of Religion.—Mr. Barry agrees that the Presbyterian, Anglican, Catholic and Metho- dist Churches are in favour of compulsory mili- tary training. These number 87.45 per cent. of the Christian population of the Commonwealth. lie then goes on to refer to the others who, lie states, are against, viz., Congregationalists, 13aptists, Seventh Day Adventists, Church of Christ, and Friends. According to the census returns the Baptists and Congregationalists to- gether form 3.84 per cent, of the population, and the Society of Friends is evidently so small that no separate record is given. Ido not give.theee figures with the slightest desire to belittle these denominations, but in order to render possible,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>PLAYGROUNDS AND RESTING-PLACES FOR</head>
        <p>PLAYGROUNDS AND RESTING-PLACES FOR</p>
        <p>CRAPPENHALL AND STOCKTON HEATH. Sir,—Although Warrington itself makes an effort to provide for the comfort and conveni- ence of its childhood and age in supplying open spaces, seats and shelters, to a limited extent, I am often struck with the fact that the surround- ing parishes of Grappenhall, Latchford With- out and Stockton Heath make no effort even to provide a plank seat, where a tired mother can rest while she nurses her child, or an old man or woman who has walked or taken a car from town, can sit, while they breathe the pure coun- try air. In the summer I frequently see such people sitting in the hedge bottoms, which is the only -place on which they can recline, and I wonder what our parish councillors are for, if not to remedy such grievances? You may walk for miles in this district and never find a seat, and yet the open spaces are plentiful if you only look for them, and XS or £lO would provide several suitable wooden seats. The waste ground near "The Glen," at Knutsford-road Swing Bridge, and alongside the Ship Canal between Grappenhall and Stockton Heath; the Ackers Pits, if properly laid out and attended to, would make an ideal little park, and no doubt a nurseryman who lives near would look after them for a moderate pay- ment. At present they are of no use to any- one. Again, why do not such districts purchase land before it gets too costly, and lay it out in parks and playgrounds, as our children and grown-up people are being driven off the roads by the dangerous traffic, and must take their recreation elsewhere? Are we so busy money- grubbing that we cannot afford the few pounds necessary to provide for the comforts and amenities of life? We are very far behind Con- tinental countries in this respect, and also many of the similarly situated districts in our own country. I suppose it is no use appealing to our parish councillors; will not some other body of influ- ential persons take the lead in remedying this defect before the coming summer? GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>' "7"..cu• MR. CROSFIELD AND MR. McKENNA. Sir,—l thoroughly enjoyed .Mr. .Crosfield's in- dictment of the "loyalist" party in last week's "FA:miner," but I liked still better his article the previous week, calling attention to Mr. McKenna's "do-nothing" policy. It makes one hope there is still some meaning in the word " Liberal " when men like Mr. Crosfield have courage to criticise so-called Liberal Ministers. As a writer in one of the papers recently said, "Mr. McKenna, as usual, replied that he could do nothing. He never can do anything. There has not been such an all-round helpless fellow since Pantagruel ate the jellyfish. There is a considerable feeling that Mr. McKenna ought to be made a peer, or given to Mrs. Pankhurst, or something of that sort." Is it not time that Liberals (real Liberals) realised that the Cabinet as at present consti- tuted, is miles away from Liberalism? I refer to the imprisonment of Labour speakers for indiscreet speeches; the prosecution of street orators who do not happen to believe in the particular type of divinity which it is conven- tional in this country to—at any rate, pretend to—believe in; the torturing of women who. however misguided in their policy, show a height of self-sacrifice which has not been equalled in our country's history for genera- tions; the passing by a trick of such a horrible measure as the "Cat and Mouse" Act, on the plea that it was to take the place of forcible feeding, whereas it was merely intended to be an additional torture. And compared with all these, not a single action taken against men of Position who publicly and flagrantly preach and tPoregkr7a for t open et r a rebellion nd Na and do diond f h theirai utmostt etheir h Call Se.</p>
        <p>A Prime Minister, that "great democrat who believes that democracy is for one sex only, and refuses to see deputations of representa- tive men who wish to convey to him the views of many thousands of men and women in the North. ire had nothing further to add to what he had already said. They did not wish to hear what he had to say, but to let him know what they, and the thousands they represented. thought and felt. Such action is that of a dictator, not a leader. A Chancellor of the Exchequer, who is acclaimed a great apostle of freedom, and yet prided himself on having " torpedoed " the only measure which had a chance of passing through the Commons to broaden the franchise so as to break down the sex barrier. And a First Lord of the Admiralty, appointed by the party of "Peace, retrenchment and reform," bringing in naval estimates absolutely unprecedented in amount, and defying the House of Commons by spending nearly three millions of money without any sanction from the Commons at all. If these things represent Liberalism, as known to-day, the sooner a party truly representative of the democracy of this country is formed the better.</p>
        <p>J. S. TILLING.</p>
        <p>Beweey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 14, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 14, 1914.</p>
        <p>"LITTLE EL DORADO."</p>
        <p>Warrington One of the Wealthiest Towns in Lancashire.</p>
        <p>" ONE OF THE STINGIEST" SAYS</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>It was with difficulty a quorum was main- tained at Warrington Town Council meeting on Tuesday to pass the annual Budget. When Councillor Wilkinson rose to make his annual financial speech the councillors trooped from the Chamber.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILNINSON protested at the mem- bers "running away."</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : Yes, it is a pity, for it is the most important matter we have discussed during the past few months. Councillor WILKINSON: We have had some twaddle talked this week and last. I am only sorry I did not put the rates at Bs. 6d. instead of Bs.; it would have made you beggars serious. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>, As Councillor Wilkinson was making his speech, Alderman TINSION interjected : Don't read it; hand' it to the Press. Councillor WILKINSON: I don't know what Alderman Tinnion would say if I treated him like that. Councillor PEMBERTON : He'd say a lot</p>
        <p>MONEY OUT OF PROFITS. Councillor WILKINSON said the proposal was to levy Bs. in the X, the same as last year. That, he thought, would be satisfactory to every-one, as considering what was .being done at Present in the town it might reasonably have been ex- pected that an increased rate would be required. The extra expenditure of the committees called for 0f.:5,610 more than last year, in addition to the sum of £1,849 which it was then proposed to take out of the improvement rate balance. They were, however, not yet at the end of the financial year, and so were not in a position at present to say how far that call on the balance would have been made, but as there had been several increases in receipts which were not anticipated, it was expected that the major portion of the balance woultl still remain to be utilised this year, and it was proposed now to stake ..£3,93olfrojn it. As to the increased expenditure of £5,610, it was proposed to raise it as follows: £l,OOO extra from the Tramways, making £2,500 in all; £l,OOO from the Electricity Department; -ON° from the Water Department; and £2,328 WaB estimated to result from the increment of the ,rate. The difference would. be met by drawing upon the borough rate balance for £7Ol, and by taking £Bl further from the improvement rate balance.</p>
        <p>• It was gratifying to find that all the under- takings of the Corporation—gas, tramways, electricity, and water—were contributing to enable them to levy the same rate as last year. It was the first occasion that this had been done. On the borough rate the chief increases were: Police and administration of justice, from 4.79 d. to 5.56 d.; baths, .80d. to 2.02 d.; educa- tion, 18.84 d. to 22.61 d.; bridges, .15d. to .89d. On the improvement rate the increases were : Health, 2.54 d. to 3.08 d.; paving and care of streets, 9.54 d. to 12.01 d.; improvements of streets, 11.97 d. to 14.05 d.; parks and recreation grounds, 2.70 d. to 3.24 d.</p>
        <p>"A WRONG PRINCIPLE." Councillor OSBORNE expressed his regret that the empty state of the. room showed the coun- cillors took little interest'in a most important Budget. He did not agree that the trading con- cerns ought to be called upon for contributions towards the relief of the rates. He was sur- prised to find that even the water undertaking had to provide 1.100 this year. It was a wrong Principle, for it appeared to him that the Cuun- ad Ought to make the water as cheap as possible, and thus give the advantage to the users. In regard to the dernand upon the tramways sufficient money was not being set apart for the renewal of lines. The rails on the Sankey and Cemetery routes were already a disgrace to the town, and he was told that the cost of renewal would be .P. 3,000 per mile. As to the gas under- taking, they were assured a short time ago that they would deplete the funds if they made con- cessions to consumers, and that they might pos- sibly have forced upon them an extra shilling rate. They even heard from one alderman that fuel was going to cost the department £3,000 more in the coming year, and that the sale of by-products was expected to yield about £2,000 le€s: yet the gas undertaking was actually pro- viding over .12.13,000 for the relief of rates. Alderman Jour; EVANS said that only £lO,OOO of that amount was actual profit for the year. The balance had been taken from money in hand.</p>
        <p>REDUCED GAS PRICES. Councillor OsaoHNE agreed that if the Gas Department could contribute -43,000 to the relief of rates, it ought to he in a position to reduce price to consumers. The Electricity De- partment, which was barely self-supporting, was to be called upon for -4:1,000. This method of disposing of profits was bad finance, and the time was coming when each department would have to recognise some settled basis of contri- bution, as they had in Manchester. It was not fair that, when the Finance Committee found they wanted a certain amount to keep the rates down, they should take it from departments which could ill-afford to .give it. (Hear, hear.) Councillor PEMBERTON thought the financial statement was most alarming. Although Coun- cillor Wilkinson had sugared the pill to a great extent, the fact remained that it was a very nasty and ugly pill. The position was getting very serious. Councillor Wilkinson had simply .'got home by the skin of his teeth"; by a little bit of dodging he had just managed to keep the rates at Bs.</p>
        <p>II Under the new valuation the Corporation con- cerns were actually being rated at .0,084 more than before, and that meant a further call upon the profits. They were keeping the rates at Bs., but at what a cost? Water, electricity and gas should be sold at moderate prices, as compared with other towns, but he also contended that the ratepayers, as owners of the undertakings, had a perfect right to the profits.</p>
        <p>"A LITTLE EL DORADO." Councillor WILKINSON said if the ratepayers were not to receive any relief from the Cor- poration undertakings, why on earth should they own them? The Electricity and Tramways Departments were only being touched to the extent of .£7OO on the current year's working, which could hardly be regarded as very cruel. Without the help of the municipal undertak- ings the rates would have risen to Bs. 4d. There is no doubt that Warrington is sound. She is a little El Dorado--one of the wealthiest towns in Lancashire. The MAYOR: And one of the stingiest. (Laugh- ter.) .</p>
        <p>The rates at Bs. in the mously. were passed unani.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>DISFIGURING, ITCHING ECZEMA.</head>
        <p>DISFIGURING, ITCHING ECZEMA.</p>
        <p>47, Enid-street, Bermondsey, London, S.E., I tense pain from eczema. It was of a dry, scaly Eng.—" For three years or more I suffered in- form, disfiguring and itching. It began from a small spot about the size of a threepenny-piece just above the ankle and appeared in small red blotches. It spread to my knees and arms caus- ing great pain by the continuous itching arid terrible burning. It looked like a mass of red sores. I could get no sleep night after night. I saw the Cuticura Soap and Ointment adver- tised and it gave me great relief when I the sample. So I got three boxes each of the tried Cuticura Soap and Ointment which completely cured me. "My baby's complaint came in a red rash all over the lower part of the body. Thanks to- Cuticura Soap and Ointment I caught it in time. She is now quite clear all over." (Signed) W. Charlesworth, July 9, 1913. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A ' sample of each with 32-p. book post free. Ad- dress: F. Newbery and Sone, 27, Charterliouse- square, London. or Potter Drug and Chem. Corp., Boston, V.S.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>TOWN PLANNING RELAXED.</head>
        <p>TOWN PLANNING RELAXED.</p>
        <p>Town Council Depart From Their Former Scheme.</p>
        <p>CRITICAL POSITION CREATED.</p>
        <p>The much-discussed question of how many houses should be erected per acre in Warring- ton was further discussed at a meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesdly. It arose through Councillor lieNAdfu moving:— " That the following resolution of the Council of the 4th day of February, 1913, be oo.spended until the scheme authorised to be made under the Housing, Town Planning &amp;c., Act, 1909, has been submitted to the Council: That under the Housing, Town Planning, &amp;c., Act, 1909, no houses shall be erected within the borough in excess of a maximum of 20 to the acre."</p>
        <p>As this wa-s adopted the Warrington Council were warned at a conference in Liverpool on Wednesday that they had put themselves in a critical position.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>HIS THREE REASONS.</head>
        <p>HIS THREE REASONS.</p>
        <p>Councillor HENSHALL gave the following three reasons for moving such a resolution: (1) That the proposal to limit 20 houses to the acre was not practical; (2) it was needless; and (3) those who were afraid that if the resolution was passed that builders and landowners would rush in with more'houses per acre based their case on a groundless fear.</p>
        <p>The rents in Warrington were from five to 10 per cent, lower thandn similar Lancashire towns and the town planning of land already partially developed was impractical and needless. It was surely not unreasonable to ask that before they exchanged the English idea of building for the German idea they should first obtain some exact information. They should get to know what the outer districts were doing. In Stockton Heath it had been decided to hang up their scheme for six months, and unless Warrington also hung up its scheme they would land in the soup, to the benefit of that area in the Runcorn Rural district.</p>
        <p>Rushing Out, Not In. It was said if the resOlution was passed builders would rush in and get their own plans passed. If there had been any rushing in the building trade recently, it had been not rushing in, but rushing out. , In' Warrington in 1908 they built 241 houses, 1909 they built 237, 1910 saw 202, 1911 there were 221; in 1912 the number was 99, and in 1913 the number was 89. These figures were not so bad as the actual facts, for the only building going on was where they got landlords with the land round their necks—they were not on the land, but the land was on them. (Laughter.) When the present landowners got rid of their land there would be an end to building. (" No.") The German idea of town planning was chang- ing completely, as was shown in ar.i article on the dark side of German town planning.</p>
        <p>The Dark Side. This article stated: "The account of German town planning, which Mr. Charles Reade, of the Garden; Cities antl. Town Planning Association, gave at the Royal Institute of British Archi- tects was informing almost to excess, so detailed was it.</p>
        <p>"Two points, however, emerged very clearly, namely, .the length to which land speculations has gone in Germany, and the utter inadequacy 'of the old ideal of town planning 'which made' wide streets, vistas, and green squares, its be-all, and end-all.</p>
        <p>"Mr. Reade dealt in turn with Cologne, Frankfort, Leipsic, Munich, and Berlin, and of each the same tale fell-to be told, with mere variations of detail. Fine, straight streets, and avenues, are there to impress-• the stranger who does not penetrate through tunnel entrances to the air- less 'backs.' In other words, in Germany town planning is divorced from housing. Thus we find such scandals in Berlin as a room 6ft. high, 13ft. long, and Sift. wide, in which are housed a consumptive youth and his mother. "Rents generally are higher, and accommo- dation worse in Germany than they are here."</p>
        <p>A Vista, Not a Villa. It was his opinion that town planning was impractical. The German ideals showed that when they asked for a villa they gave them a vista. (Laughter.) In all the history of War- rington there never was a period when they ,should do less to hamper the builder than at present. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Wu.KlNsox, in seconding, said houses built on such a system meant another 3d. per week on a ss. house, which would have to come out of the workers' pockets. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>AN IMPORTANT QUESTION.</head>
        <p>AN IMPORTANT QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Alderman -Toms EvAxs said they could not discuss a more important question than the hous- ing of the people. They had in Warrington 3,000 houses let at 4s. per week, and there were 5,000 unskilled labourers in the borough who earned on the average 245. per week. Only one thing -would happen if houses Were built under town planning. The already limited income of the unskilled labourer was going to be taxed, and his children were to have less food, in order that more rent might be paid. (Councillor Pemberton: "Shame.")</p>
        <p>He considered the physique and health of the children more important than artificial condi- tions and a fine vista. (Applause.) What was the use of providing a villa at 7s. esl. per week for people who wanted cottages at 45.2 If cottages were not provkled, two or three fami- lies would have to live in one house, and thus be a menace to health.</p>
        <p>COMMON-SENSE WANTED. Councillor JENTans said they wanted to apply common-sense in the consideration of that mat- ter. They could not all afford to live in cloud- land like Alderman Bennett. The town planning scheme was an impossible thing, and it was impossible to develop land on those lines.</p>
        <p>COIITICiBOr PEMBERTON said slums came about through the absence of fresh air. There were houses built last year which in ten years would he slums. They would never settle the question of the housing of the poor by town planning; it was purely a wage question. (hear, hear.) Alderman BURTON said one point was that if they insisted upon 20 houses per acre they would force the builders to build outside the borough, and that would take the respectable artisans outside and leave the very poor people to. pay the borough rates. The whole thing was wrong.</p>
        <p>A COUNCILLOR SURPRISED. Coun. IT4WTHORN was surprised that a progres- sive measure should receive such poor support. That discussion should really have taken place when they dealt with town planning in Febru- ary, 1913. Suppose they had 20 houses to the acre, and five persons to the house; that would give 100 to the acre. Was that not near over- crowding? Their system of building long rows of brick houses with their vitiated atmosphere created slums. They must also remember that the day of 4s. per week houses had gone. The resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WARRINGTON WARNED.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WARNED.</p>
        <p>At a Town Planning Conference, representa- tive of the whole country, held at Liverpool on Wednesday, the position created in Warrington by the above decision was discussed. Councillor PEMBERTON asked for information in regard to the position in Warrington. Mr. RAYMOND UNWIN, the conference chairman, said the Warrington Town Council, by relaxing a prospective town planning scheme so RR to fall back upon the ojd conditions of building 45 cottages instead of 20 to the acre, had placed themselves in a difficult position with the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>The builders might have a remedy against the Council, and it was doubtful whether the Surveyor would sanction plans under the pre- sent circumstances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>"EDUCATION FANATICS": The Other Side of the Picture,</head>
        <p>"EDUCATION FANATICS": The Other Side of the Picture,</p>
        <p>Sir,—l gather from your last Saturday's issue that the few disquieted spirits of the Warring- ton Town Council, who periodically explode, with the obvious purpose of relieving a condi- tion of mind which is associated with extra- ordinarily high tension, have once more hurled their fierce anathemas in the direction of the Education Committee. The otherwise calm atmosphere of the Council Chamber was made to hum with the doleful prognostications of those who felt they could not "prophesy unto us smooth things?' The evil days were coming, hurried on by the "education fanatics," to I whom "money was no object." Wild declarations of "wilful extravagance" uttered in general terms, in the absence of proof, simply amount to nothing better than a display of boisterous "piffle."</p>
        <p>If there is one direction more than another in which the ratepayers, including the thoughtful section of the working classes, wish to be gener- ous, it is in the direction of providing the fullest opportunities for the higher education of theiz children. The truth has gradually, but surely, dawned upon them that the child of rich parents has not necessarily more brain power than the child that is born and .reared in humble .ur- roundings. The "blue blood theory" they know to be a farce, and are aware that the "giants among men" in the near future v ill be giants of intellect, provided, of Course, UNA due care has been given to their physizl up- building. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>--- - Some will be surprised to learn that those extravagant spendthrifts, the "education fanatics" of Warrington, are actually making provision in cases where exceptional mental ability is displayed, for giving the fullest op- portunities—without regard to rank or class— for its development. In the autumn of this year a beginning will be made, and two of our young people—who may come from the homes of .working men—will be sent from ,the Secondary Schools to one of the Universities.</p>
        <p>How some of us, who have had to fight for a little education against great odds, would have rejoiced, if the species of fanaticism exhibited by the Warrington Education Committee, had been a little more in evidence a generation ago</p>
        <p>As a possible lonic that might help to dispel the pessimism that disturbs a small section of our councillors, I would recommend a tour of in- spection, commencing with the infant section of one of our best equipped Council Schools, and concluding in the Secondary Schools. They would see buildings equipi)ed with the most modern appliances—considered from ' the hygienic point of view—and methods employed which compare with those of the old days as does the express train with the stage coach. They would discover that the scholars are much hap- pier and healthier than was the case under the, old condition&amp; It is now but rarely that one sees the boy that the great poet depicted—a familiar figure a few years ago—" crawling like a snail unwillingly to school." At the very initiation of a child its interest is aroused; by a gradual process the mental powers are awak- ened and the serious business of study is entered upon almost imperceptibly. And what an inspiring picture would be pre- sented if we could induce these gentlemen to attend one of those rare gatherings when the students of the Secondary Schools are assembled together. They would meet the sons and daugh- ters of parents representing every section of the community, whose faces, aglow with intelli- gence and hopefulness, would, I think, make them feel that there are better days in store not only for Warrington, but for the nation, of which we are all so proud. Such scenes bring the assurance that the growing power of the democracy will result in better things Than we have hitherto known, because it will be linked with an ever-ascending, ever-widening know- ledge of the things that make for the highest good and most symmetrical development of the race. _ _ .</p>
        <p>G. DALE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CATHOLIC LECTURES:</head>
        <p>CATHOLIC LECTURES:</p>
        <p>Reply to Rev. H. Ogden's Recent Criticisms.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The Rev. H. Ogden takes exception to the use of the word Catholic. He admits that the word Catholic means "universal," ." cos- mopolitan," &amp;c. But I would ask him, Is his Church universal or cosmopolitan? Who was the founder of his Church? Not Jesus Christ. There was a religion 1,600 years before his belief came into existence. That was the Catholic Church of to-day, embracing all nationalities. Again, Mr. Ogden asks would he be admitted to the Mass or Communion. To the former, cer- tainly. Protestants very often attend Mass in the Catholic Church. But to the Communion, certainly not. In breparation for the reception of the Holy Eucharist, confession of sins is necessary, a doctrine I presume Mr. Ogden dis- believes. I would refer him to 1 Cor. xi. 23,30, wherein our Divine Lord says "Whosoever shall eat this bread or drink of the chalice of the Lord unworthily, shall be guilty of the body and blood of the Lord, for he that eateth and drinketh unworthily. eateth and drinketh judgment to himself." Therefore the Catholic Church could not allow anyone, who does not believe, to eat and drink unworthily of the divine flesh and blood. As to the invitation to attend his church to receive Communion, I am sure there are not many who will take advantage of this generous offer. . Mr. Ogden attacks the dectrine of the Real Presence, as believed in by the Catholic Church. On this one point I will take my stand by that arch-apostate, Martin Luther, and defy Mr. Ogden and the world to say that the words of Jesus Clp.rist, "This is My body, this is My ' blood," were symbolical. Again our Divine Lord said: "I will give you My flesh to eat, for the life of the world." How could He do it? The Catholic Church believes that Jesus Christ was God, and he committed this sacred trust: "To do this in remembrance of Me." Do what? I To "give His body and blood for the life of the world." "And I will be with you all days, even to the consummation of the world." Referring to the Last Supper, Mr. Ogden says, "The minister or bishop blessed the elements, and the deacon handed them round." How un- true and misleading this is can be proved by I -scanning. the pages of any Christian Bible, wherein we read that Christ (God) blessed and broke, and He (Christ) .gave to His disciples, saying, "This is My body, this is My blood." 1 Our Lord's meaning is fully borne out in John vi. 56, 59, when He said, "My flesh is meat in- I deed, and My blood is drink indeed; he that eateth My flesh and drinketh My blood abideth in Me and I in him. As the Father has sent I Me, and I live by the Father, so he that eateth Me the same.also shall live by Me. This is the bread that came down from heaven; not as your fathers did eat manna in the desert (symbolic- ally) and are dead. He that eateth this bread shall live for ever." So you see that Mr. Ogden's assertion, that Christ did not mean what He said, absolutely falls to the ground, or , else Mr. Ogden denies that our Divine Lord was truth itself.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ogden did not relish the gibe, by one of the lecturers, at the Church of England, but upon inquiring further he will find it is an absolute fact, that a considerable number of Church of England ministers have a decided leaning towards the doctrine of the Real Pres- ence.</p>
        <p>The attitude of the Free Churches I cannot define, when they, without authority, con- demnation or censure, hold the most contradic- tory tenets on fundamental questions. We had an example a few weeks ago, when one of their most' famous preachers publicly announced his disbelief in the miraculous birth of Christ. This one example brings us to the conclusion tkiat the Free Churches have no belief what- eve. Their Christianity, to my mind, can be summed up in a few words, viz.: "Sing the praises of God, read not your Bible in case you are misled, and eternal happiness will assuredly be yours." J.D.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Warrington,. March 10, 1914.</head>
        <p>Warrington,. March 10, 1914.</p>
        <p>COUNCIL SQUABBLE.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion and Councillor Pemberton Have a Sharp Debate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>PAVING QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE PAVING QUESTION.</p>
        <p>A tedious discussion ensued at the Warring- ton Town Council on Tuesday over the question of paving secondary streets. The torrent of words arose over the following proposal of the Paving Committee: "That, for the purposes of experiment, and in view of a silent pavement being desirable around the Oakwood-avenue School, the following streets be paved with asphalt—Steel-street, Oakwood-avenue, Elm- wood-avenue, Pinewood-avenue, Ashwood- avenue, Briarwood-avenue." The subject was under discussion last week, when the Council adjourned, Councillor Goron having moved the matter back. Continuing his speech, he con- tended that the Council should help the pro- perty owners .rather than hamper them.</p>
        <p>Councillor CRATE( held that tarred limestone should be used rather than more expensive material with which the committee desired the property, owners to pave the streets. , Councillor HENEIECALL contended that " the overburdened property owner" should have some consideration. He thought in the second- ary streets the carriage way should be reduced to 18 feet, with 11 feet wide aide-walks on each side, and that would reduce the cost of making the roadway 30 per cent. Councillor ROBERTS suggested waiting until they saw the result of an experiment to be made with different materials. Some materials had been recently introduced which were excellent sobstitutes for granite setts in streets with light tra.ffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>NOT HIS GOVERNMENT.</head>
        <p>NOT HIS GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said that the scheme now suggested by the Pavino, Committee had been carried by a majority of one. Recently great improvements had been made in tar ,macadam for paving purposes. The-Local (bov- ernment Board, at a conference at Westminster on Town Planning, had made it dear that they intended that the cost of street-making should be reduced, with a view to encouraging the erection of dwellings for the working classes. Alderman Tinnion should learn what his own Government was doing. Alderman Tninrox : I beg to say that it is not my Government. Councillor PEMBERTON : I am very glad that Alderman Tinnion does not belong to that party. (Laughter.) The MAiroa : Beep to the point, please, Coun- cillor Pemberton. Councillor PEMBERTON made an appeal for better treatment for the builders. Some people seemed to treat builders as smugglers and coun- terfeit coin makers, but the Council ought to do everything they could to cheapen, within reasonable limits, the cost of street-making and to encourage house-building.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CHALLENGE TO BUILDERS.</head>
        <p>CHALLENGE TO BUILDERS.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON said the Council were ,now asked to drop suddenly from efficiency to inefficiency. The life of tarred limestone was unknown to any member of the Council; it was anything from five to ten years. Would a builder, at the end of ten years, come forward and pay the difference in the cost if it was found necessary, to-bave the road made properly? No, he would have gone, and the property would have changed hands.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION said his object had been, and always would be—and he said this honestly and conscientiously—the best interests of the property owners and the ratepayers generally. He hoped he should have the credit at any rate of being transparently honest. He repudiated any suggestion that he had been actuated by a motive. One of the deputation had said that so far as he was concerned the committee could pave with limestone asphalt and charge him the cost of granite pavement. He asked: "Would you mind putting that proposal in writing?" but up to the present time that had not been done. The proposal might have been serious, but not serious enough to put in writ- ing. He had been much criticised, more parti- cularly by Councillor Pemberton. Charges had been/ made by Mr. Pemberton against him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>LIVELY SCENE.</head>
        <p>A LIVELY SCENE.</p>
        <p>" Councillor Pemberton," deliberately said Alderman Tinnion, "should be the" very last man in this world to make charges against me or anyone .else" Councillor Psmssaros : Why, last man? _ _ why? Why the</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION : You have made charges, sir, against the Surveyor. Councillor PEMBERTON : I think Alderman Tinnion should justify that or withdraw. Alderman TINNION : You charged the Surveyor with having arranged for the paving, and that I had influenced him. Councillor PEMBERTON (excitedly): It was at your instigation. Alderman TINNION : I never spoke to the Sur- veyor about it. Councillor PEMBERTON : Then it's a shame to put it into his mouth. Alderman TINNION : You are wrong, sir. It was in the report that came before the com- mittee. COUILCiIIOT PEMBERTON : PECI not wrong.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>PERSONAL MATTER.</head>
        <p>A PERSONAL MATTER.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINIqION : This seems to be becoming a personal matter between Pemberton and myself. Councillor PEMBERTON : This is intolerable. Alderman Trsiaos: It has been a personal matter for some time. Councillor P ERTON (excitedly): Mr. Mayor, EMB I claim your indulgence. I ask him to justify or withdraw. Why am I the last man to make charges? What is against me? Let us know 'what it is! I won't have these insults! I 'Alderman Tirstox : I have not insulted you. I simply said you should be the last man to I make any charge. Councillor Psainenrox : Why? Alderman Tixsiox : You know why. I leave that to yourself. I Councillor PEMBERTON (angrily): I want to know why ! Alderman TINNIox : You are not getting to know why from me. Councillor PEMBERTON : It only shows what personal things I've got to stand. (Laughter) The vote resulted in the amendment being defeated by tbree votes, the figures being 10 for and 13 against the amendment. This means that the streets named will be paved with granited asphalt.</p>
      </div>
