        <p>Scientifically Fitted GLASSES Sight Testing Optician, 84, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON. Steel Spectacles from 216. Rolled Gold from 8/6.</p>
        <p>Hours of Business 9 to 7-30. Saturday 9to 9-30. Please Note.—Mr. Watson attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11-0 to 4-30; Wednesday 11-0 to 5-30; Thursday 11-0 to 1-0; Friday and Saturday 11-0 to 5-30.</p>
        <p>POULTRY PEN.</p>
        <p>Hints and Interesting Facts and</p>
        <p>Chat for Amateurs.</p>
        <p>" EXAMINER " SPECIAL.</p>
        <p>Several of the best known breeds described in our last issue, but the list had been, by any means, exhausted. As then $ the Wyandottes are at present extremely p( lar and have figured remarkably well in laying competitions of the Utility Club. T</p>
        <p>chief rivals are the Orpingtons, and I note the last monthly competition the latter 119 given the Wyandottes a close run for preo position. in point of laying powers.</p>
        <p>There are several -varieties of Orpl known reepectively as the Black, Buff, _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Jubilee and Spangled. The Buff are, per the more popular. They are as a rule re' layers and make excellent table fowls.</p>
        <p>AS WINTER LAYERS. A strong feature in favour of the Buff</p>
        <p>tons is their adaptability as winter layers, a probably this feature commends them ra strongly. Welt-built pullets of eight mool old usually start laying at the end of Octal and keep at tt right through the winter. TI ___ _ a</p>
        <p>are thus valuable birds when the eggs Call 11 a ready market at 4or 5 a is. These birds bo one drawback, howeve4 and it is that in ra winter weather, such as we have experiellt this season, they are inclined to be broody. The White Orpington is another fine fowl keep, and between this class and the Buff tlll seems little to choose. They lay well and 3 quite as useful for culinary purposes. Both 6 hardy types of birds, well suited to stand t vagaries of the climate in the North of 133 land.</p>
        <p>LEGHORNS AND VARIETIES.' The Leghorn is a popular bird in the Warrilt ton district, and those who keep this breed /0'; nothing but praise for them. There are ØO six or seven varieties, chief amongst which the Brown. White. Buff and Black. Of al! te. varieties the White seems the most poPot° and possibly • this is due to its value as a,.laYer.) An average from each hen in the matter _ laying is about 196 eggs per hen per annoy There are a few breeds that can beat this, bi there are no more consistent layers. The Black Leghorn is a more recent varl6 than the White, and is even more hardy flrg prolific than the White Leghorn. The egg° fe white and of a good, , medium, saleable There is one feature in favour of this tYPe "b-o- -'it is that they are non-sitters, and so tr" through broody hens is avoided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>MINORCAS.</head>
        <p>THE MINORCAS.</p>
        <p>Experts are agreed that the Minorcas const tute one of the very best and most valuable fowls, still they are not so popular as they e1l! were, and the reason for that is difficult to di cover. They are black in colour, and are vel handsome birds. They lay freely and the egl are large and white. They are comparative' small eaters, and unlike some other breeds, not cost much to keep. The Minorcas thrive either in a small ray on a free range, and fewer birds cause as litt trouble to keep. If hatched about Marc or April they begin to lay in the late aututni and are usually most prolific at the time egge scarce. In every respect the Minorcas are Pl' &amp;table birds, and are well adapted to the 801! poultry-keeper. In fact, few birds are 80 13111 able. They are non-sitters and generally bal many qualities to commend them.</p>
        <p>PLYMOUTH ROCKS. In some parts of the country the Plymol. Rocks are in great request, although for unknown reason--their qualities have not bl appreciated in the North of England as would have expected. They are comparatif' a new breed to this country, and perhaps 0 may account somewhat to their lack of P°l larity in this district. The Plymouth Rocks originally came fr America, where they are amongst the most f' cied. They made their first appearance in country at a 'show held in Birmingham in 0 and in the Midlands they are amongst the to favoured breeds.</p>
        <p>The Rocks are very free layers and the age production of each hen is about 200 to eggs a year. -T.Tntil recently they more Oa held their own in the Utility Club's contest,! Of all the varieties the White species is t° more hardy and the more free layers. Other breeds will be dealt with next weOlc"'</p>
