      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>VALUE OF PURE BREEDS.</head>
        <p>VALUE OF PURE BREEDS.</p>
        <p>In the poultry yard at present hens are 104111 plentifully, and in consequence the price of airci are rapidly declining. The rates are still ri° and are likely to continue so for a week or tic° until the glut reaches its height. When t.lijti shop demands are more than met, those Wir good breeds cannot apply the eggs to a tOi useful purpose than that for sitting, an):1 these are advertised in such a .paper as Examiner," seller and purchaser should be brought in touch. If the breeds are reliable there should of surplus eggs 1:L Sbrnegeds always et:mu:lB;i littlesi ttingd difficulty purposes. Pure dispo better prices than mongrel breeds. The P13;1 strain are always the superior strain. o.o' cost no more to feed or keep and they al fetch more when the eggs are on the market 4°l hatching. The amateur should profit hY fact, and eke that he gets nothing but the be: I II breeds, a few of which have bee;mentione- _A this column.</p>
        <p>THE POULTRY COOP. An important point to observe in the constrt tion of the coop (says a writer in "Fan:a 31 Home") is that there shall be a portable 0111 ter, or some similar arrangement, which *. protect the inmates from wind and rain. later on, it is to be hoped, from the sun• better to have the coops built without a manently fixed floor. It is absolutely necesv:; however, during the early months of the ni even on the most suitable soils, to have a v0;1 floor. The coop should be placed upon thei.ti porary floor, which should be made a `I, larger than the coop, extending a few inchebi front, say six or seven inches, so that whe,.„ri and chickens are confined they can alwaYoreav on a dry place. If, however, the soil ig Pt a floor a this description is required seasons. The oali object pined by bafl</p>
