      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <p>Portable floor is that the work of cleaning is more rigidly carried out, and nothing that Bina- Plifies this is too insignificant to deserve con- sideration.</p>
        <p>POULTRY-HOUSE WINDOW. Nearly all Poultry-houses are much too dark, and particularly is this the case when an out- building is utilised. In many of the sheds foUnd on small holdings there is no window at all, or else one so small that scarcely any light enters. In an ordinary sized poultry-house there should be a window at least 181 n. or 2ft. square, which should be made to open and shut; and. then, during the hot weather, it can !e- -inem open all night, some wire-netting being fastened over the opening, so that the birds can- ?lot escape or unwelcome visitors enter. Per- !laps why the importance of light is not real- as frequently as it might be is that when one enters a house the door generally remains °Pen and the place appears quite light. The !.14ht way is to enter and close the door, then iif t is light the window is large enough, if it Is dark the fault can be, and should be, quickly rernedied •</p>
