      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>INCREASING PRODUCTION.</head>
        <p>INCREASING PRODUCTION.</p>
        <p>There is a School of reformers who talk lightly of doubling agricultural production, says "The 'Times." The farmer is not impressed by .ex- I orbitant claims for altered methods because his ! own experience teaches him that in rotation ,cropping exuberant growth ,is often associated with inferior quality or, some other depreciating circumstance. Whether in cereals or in green crop, medium. bulk is usually, in our unsettled climate, the most profitable; but in grass, and especially in pastures, there are no discount- ing considerations. Proof of the benefit derived from the manur- ing of grass land is accumulating in a variety of forms—in reports from. the agricultural col- leges and also in the experiences of individual landowners and farmers. The lessons of Cockle Park in the raising of mutton and of the Mid- land Agricultural College in the production of milk have been confirmed at Kineton in the growing of hay and in Huntingdonshire in the grazing of cattle. When Dr. Somerville, some .20 years ago, instituted the system of measuring the effects of manures by the progress of sheep grazed on the experimental plots he probably ! did not foresee the far-reaching influence of his ingenious scheme in swaying general practice !in the management of grass land. Many farmers are adopting the, same principle now; , and, although they are less I strict in attention I to details, they are. satisfied that 'money 'ex- , pended in manures is yielding them a handsome return.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>PROMISING OUTLOOK.</head>
        <p>A PROMISING OUTLOOK.</p>
        <p>The drilling of corn is now general, and RE3- a tions. The common experience contrasts pleas- antly with that of last year, and the situation and outlook are described as unusually encour- aging. The season, however•has varied greatly counties and in different he same county. Whereas the heavy even in f itieighbouring rainfall retarded work in most districts south of the Thames, and also in the north-western counties, no inconvenience was occasioned on-the eastern seaboard and in the East Midlands. In Cambridgeshire and Lincolnshire- field opera- tions have proceeded without interruption. The land was ao dry that corn was rolled in excel- lent condition in February, and, indeed, the •appearance of the countrysitle was suggestive of April. The first of the spring corn is already through the ground in Buckinghamshire and presents a healthy appearance. Opinion is divided as to the advantages of early and late sOwing, but the weight of evidence is in favour of drilling as soon as a goodigeed bed, can be, prepared. The early potatoes were mostly planted last month and it beginning has been made with the planting of the main crop. The February rain- fall was moderate in nearly all the districts where potato growing is a prominent business; and advantage has been taken of • the fine mechanical condition of the land to push ahead with work that is impracticable in broken weather, and in adverse seasons may be delayed until April and even till May. Potato growers need the satisfaction of a favourable planting season to compensate them for the disappoint- ing returns from the crop of 1913. The potatoes have kept badly in the clamps; and prices have been low throughout 'the w.inter and ,eVen:uow give little promise • of improvement.—" The Times." -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>LIVE STOCK IMPROVEMENT.</head>
        <p>LIVE STOCK IMPROVEMENT.</p>
        <p>Government Scheme Rejected by Lancashire, Accepted by Cheshire. At a conference of the Federation of Lanca- shire and Cheshire Agricultural Societies held in Manchester on Saturday, Mr. Arthur Stanley, M.P., president of the Federation, in the chair, there was a long discussion on the Board of Agriculture scheme for the improvement of live stock and the refusal of the Lancashire County Council to take Part in it. • •</p>
        <p>Mr. Young, the principal of the Holmes Chapel (Cheshire) Agricultural College, explained that for the purposes of the Board's scheme the wholç. country had been divided into provinces, and Lancashire and Cheshire together formed a single Province. The grants for the improve- ment of stock were made as one part of the whole scheme, which had also branches for ad- visory and educational work in the provinces and for agricultural research. It was necessary to come into the scheme as a. whole to receive its benefits; Cheshire had done so, and ail the other provinces were constituted; but Lanca- shire stood out. Mr. Parkinson thought the explanation of Lancashire's refusal was tat the Board had produced a chst-iron scheme which had to be accepted as whole, and the County Council had done so much work of its own on similar lines that it could not fall in with the new ar- rangements. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Hall (secretarS7 of the Altrincham Agri- cultural Society) said that the present position was practically this, that the Board of Agricul- ture said that if Lancashire would not come into the scheme it would give the whole of the money allotted to the province (some £2,600) to Che- shire. That county had already a live, stock officer appointed, and would probably get the grant intended for Lancashire if they did not bring pressure to bear On the Lancashire County Council. It was hard to see why Lancashire should hold aloof, for though she had spent it lot of money on agricultural research, other counties had done so too.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ashworth suggested that tho fact was that Lancashire had already spent at 2,000 on build- ings for 'her own scheme, and wanted some of the grant from the Board for the slime purpiase, instead of beginning again. This view was sup- ported by Mr. Airey (Whalley), who said the Board's proposals would mean that Lhneashire would not only have to pay for her own agricul- tural schools, but make an additional contribu- tion of £.1,000 to the scheme for the north-west- ern province. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Young, on the other hand, said that the Board was prepared to bear the wholo 'cost of its scheme, and if Lancashire had to pay any- thing, it was quite different from' the 'arrange- ment in any other province. Mr. T. HallllllM said that the proposed grant came through the Board of Agriculture from the Development Commissioners, and it was ear- marked for new work. That was why the county could not claim the grant for its existing insti- tutions, and the County Council had declined to go into the scheme of provinces, because of the fear that it would mean a fresh 'charge on the rates. . Mr. Bill. of the Mid-Cheshire Farmers' Asso- ciation, said that the subsidy from the Board for the improvement of stock would be of great value to both counties. Cattle in Cheshire were giving nothing ! like as much milk as they ought to do. His association had very good results from the two pedigree bulls it bought some \ "CM'S ago, hut they could get a much better ser- vice under the present scheme. The Federation has already urged the Lanca- shire.. County Council to fall in with the scheme, iOld the Chairman said it looked as though they had made up they minds to have nothing•to do with it. The question was whether the Federa- tion could not get some of the grants by forming a society to come under the scheme, even if the county stayed out. It was aimed that nothing could be done for the present, but the chairman promised to see 31r. ljuuoimau Dud find out from him whether</p>
        <p>.1•••••..</p>
        <p>in the event of Lancashire's continued refusal to come into the scheme, a society could he fOrmed to work with Cheshire; whether such a Society could obtain the grants offered, and whether it would be involved in any further expenditure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Sow seeds of half-hardy annuals, such as snaps, lobelias, petunias, nicotiana salpiglossis, verbenas, in shallow boxes or pots, and raise them in beat. Hoe between spring-flowering plants and pick out gilaNs clumps. The frame lights should now be tilted, increas- ing the opening gradually, so that the plants may become hardened.</p>
        <p>Prepare trenches for sweet peas, digging plenty of well-rotted manure in the second spit. Powdered alum is a most- effective remedy for slugs and snails. Try it. Prepare soil for parsnips, trenching two feet deep. Do not use any manure now, but sow on ground that was well-manured for a previous crop. Sow in drills threequarters of an inch deep; dropping in the seeds two or three inches apart. Dust. with soot when the seedlings appear. Plant roots of the flame flower.</p>
        <p>Plant hardy ferns,iir good h;;lmy soil. Plant pyrethrums, and ohl clumps may be taken up and divided. Cut the clumps with a knife, and avoid injuring the roots. Sow leeks in a well-dug soil, enriched with manure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>RIGHT ROTATION.</head>
        <p>THE RIGHT ROTATION.</p>
        <p>When sowing your garden or allotment seeds this year, says a writer ill The Smallholder," giro each 'crop a change of ground. You will, be surprised at the improvement this plan effects. We suggest that you sow: Carrots on the broad bean plot; parsnips on the runner bean plot; celery on the potato plot; broad beans on the turnip plot; runner beans on the onion plot ; dwarf beans on the cauliflower plot ; peas on the cabbage plot. Anything almost may follow potatoes, and potatoes may follow almost any- thing.</p>
        <p>MAKING A HOTBED FOR SEED SOWING.</p>
        <p>The hotbed is a good substitute for a heated house for the raising of half-hardy seeds. About two loads of manure are 'required to make' a fairly flood bed, but amateurs will probably be content with one load.. Shake all the. manure out Intc; a heap, turning it inside out in three days' time. Repeat this process every third day for nine. days to get a good, sweet and lasting hotbed. Pile up the manure so that it is at least nine inches wider than. the frame, which is then init on. Cover the inside with nice fine soil, about three inches deep; put on the lights and insert a pointed stick, as far is it can be conveniently pushed into the middle of the bed. The heat. of the stick should be about the iF.atue heat as the hand, and when the right heat is assured put on the boxes or pots of seeds.</p>
        <p>WINTER-FLOWERING SWEET PEAS. A new,race of'w inter-flowering sweet, peas has been produced in' A u4ralia. About. five years ago, among a IMtcli, of plants of true Spencer type, onc appeared which was more vigorous and much earlier than the others, being in full bloom when thO—rest were only a few inches high, and going to seed before they had shown flower buds. Seeds from this plant were sown, showing the same characteristics. It was named Yarrawa Spencer. ,The colour is a bright rose, with lighter wings on a creamy ground. It has since been crossed with some of the best of the older. winter-flowering grandifloras, the result being that there are now upwards of thirty dis- tinct colours, with plants much stronger than either of the parents. It is stated that none of the early floWering varieties so far introduced compare in substance, form and vigour with these new Australian Spencers. It is claimed that these varieties will flower in England dur- ing winter and early spring, if sown in late summer and grown in a cool house, or planted out in the open ground they will bloom six to eight weeks earlier than the ordinary varieties..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Citric Acid Test for Basic Slag.</head>
        <p>Citric Acid Test for Basic Slag.</p>
        <p>BOARD OF AGRICULTURE'S ADVICE.</p>
        <p>One of the most widely useful functions of the Board of Agriculture is the issue of Leaflets on matters of importance to farmers. These are generally reviewed, and in some instances pub- lished in fuq, by the Press, and are obtainable, gratis, from the Board, 4, Whitehall-place, London.</p>
        <p>------- NVe have received a copy of the latest leaflet on Basic Slag, No. 267. The main point dealt with is one that has received much attention of late, viz., the great value, as a protection to farmers, of the 2 per cent. citric acid test, to determine in the chemist's laboratory " whether the phosphate (in any particular slag) is likely to become ' available' or useful to plants in a reasonable time." The experts at the Board, after giving the subject the full and impartial consideration which their detached position makes possible, have pronounced decidedly in favour of the test, saying " it may safely be assumed that the value of a Slag is roughly pro- portional to its content of phosphate soluble ill the approved 2 per cent. solution of •Citric Acid," and recommends "every farmer intending to use slag to obtain quotations for different grades, and calculate what 1 per cent. of citric-acid- soluble phosphate would cost him in each." They 'further state " without going so •far as to say that a farmer should insist on having a high quality slag, it is safe to say that, as a rule, one containing not less than 15 per cent. of citric- acid-soluble phosphate should be used." In dealing with the crops for which as phosphatic manure slag is recommended, turnips are specially dealt with as follows: p ;‘a tSei 'tug his sn used soils aUrl e auCeifde,r ae Inlede t reS tit is edraPili igues; rottifffne. cneruciferous t i negeorl-)asnd 1' urni ps When &amp;e used leing for is best applied in the drills in • spring." All farmers should obtain a copy of the leaflet and carefully study, same.</p>
        <p>The death took place on Saturday at Coi.k of Archdeacon Cotter, of the Catholic diocese of Cork, aged 84. .11e had spent 60 years in the ministry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Stomach Troubles.</head>
        <p>Stomach Troubles.</p>
        <p>Stomach troubles always tend to become worse if neglected. The occwional head- ache or pains after eating, which trouble you now, may sca7cely seem worth atten- tion, but twelve mouths hence, if you neglect them, you may be tumbered amongst the great army of dyspeptics, whose daily toil becomes 'almost intolerable •through chronic headaches, pains after eating, flatulence, biliousness, constipation, languor, sleeplessness, and depression. It is easier to prevent than cure, therefore act promptly, now, and take Mother Seigel's Syrup daily, after your meals. It is a herbal remedy, male from more than ten varieties of roots, barks, and leaves. You will find, as have tens of thousands before you, that it has a most beneficial effect upoa the . stomach, li7er, and bowels. Forty years of world-wide successes have proved it. You can prove it, too.</p>
