      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>ft:" THE RialLEIGm ,•.., T. T. 411111011110.111.1111.0111 W</head>
        <p>ft:" THE RialLEIGm ,•.., T. T. 411111011110.111.1111.0111 W</p>
        <p>Senior Association. It is rather interesting to note that in the fortheoming trial for light ears a standard has been fixed according to engine capacity, which must not exceed 1,400 c.c.</p>
        <p>Danger Signs. A leaflet has been recently .issued by the Auto- mobile Association to local authorities sending in application for danger and direction signs, which serves to emphasise the importance of the subject and the praiseworthy efforts of the association and Motor Union. Two signs are usually included in each application, and out of every hundred applications. received by the asso- ciation about thirty-five are granted. In.num- erous cases the association is able to induce the local authorities to move sign-posts into more conspicuous positions, thus rendering any warn- ing sign. unnecessary. By obtaining the lower- ing and trimming of hedges and trees the safety of road users has been increased in several dis- tricts. During 1912 over 2,509 warning road signs were supplied by the association. Last year the number was 1,957. For 'danger signs the following are the standard letterings I-- Schools, Cross-roads, Concealed .Turning, Dangerous Bend, Dangerous • Corner, • Level Crossing; and Water. Splash. The association does not supply signs for private entrance drives; as it is of opinion that the chief onus of care is always upon traffic entering A main road from a thoroughfare of lesser importance. •</p>
        <p>A New Type of Lamp, . An interesting new departhre in. electric light- ing for ears is the introduction of an improved glow lam, which contains 'a tuetal filament, that, instead of being rendered incandeseent in a vacuum, is surrounded by an atmosphere. of pure nitrogen. The latter being notoriously inert, allows the filaments to be brought to a very much higher temperature than has for- merly been possible, without greatly shortening , their life. Consequently the effeclive candle- power will be practically dimbled, and even a further improvement may • be possible by a . modification of the present • mercury vapour lamp. The latter gives the greatest light for the least current of any form of electric lamp yet intrixliteed,. but its rather ghastly effecthas had a restrictive result up to the present. In this connection it is rather interesting to note that the newly-discovered element called Neon, which already, exists in the atmosphere,. is be- lieved may be introduced successfully into mercury vapour lamps to supply the necessary red rays they now lack.</p>
