      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>MAN "ALWAYS IN HARNESS."</head>
        <p>A MAN "ALWAYS IN HARNESS."</p>
        <p>Song, music, presentation, and speeches blended into a harmonious whole in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall„ on kiaturday night at a concert promoted by Court " Winmarleigh " (Ladies' Court) and quirt t`laaziel" of the Ancient Order of Foresters. The Mayor (Councillor Peter l'eacock) presided, and, accompanied by the Mayoress, he was supported by P.H.C.R. E. E. Farrow, of Manchester, and Bro. Healey. The MiYOR said. it was always of interest for him to attend any event in connection with friendly societies, but the Foresters had his first claim because he -was made a member of that order when 16 years of age. He was proud of his association with the friendly society move- ment, because it made men better husbands and fathers, and brought out the sympathies and best qualities in all individuals. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>FORESTERS PROVIDE LIFEBOATS.</head>
        <p>FORESTERS PROVIDE LIFEBOATS.</p>
        <p>BYO. FARROW said his first duty was to con- gratulate the Mayor upon the proud position he held at the present time, and he would like to express the congratulations of the neighbouring city of Manchester. (Applause.) They saw some time ago that he had been raised to the Mayoralty, and they were delighted to know that they had in their Order a man who was considered competent enough to carry on so important a position in such an important town Ile understood that two Courts were met there that night for a festival, and when they con- sidered that the male Court commenced its work many Tears ago they had to admit that they had been engaged upon a noble task and been successful, or they would not have been in - existence to-day. They started at a time when • England was passing through troublesome times —wheu Wilberforce was attempting to set free the' slaves—an undertaking which cost the nation a vast amount of money. - The duty and the watchword of the Foresters was " Spa- pathy,". and sympathy was the safeguard of Hie soul against selfishness., By their teaching the people self-dependence they had been help- ing the country in a remarkable way. A great principle of • the friendly societies was to save life, and theie was one thing they sometimes forgot—that vas that their Qrder provided life- boats. (Applause.) The Female Court. In 1911 some Of the females in Warrington decided to open Court, not for women's rights or the vote—(laughter)—but a Court carried on by females for females, with the same rights, privileges 'and opportunities as the male mem- bers. That was a step which their Order should have taken long ago, for he thought the Order had been very backward for a long time in that direction. • However, he was pleased to learn they were making steady progress. They could do untold good; the good they were doing along with the mew would never be fully realised. (Applause.) ALWAYS IN HARNESS. , A pleasing diversion from the ordinary pro- gramme was the presentation of a large framed photograph of himself to Bro. J. T. Taylor. The MAYOR, in handing the picture to the reci- pient, stated that whenever he had visited Court " Jaaziel " he had always noticed Bro. Taylor was undertaking'one "job" or another. He had shown sympathy and had given his services to his Court by taking the position of anyone who bappenedJO:be,:away.4, • In 190 and ,1911% Pro., Taylor had been the, chief :Ranger, araL:fon the last 15 'years lie had '.held office in one *ay or another; he had never keen out of harneas. -clApplauge.) That showed • that a great' amoutif of time must have been given for the good of the Order. He, believed that was only the third presentation of the kind made in Warrington. (Applause.) "DONE HIS BEST." In reply, Bro. TAYLOR said lie had done Ids best, and if he lived to lie forty years older he would try to do as much again. Votes of thanks were passed to Bro. Farrow and to the Mayor.</p>
        <p>The music of time evening was especially well rendered by the Premier Quartet Party, which consisted of the 31iss1ms Bipkiss and Wardle, and Messrs. W. Tynan and A. W. Pearce, whilst humour and music was supplied by Mr. Sam A ukland, who pleased especially with his con- certina representation of a church organ. Mr. Banner ably presided at the piano.</p>
      </div>
