      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Remarkable Display at the Scholars' Bulb Show.</head>
        <p>Remarkable Display at the Scholars' Bulb Show.</p>
        <p>THE MAYORAL "PACE."</p>
        <p>The large drill hall of the .Warrington Terri- torials was on Saturday transformed into a temporary greenhouse, and the drill in arms was superseded by the training:of the youthful mind Co the beauties of nature: The occasion was the eighth annual bulb show of the school igarray cl i jof di drawings children, when nearly 600 .entries were made, out of a distributiOn of a thousand bulbs last November. In addition to the blooms there ."1-am of a remarkably high standard, the work of the e oftleald borough. paintings Mr. C. Mawson, head master of the School of Art, who undertook the judging. of this section, awarded the "Alawson" Tree scholarship to S. Hall, ancl recommended the following to com- pete for the " Charles Middleton" free scholar- es a e nst striking school ships : N. Davies, 11.. Cross, E. Tyrer, W. Shaw, R. 13arlow, C. Makin, S. Eccleston, E. Thorn. hey, J. Leigh, E• Wolfe, J. Jones, IL Birks and C. Craske. . . •</p>
        <p>The opening ceremony was -performed by the mayoress (Mrs. .Feacoek). There. were also on the platform, in addition to- time Mayor and Canon Stevenson, who presided, Messrs. J. Long- land, E. Wallington, S. W. Jeffery, F. H. Long- shaw and J. E. Morgan. Ain F. H. LONGSHAW, the. 11011. secretary, thanked the •donors for their helP. In the re- port of Mr. Powell, who judged the 'bulbs, it was stated that notwithstanding the poor re- sults which Professional gardeners had obtained this year, the children's exhibits were highly satisfactory. , . The CHAIRMAN Said it was pleasing to find that the interest in the Show was maintained year atter year. Anyone who looked at the draw- ings and paintings could not fail to be struck with the great improvernent which had been made- during the past few years. All the teachers would agree that 20 years ago it would have .beea impossible to show drawings any_ approaching the excellence of those in The MAYORESS, after declaring the show open, received' a handsome bouquet from ,-Miss Rebe Ashton, who made a neat little speech. . • SETTING THE PACE. the thing gnill-that day.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. WALLusiaroNr—pi:o.i;o—sed a vote of thanks to the Mayoress, and Air. S. W. JEFFERY. ill Sec- onding, said that Councillor and Mrs. Peacock were gaining the title of the." Children's Mayor and Mayoress." He sometimes wondered what future Mayors would think, for, to use a sport- ing phrase, their present- Mayor and Mayoress were "setting the pace." They were working morning, now and night. (Applause.) The MAYOR, in reply, said when he was a boy at scnool they had no of the privileges which children now enjoyed. Me did not know whether teachers .were taking broader views, or was• broadening, hut the fact re- mained that children were -now better catered for. (Applause.) ' .A vote of tha»ks was missed to the Chairman. Mr. Rigby 's troupe of" Merry Dandies" gar, an entertainment during the afternoon,</p>
      </div>
