      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>POLICE COURT MISSION.</head>
        <p>POLICE COURT MISSION.</p>
        <p>First Meeting Hel-- d in Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Mayor presided. the first time in the The. annual meeting of the Police Court and Prison (late Mission was held in the Town Thai meet- ing of the Liverpool Diocesan Branch had been held in the town. Ile certainly thought that I°l litts'.llllol.‘77.ey'll flo'Aefl9.:)3lßaN:a'silifidtnegirttlii:wi° hata'at.. the a i Warrington was the best place for a meeting of that kind, for they would remember that years ago John Howard lived in Warrington in his early days. He fought hard for prison reform and in many respects his work seemed connected with that'of the Mission. The Rev. B. T. Pluiv.iFY read the animal re- port, which referred to the resignation of 31r. J. J. Firth, .of , Warrington, who had been the officer in that town since 1906. The vacancy Wilti filled by the transfer of 'Mr. W. R. Laws, who had been engaged in Bootle since 1906. Many of the discharged prisoners who visited the 11110 doubt3issioii Room g worked on right lines, that by means of it a helping hand was held out to those who were inclined to give olouit m the re mliossstiosn sight of, hut there was bein— vi way to despair. .. ..</p>
        <p>.Mi. STUART DRAM, the stiPendiary of Liver- is,ol, said he had known Warrington since his childhood, for lie had family associations very close. and dear to this part of I.4incashil.e.lie would like to congratulate the burgesses of War- ringtou'ihrough .the Mayor ilpint 0140 Rind Mr: Lame as the Police Court Missimier, because he had kitaw„ him in the work for many years. M ' The ayor had told him in confidence that Mr. Laws tried more eases in the Warrington Police Court luau the mole of perhaps the Mayor illeatit cases." lie could believe it. lb (the speaker) occupied a peculiar position, one of which he could not boast, but Pe supposed he could say with truthfulness that he was the busiest and largest criminal judge in the. whole of Great Britain, so he thought that he could be dlowed to Speak with some authority upon. the value of .the mission. Miss CHAMBERTAIN itildrUSSed the meet'. Mr. LAws, ill proposing a vote of thanks to the 'Mayor, 'spoke of the 'assistance he receive from the magistrates and the Chief COI-4sta*, who was a inissionary in himself,</p>
