      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>Can the Atonic Horn Nuisance be</p>
        <p>APPROACH OF EASTER.</p>
        <p>I wonder when something is going to be done to prevent the use of those vicious horns which SO many motorists seem to take a perfect delight in employing on every conceivable occasion? As an enthusiastic cyclist, I really must utter a protest against ,such evil-sounding creations: It is quite bad enough to be smothered in dust, mingled with the fragments of lubricating oil and exhaust gases, 'without being nearly 'deaf- ened every few yards. Only quite recently, while riding on one of the main outlets from London, about twenty miles from the ,Metro- polis, the nuisance became almost unbearable, and I was glad to seek refuge in a by-read • long before I had intended to leave the main thor- oughfare. It seems to make no difference to seine drivers whether they are approaching cyclists in the middle of the road and really in the way, or others, like myself, who prefer a reasonable proximity to the footpath. All are treated to the same ear-splitting blasts, which spoil both the ride and temper Of the rider.</p>
        <p>Riding Too High. . One of the commonest faults of lieginnerr,and even of experienced cyclists ,is riding too high. Particularly is this noticeable in the case of young children, who come into possession of a machine formerly belonging to one of their elders. Time after time I have seen boys and girls vainly trying to reach the pedals of a machine much too big for them, and have won- dered who was responsible. A cycle must be of suitable height if any •real enjoyment can be secured. As a general rule, a good test is to try and rest the flat of the foot on the pedal at its lowest; if this cannot be done, lower the. saddle at once. A small frame with a high saddle pillar is not really as effective as a la rge frame with less of the pillar protruding. True, the actual distance from the l)racket to the saddle is exactly the same in faith cases, hut in the former the rather unnatural position throws an undue strain on the frame and makes comfort- aide riding almost impossible. For growing boys and girls it is particularly desirable to have the longest frame possible at the time to allow for future growth.</p>
        <p>Buying a New Machinc. • The approach of Easter means the usual rush of business for the various. .cycle makers and dealers,- and no doubt many of our readers are meditating the purchase of a new machine. In very many cases, pounds, shillings and .pence must be the all-powerful consideration, but no prospective purchaser is too well off to 'neglect the necessity of securing good value for money paid. We unhesitatingly recommend the cycle by one of the large makers, particularly when a second-grade machine is all the intending pur- chaser can afford. No well-known maker can risk a reputation, built up, .perhaps, by . years of effort, by affixing his transfer to an, unreliable article. In any case, whether themftkerbe well- known or obscure, we are confident that the most expensive machine is the cheapest in the end. A first-grade cycle requires little in the way of repairs, such accessories as tYres, saddle, pedals. &amp;c., are of the best, and botili enamel and plating will stand any amount of wear and tear.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Cycle Cars and Light Cars.</head>
        <p>Cycle Cars and Light Cars.</p>
        <p>It seems quite likely that before very long the diflicultY of classification Of eycle,cars aild light cars Will bo. definitely eStablished,by the Auto, Cycle Union and the lioyaL:Automalblle Clitbt;'4 the two bodies which have been considering th6' question, for some, little time. One unanimous decision recommends the abolition of the term "cycle car," and but few war regret its this- appearance. The name Ii as never lwen a satis- factory one, for, as a general, rale,- the vehicles so called have been far more of a car than a cycle, although the lattet may bave been their Origin. Now the term " light car will include the cycle car class, with some few exceptions; also the rapidly-increasing number of small ears now being placed on the market. In all probability all three-wheeled vehicles,, whether they be motor cycle and sidecar, "Sociable" or other pattern. will in future be under the -juris- diction of the Auto Cycle Union, all four- wheeled vehicles being under ' the wing of the</p>
      </div>
