      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>"THE SANCIITY OF MARRIAGE."</head>
        <p>"THE SANCIITY OF MARRIAGE."</p>
        <p>Dr. Mellor and the,Mockery of Unhappy Weddings.</p>
        <p>Idealism as opposed to sentimentalism was the pith of Dr. Mellor's address on " The Poetry and Philosophy of Henrik Ibsen," at the last meeting of the session of the Warring,ton Liter- ary and Philosophical Society, in the Cairo- street Hall on Monday evening. If men were to realise themselves, said Dr. Mellor, they must do so in spirit and in truth; they must realise their deepest and most genuine self, not any sham self that arose and then faded away into the mists of unreality; not any ephemeral flut- tering self that endured only for a moment and then was forgotten. "The descent from a high idealism to a mean sentimentalism is easily made," said Dr. Mellor. - "Let us put a concrete case—such a case as Ibsen and Mr. Bernard Shaw might find joy in dissecting. Under the sway of passion, or moved by the machinations of interfering friends, two persons elect to enter the bonds of matrimony. After a time they fin'd that they are not properly suited to emit- other,-/ind the arrange- ment of marriage bekniite:S. standing misery, bringing all sorts of suffering, deceit, hypocrisy, " and worse, into the'lita'othoth. What is the right thing to do lit tliese Orcurnstances? What ought to be done from the point of view of genuine self-realisation? "A true idealism will reply: 'Let these people separate completely one from the other. Let them try again, and let us as a society take the risk. Their marriage is in no sense a real marriage, but a sham and a fraud; and individuality cannot tolerate shams and frauds.' Sentimentalism, on the other hand, came along holding up its harls and crying: 'Ah, hut there is the authority of the Church, the sanctity of the marriage bond, the necessity for avoiding a scandal, the beauty of self-sacrifice, the duty of wife to husband,' and heaven knows what else, parading specious phrases whose meanings it has never considered, accepting as absolute vain standards of conventional imagination which it supposes to constitute the world of real and, still worse, of ideal life. "Sentimentalism invites men and women to live a lie. In the name of duty, the watchword of sentimental morality, in the name of Provi- dence, the hypocrisy of- a sentimental religion, it asks them to base their whole personal being, all their chances of self-realisation, upon a morass of falsehood. Genuine idealism, on the other hand, will take the risk of truth and • accept the adventure of saving one soul at the expense of many pet ideas."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
