      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RICHMOND'S SUCCESSFUL YEAR.</head>
        <p>RICHMOND'S SUCCESSFUL YEAR.</p>
        <p>Contemplated Extension of the Works at Grappenhall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>TEN PER CENT. DIVIDEND TIND BONUS.</head>
        <p>TEN PER CENT. DIVIDEND TIND BONUS.</p>
        <p>At the twenty-third ordinary general meeting 1 of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co.,' Ltd., held at the offices of the company on Tuesday, the shareholders had a very pleasing report once more laid before them. The figures, which were dealt with in detail by the Chairman of the Company, and are re- ported below, showed a credit balance of over £36,000, which was devoted to the payment of 6 per cent, to preference shareholders and 10 per cent., with a bonus of 6d. per share to ordinary shareholders, and to other purposes, including the addition of £5,000 to the reserve fund, which already stood at £50,000. The most important announcement made by the Chairman was that the directors contemplate extending the works at Grappenhall, and hoped that the whole of the works would be concentrated there shortly—a concentration which would effect many economies. Mr. Thomas Dwerryhouse presided, and was supported by the following directors: Mr. H. M. Thornton (vice-chairman and managing director), Mr. John Coxon, Dr. J. F. Atkins, Mr. J. A. Rensome (managing direeitoo, Mr. J. W. Glover, and Mr. James W. Slack (director and secretary).</p>
        <p>and of the manufacturers of gas appliances, whether they be makers of machinery,. &amp;c., for the manufacture of gas, or, as ourselves, makers of appliances which consume gas. It is the age of advertising, and until very recent years gas and its manifold domestic uses, as well as its importance in connection,with our national in- dustries, had been largely unknown quantities. All this is now changed, and the many advan- tages which gas can claim are being fully and honestly emphasised by the British Commer- cial Gas Association, by advertising in the daily papers, periodicals, and in the technical and educational Press.</p>
        <p>The Policy of Specialisation. It has become very fully recognised by the gas industry on one side and the manufacturers of gas appliances of all descriptions on the other side, that co-operation offered in many ways is to the advantage of both, and the good rela- tions between buyer and seller which have be- come so satisfactory in recent yeara, and which are evidenced by joining of forces in connec- tion with the National Gas Exhibition and the British Commercial Gas Association, have greatly strengthened the prospect of a continu- ance of the prosperity of gas undertakings and manufacturer alike. And now, having given you some idea of the activities of the gas indus- tries as a whole, I can very justifiably say that this business is progressing steadily .and the high reputation for our manufactures which it aas been our endeavour to build up has been considerably enhanced during the year now gone. We have always considered it the best policy to specialise and concentrate all our forces on the requirements of the gas indus- try proper. (Hear, hear.) We think this is fully appreciated by our customers, and they realise that we have at our command an or- I ganisation comprising science, designing, con- struction and general manufacture, which is, and does produce, the very best—and only the best—appliances for their use. As an instance of the advantage of specialisation, we have, after several years of steady laboratory work, suc- ceeded in effecting a very important and far- reaching change in the efficiency of the gas cooker. This, coupled with the important ad- vances we have made in recent years in gas fires, water heaters, and appliances for indus- trial uses, is keeping us well to the front, and able to meet the more exacting requirements of the industry for articles scientifically made, economical in use, and easily maintained. The tendency of our time is for labour-saving ap- paratus, and with the gradual reduction in the Price of gas we can depend—allowing for the ordinary fluctuations of industry—on a con- stant demand for our manufactures. (Hear, hear.) We have always been careful to cultivate the confidence of our customers, and this is a foundation for our business upon which I am sure we cannot improve. We have in our works, in our offices, and in our selling organisa- tion, a loyal and devoted staff, all pulling to- gether for the advantage and profit of the busi- ness. There is a quiet and complete confidence in the future which augurs well for a continu- ance of the company's prosperity. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>SATISFACTORY YEAR AND PROSPECTS</head>
        <p>A SATISFACTORY YEAR AND PROSPECTS</p>
        <p>G 006:</p>
        <p>The CIIAIRMAN said : I am pleased to call your attention to the fact that the year covered by the report and balance sheet put before you has proved to be a prosperous one, and that the favourable anticipations et:pressed at our last meeting have been justified., the output for .the year being largely in exceli of that for the pre- vious year. (Hear, hear.) This in itself is sat- isfactory, but must be qualified. Owing to strikes and general unrest in the labour world, the consequent increase in wages and the in- creased prices of almost all materials and fin- ished work incidental to trade, our goods have cost more to manufacture, and the profit de- rived has not increased proportionately with the growth of the business. On the whole, how- ever, we may be satisfied with the year's re- sult. In the report mention is made of the growing demand for the company's goods. This is so, and we hope and expect that this will still further increase. (Hear, hear.) Relative to this statement I may nay that during the last few years a fair proportion ofour orders have been of a character demanding considerable space in their construction. Tie meet these de- mands the directors propose to put up further buildings at Grappenhall, which will give the desired accommodation. These buildings will be of the size and extent to justify the demand that will be made upon them. , It is hoped that at no distant date the whole cif the works will be centred at Grappenhall, w hick will effect many economies. It is not, :however, antici- pated that the erection of these additional build- ings will interfere with the dividend-paying ability of the company. (hear, hear.) With regard to our prospects- for the present year, the outlook is fairly goodj and we can see no reason to believe that the business will be much different in its results tji an that covered by the report and balance sit ae§:. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Gratifying Fig arcs. Turning to the figures, we will find very little but what is of a gratifying character. Taking the assets, you will find we have put £12,000 to the cost of freehold and le; iseholu works, mak- ing the sum now .1:115,616. The good-will re- mains as it is and has been,- for many years. A good many people would like it cut out. I would like that myself, b-it we have to fall in I with the way in which otl ter people make their accounts. They include good-will, and so do we, and we put the figuro at ,15,000, which iS -not an extraordinary sum for good-will. Qur. trade stocks are oi:so,o4s—rather lees than last year, although we have done a great deal more business. - We do not make goods to go into stock, we make the stufr and sell it, and that is the most profitable way of dealing w ith a busi- ness. (Hear, hear.) sundry debtctrs—rather less—represent £61,434. Cash at 0,, bankers and in hand and in dividends due al °omits to £18,159, as against A;6,000 last year. (Hear, hear.) Investments in the associated c ampanies are as they were. We have taken th e cost of the other investments down from time • to time, and it is not a paying game, but still w e have to do it. The tatal figure for the year is A:341,074 3s. 6d.</p>
        <p>Strong Position of the Companty. The share capital remains as it w as. The ordinary shares are £Bl,OOO, and tha.t. as the ordinary capital of a company doing this large business is very small. (Hear, ear.). Prefer- ence shares are as they were, debenture stock is as it was, and the sundry creditors and..contin- gent reserve stands at £45,654. If I ren ember aright I told you at the last annual meet ing we initiated that contingent reserve so th at we could call upon it for making up dividei ids or any other purpose. We have strengthen&amp; I that reserve this year. The reserve fund is ..et iO,OOO, and the balance as at December 31st, 191 , was £8,327 us. 4d., less directors' fees of xsilloji mak- ing the total £7,477 us. 4d. The balance from the trading account is £29,243, so, the ha lance remaining to be disposed of is ..t36,720 12s, . against £35,024 last year. (Applause.) The depreciation on buildings is £6,200, a ainst £5,600 last year. The dividend is the same as last year, and we are again paying a bonus this year. We are putting to the reserve .t 5,000 which makes t hat fund £55,000, which, on a capital of £Bl,OOO. • is a very fair proportion. (Hear, hear.) The b al- once to next account, subject to the directo re ! remuneration, is £9,434 18s. 11d. That seei as! rather large, but I(eeing what is to be done NV ;h it, I think it very reasonable. and it gives ti ie company a sense of safety. (Hear, hear.) IA .e have to go on building this year, and we shall want a good proportion of the money to carry on. It is perfectly safe, but we want this bat% once to carry on with in the meantime. Tile company, I consider, is now in a safer state than at any other time in its existence. (Applause.) I do not think there is any donbt about that. I have spoken to some competentil judges about it, and they all think this a capital balance sheet. That is also my own opinion. (Applause.) I propose: " That the directors' report and statement of accounts for the year ended December 31st, 1913, as certified by the auditor, be received and adopted."</p>
        <p>THE ADVANTAGES OF GAS. Mr. 11. M. THORNTON, vice-chairman and mhn- aging director, in seconding said; The Chair- man has given such an exhaustive account of the balance sheet for 1913, that a very few re- marks from me on the financial result for the past year will he all that is necessary. On one Point I can assure the shareholders, that is, that the dividend which is now proposed is jus- tified by the results, and without in any way neglecting to set aside necessary sums for safe- guarding the business. The event of the year under review, as affect- ing your business, was the National Gas Exhibi- tion in October last, which was organised jointly bY the gas authorities and the manufacturers. We took a very protainent part, both financially and otherwise, in this -exhibition, and it was hundred my privilege to be vice-chairman of the Execu- tive Committee. The exhibition was an thattin- doubted t hfeo er d au c laotni Metssas; t people—viandd Ibya ms smaet stfi7l ust produce in the cWO s benefit ognatlimreesutoltscowm-hich such demon- must pr of modern, and up-to-date appliances minds of gas consumers.</p>
        <p>Advance of the Association. The continued success 'during the year that is past, and the steady advancement of the Brit- ish Commercial Gas Association, are also to us matters of considerable moment. As I explained last year, the Association has for its object the provision of an annual fund for the purpose of advertising gas for all purposes. These funds, to which we subscribe, are gradu- ally growing, and its activities are becoming greater as a result.</p>
        <p>- Speaking as a member of the Executive Com- mittee, I regard the future of this great or- ganisation with every confidence, as also its influence on the prosperity of gas undertakings</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>10 PER CENT. DIVIDEND.</head>
        <p>A 10 PER CENT. DIVIDEND.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN proposed : " That a dividend at the rate of 10 per cent. per annum (less 5 per cent, already paid) and a bonus of fid. per share for the year ended December 31st, 1913 (free of incomq tax), be now declared and paid." Mr. J. CoxoN; seconding, said: I have seconded this resolution for a great number of years, but never with greater satisfaction than to-day, because the company has never been in such a sound condition during the whole of its history'. (Applause.) Some of the shareholders may think that the 'carry forward" is rather more thaii tieceeSary, but a bitsiness like this is sub- ject.,togteat ..fluctuations. But, fortunately, as the Chairman has told you, we have been very busy, and the works have to be extended in consequence. I am told that you have every- thing to-day connected with this business to make it a.really sound one. You have a world- wide reputation—a reputation second to none. r - (Applause.) You have a staff, from the manag- ing directors and secretary, second to none; and, gi normal conditions and normal times, I think we may look forward with every confi- dence to the future. I have great pleasure in seconding this resolution. (Applause.) ' The resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>DIRECTORS' WELL-EARNED REMUNERA-</head>
        <p>DIRECTORS' WELL-EARNED REMUNERA-</p>
        <p>TION.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. DUFF moved the remuneration for the directors, and said : I propose that the sum of .1:850, free of income tax, be placed at the dis- posal of the directors for their services during the year 1913. I have never had more pleasure in proposing such a resolution. (Hear, hear.) The company has done exceedingly well during the past year, and I have every confidence that it will continue so in future years. (Hear, hear.) - -</p>
        <p>Mr: T. Boovu, who seconded, said: After such an interesting and successful balance sheet the directors have presented to us, this remunera- tion must be voted without any doubt, and with great enthusiasm. (Hear, hear.) The result In the past year has been immensely satisfactory. I would like at this point to compliment Mr. Thornton for the interesting and excellent paper he read at Newcastle a few days ago. Such a paper as he placed before the industrial world cannot be done without bringing grist to the mill. (Applause.) What we want, and what t great sties is ' being done at the present day. is to bring gas very largely into use in our national indus- tries. There can be no question that where it has been brought into use he are deriving great advantages from it. (Hear, hear.) Personally, I am sure the shareholders indu will agree that Mr. Thornton deserves the high- est compliment for the hiteresting practical and satisfactory paper he read. (Applause.) .. The resolution was carried. , The (7nAißmAx : I must thank you on behalf of the directors for this Vote, but—it has been well earned. (Laughter and applause.)</p>
        <p>1 THE AUDITOR'S TESTIMONY. The CHAIRMAN continued : Mr. Voisey has acted for us for a great number of years as auditor, and I think we are satisfied that he has done his work well, and we would not change for any- thing. (hear, hear.) I ask Alderman Smeth- urst to propose a resolution. Alderman SMETHURST proposed: "That Mr. L. . A. Voisey, chartered accountant, be reappointed ') auditor for the company for the year 1914, at a i remuneration of „t 1.50. lie said: It always 1 : gives me great pleasure in proposing Mr. Voisey. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>• j Wonderful 'Growth. As I was Coiling down Scotland-road I looked ; up the yard where Mr. Unsworth used to be, and thought: "Dear me, fancy me being in- vited to go into that place to meet Mr. Rich- mond to join him in what has developed into this wonderful undertaking." To look at that '.place and look at Richmond's now, why, it is something wonderful. It is due to the great perseverance of those connected with the com- pany—the chairman, -directors, and, above all, .our auditor, because if we got wrong in our :accounts and did not keep them straight, we I would be sure to come to grief. (Hear, hear.) ' I thank Mr. Thornton for his excellent paper j (in the operation of gas in manufacture. I read j :it with great pleasuie. In some points it ' touched nte a little bit, because gas gpuperseded ;something I once did a good business in.. (Laughter.) However, .1 am not in business :now, and you can go on with your gas as much :as you like. (Laughter.) Mr. frhornton has. been on a branch in every•partiole of which I :have had some interest. A company like this cannot help but succeed when wo have directors like Mr. Thornton who can show the public plainly where they call save mumey, and im- prove their manufactures. It gave me the greatest pleasure to read that there was a gen- tleman in this concern who, took: such an in- terest in bringing out so many improvements for the benefit of the country. (A,pplause.) Mr. E. J. HALL, in seconding, safid : I am sure every gentleman in the room is well aware that ttnY accounts certified by Mr. V.visey may be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>THANKING THE STAFF.</head>
        <p>THANKING THE STAFF.</p>
        <p>Dr. ATKINS, proposing a vote of thanks to the staff, said: We have a most excellent staff, from the boy in the office to our top men—the secre- tary and managing directors. I can assure , you that our heads—the managing directors and all of them—are keenly alive to the competition taking place in the gas world, and I can assure you they are meeting it at all points. We are not second; we are first. (Applause.) Mr. Thornton has been very active recently his papers, and, we feel convinced that the ques- r with tion of the use of coal gas for industrial pur- poses will. be an important thing in the future. Appliances will be needed that are not being used now, and that, I IlaPe, means that we shall get a great share of the business, for we are first in the field. (Hear, hear.) I couple with this vote the names of Mr. Slack, Mr. Thornton, Mr. I(ansome and Mr. Glover, of. George and Co., our meter works. I may tell you that de- partment is a very important thing in this business as to the paying of our dividends. It is a very fine investment for us. (hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>In seconding, Mr. F. A, Faosx said: I know the keen anxiety there is to steer the ship into Port • with such a valuable cargo On board, and judg- ing by the balance sheet, the managing three- tors, secretary and staff of this boat have brought us in very safely. (Applause.) I hope in future years we may be carrying even a larger cargo. 'The directors have to be supported by the other members of the staff, and I am sure in this concern they could not have better assist- ants with whom to deal. (Hear, hear.) The prosperity of the firm may be devised originally by the head of the board, but they could not get on without the staff serving them very loyally. And that being so, I, as one of the smaller share- holders, have great pleasure in supporting a vote of thanks to the lot. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The resolution was carried. _ _ The CHAIRMAN : I think we all agree with that.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>M. RANSOME AND AN UP-TO-DATE WORKS. •</head>
        <p>M. RANSOME AND AN UP-TO-DATE WORKS. •</p>
        <p>In reply, Mr..J. A. RANSOME said: It gives me great pleasure to thank you on behalf oT my colleagues on the board and the staff which we have working under us. The very fact that there are few changes, if any, made in the staff is in itself a reliable and good omen. We have undoubtedly an able and willing lot; not only in the heads of the departments, but also in the less important positions, and by the willing co- operation of heads and the staff we are thus able safely to steer the ship with its valuable cargo on board, of which Mr. Frost has spoken. I have pleasure in again reporting that the machinery and workii are up to date, and are being well and properly maintained. It has been a difficult year not only in the labour world, but in the purchase of materials, and I um glad indeed that the result has proved itself satisfactory. That is largely owing to the loyalty of the staff and those under them. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>HOPEFUL HERALD OF THE SPRING.</head>
        <p>HOPEFUL HERALD OF THE SPRING.</p>
        <p>Mr. SEGAR OWEN proposed the meeting's thanks to the Chairman and said: I have only attended a few of these Meetings, but it always seems to me Richmond's balance sheet heralds in the spring—(laughter)—for there is a certain tone of hopefulness about it which starts the Year so favourably for us, particularly when we receive our dividends. (Laughter and ap- plause.) The splendid balance sheet has been mentioned by everybody, but what, perhaps, the directors would like to know is that we share- holders realise that the balance sheet is Pro- duced by the earliest work of the directors. I know Mr. Dwerryhouse is heart and soul in the work. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN PLANT, seconding, said: I hope to see our chairman in that position for many years to come. (Applause.) The Citkiiniky:: Lhave not much to do but to look after 4134 66:riipany;. It's a. sort of spare time employment,' and r turn happy to further the interests of the company. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RUNCORN LICENCE REFUSED.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN LICENCE REFUSED.</p>
        <p>At the adjourned Licensing Sessions, at Run- corn, Mr. J. W. Knowles applied on behalf of Mr. Michael Coan for the renewal of the licence of the Moulders' Arms, Heath-road. Sergeant Worthington said the house was located in a rather poor neihbourhood. The sanitary arrangements were very good, but the g house was moderately repaired and not very clean. Coan had had a licence for the last five years. Cross-examined, he. said the house had been well conducted.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. Knowles called the magistrates' attention to the fact that last year they referred the Queen's Head and the Moulders' Arms tc, the County Compensation Authority. The lattei licence was renewed. They would remembei that they had to grant a provisional licence at the last sessions to the Queen's Head, as the occupant and the brewers had not received theii compensation -He believed they had not been paid yet.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: Oh, yes, they have! Mr. Knowles: Oh; I believe hot! The Queen's Head is closed, and I think the matter is to be settled on the 24th inst. The Chairman. you must be careful the County Authorities 'don't get you for libel. (Hear,- hear.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles: I don't think so. It is a state- ment of fact. ' (Laughter.) The Chairman (Sir Frederick Norman) re- marked that they had unanimously decided to refuse the licence on the grounds of redundancy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MAYORESS'S SCHOOL</head>
        <p>MAYORESS'S SCHOOL</p>
        <p>A Unique Event</p>
        <p>is Celebrated at Street.</p>
        <p>GIFT OF A PHOTOGRAPH.</p>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL YARD. (head master), the Mayoress, and Amy Lamb.</p>
        <p>, . ~. . s 4- ...........,, -,, ......... ...... ..c....... . .:::, ~,.,...r,.,, . .•.:... . . ..,.... „........ -....:::... '2-- i:.....- ,if.)' ..,..,.............:...., ::,:-‘,,..,0::,1,,A.T.:,•:A-;:;:,,.; ;... : ‹,..46#.1041'.4... ''''-: ' - .T..,;i..,;;;;;;.,,e;,,,.*w:.......,,,,,k, . • k , ;:. ••••,..;,.. . k ' ' ',&gt;.•'''.'l.': ,‘ ~. . -.. ..,,,,y-,. ~..:," ' -. x4to 4 Ar '‘ ''.''• ,‘ A * • : ' : ‘...,..- li,: 1 .. .44,tt*Ati... \-..-.,',,,,‘,,,,,,f;f;.-Tf.., ~. T . ...... - . ..:. t ..I. ... "'....: f ~ • . 8 . I, • -, - '• : ' . . - ':- . ....il:;! :::'.'.i * • - . ',..., . ~..11, -444.4 ' 2 . 'l/4:,',„,.. ~!,..." . '.. Ig4 ' '.. 4,, ' -N • , ' • ' . -.-,4•''', ttt. 47,t,.., ~,,,,,4_,, .4,-.,4t '• ±'''.,:4 ANA,. .4 ~,,,::::,.!-;....-,...,.,. ~,, :,,,,,,...-&amp;...., , ~...,. ~,• ~, ~,,,,,- `'.i..... : .4.ire'' . '-.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>GROUP IN TII The Mayor, Lilian Fainlough,, Mr. J. Upson.,</head>
        <p>GROUP IN TII The Mayor, Lilian Fainlough,, Mr. J. Upson.,</p>
        <p>It is certainly an unusual thing for two children who attended elementary day schools to raise theMselves above their fellow scholars and eventually become in after life the Mayor and Mayoress of an industrial town like War- rington but tjll more unusual is it for them to pay a visit to the haunts of their schooling days as a token of their gratitude for the educa- tion they were privileged to obtain. Two events of this uniiiue description have been celebrated in the town, when photographs were pre- sented by Councillor and Mrs. Peacock to the Wycliffe and Silver-street Schools. The Mayor presented his photo to Wycliffe School last Friday, as was exclusively reported in the last issue of "The Examiner." On Monday the :ceremony of presenting a photo of the Mayoress took place at Silver-street, and was presided over by the Rev. W. A. Beckett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>VERY UNIQUE OCCASION.</head>
        <p>VERY UNIQUE OCCASION.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. A. BECKETT said it was a great pleasure to be there as chairman of the man- agers of the Silver-street School on an occasion very unique and exceedingly interesting. They had with them the Mayor and Mayoress of a Very important borough, and they recognised the fact that the position of honour they held and then: popularity were well deserved. The merits of the school were Well known, for there were hundreds of people in Warrington and up and down the country who held them in very high esteem, and they valued just as highly the instruction they received there. They had one of those people amongst them in the person of the Mayoress. (Cheers.) They could assure the Mayoress and the Miliyor that their popu- larity would .not suffer by their 'presence that afternoon and in the name of the Managers and the staff ortlie. sehotiThe 'eXtentled A.'llearty wel- come. (A pplause •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WHEN THE MAYORESS WAS CANED.</head>
        <p>WHEN THE MAYORESS WAS CANED.</p>
        <p>The MAroL, in, addressing the ladies and gen- tlemen., and " most important of all—the chil- Area," said it 'was always a pleasure that he went amongst the children. He was proud that the Mayoress had been associated with that particular school hr her early days, when the then head, master, Mr. Spencer, used to thrash her time after time, but she always assured him (the Mayor) that she was always a ,good girl. (Laughter from the children.) Althoifgh he had i not been associated with that particular day school, he had been a scholar in the Sunday school, and it was interesting to look back upon those days. He had also been connected with the sur- roundings of the buildings for about 30 years, having lived in and been connected with the ward for abut that length of time; therefore, he was always pleased to associate himself with anything affecting the (Word Ward. ' _ _</p>
        <p>He did not know many of the boys and girls by sight—they certainly seemed to know him— and he would like to say to them that if they took advantlige of their opportunities nothing was impossible in this life.. The Education Committee were providing steps for a university education for those who merited it, and he urged 'them to persevere.</p>
        <p>"I say to you boys and girls, strike high, aim for high ideals, and I feel quite ,sure you will get something better than some of us could aspire to in days gone by." (Loud. applause.)</p>
        <p>SCHOOL'S REP-LETTER DAY. The Mayoress then handed the photograph to Lily rairclough, who accepted it on behalf of the school, And after Amy Lamb had presented a bouquet to the Mayoress and a buttonhole to the Mayor, Mr. JOHN UPSON, the bead master,: replied. He said that was a red-letter day in the history of the school. He could reveal some secrets if he said what his registers told him of the times when the Mayoress .went to the school. He thought he must be getting old or the Mayoress must be perennially young, when he said that he remembered very distinctly the time when the Mayoress attended that school. He would like to assure her how honoured they were in knowing that one of their own scholars had won an 'exalted position. (Cheers.) The Mayoress was, of course, not selected by the Town Council,' but after all they had to remember that the Mayor would not have been able to maintain his high position if it had not been for the support he received from the Mayoress, and he thought Councillor' Peacock would agree that he could never have occupied the position if it had not been for the support of his wife.</p>
        <p>Schooldays More Happy. School was now a much happier place than it was when the Mayoress attended. In those days they were compelled very often to turn out bricks without straw, for boys and girls were made to fit a certain system, and consequently there was a great •amount of pressure upon them. Then, to earn the Government grant was very harmful in many respects to the de- velopment of the 'mental capabilities of the children, but scholars to-day were on very much wider lines, and he was of the opinion that the money spent upon education at the present moment was, being better spent than ever before; he was certain that the boys and girls would agree that school was a happy place. At the time. the Mayoress was at schoor they fre- quently had, to send boys Away for being tru- ants, but for the last eight oten years they had had, no truants at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>No Longer a Place of Dread.</head>
        <p>No Longer a Place of Dread.</p>
        <p>School was no longer a pla,ce of dread and fear, but a place where children delighted to go, because their faculties were trained and de- veloped along rational lines. Children found wonderful pleasure in their,.schools, for they were training the , mind in every conceivable way. They 'tried to get out of the children all that was in them • and they were constantly seeking new ways cud methods by which the</p>
        <p>children's minds and powers might be properly developed. They had at the present moment 33 children in the school who could leave it now if they were so disposed, but they; had asked their parents to allow them to remain at school and it spoke well for the parents that they con- curred. .</p>
        <p>In these days of materialism, Lancashire zeople were charged, and properly sometimes, with the exploitation of their children at the earliest possible age, but that was not conducive to the best interests of the children. He wished that 33 was raised to 133, and he would not be satisfied until all children were kept at school until they were 14; indeed, Ile believed if it was left to the children the age limit would be 14. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>PRODUCTS OF WARRINGTON SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS said it was a remarkable fact that the Mayor and Mayoress were pro- ducts of their own schools, lie had never come across an instance like it before, and it was ex- tremely gratifying to realise that the character of the education in the elementary schools .of the town could fit them for their high position. (Applause.) The-programme was interspersed with songs rendered by the children; and a most interest- ing and unique event concluded with the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>As the Mayor mid Mayoress left the building after the ceremony a crowd of people gave them a hearty send-off by cheering lustily.</p>
        <p>Have you had you Sample Bottle of H. P. SAUCE yet?</p>
        <p>Our advertising staff are bringing them right to your door—FREE.</p>
        <p>We know you will like its new and delicious flavour, obtained from the choicest Oriental fruits and spices—perfectly blended. It's quite easy to get H.P.—all Grocers are selling it freely at gd. for large-sized bottle.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>But be sure it IS</p>
        <p>• , ;111;',%;/1:,• iffAcm,t;...</p>
        <p>A ST. HELENS COURT SCENE.</p>
        <p>Francis Gallaghan, a young Irish 17, Samuel-street, St. Helens, was arresto perjury at the local Court, at the conclusi the hearing of a charge against him of pl football in the streets.</p>
        <p>Gallaghan, when charged last week, atter( to prove an alibi, and said that instead of 1 in Samuel-street, Thatto Heath, playing ball, he was round the Clonal's, Rainhill, a man named Donnelly. The case wat journed, and two private witnesses now c borated the statement of Police-constable C that Gallaghan was playing football in street at that time.</p>
        <p>James Donnelly, 11, Derby-cottages, Re gave evidence, bearing out the alibi, hi nutted that he made a statement to Pe'</p>
        <p>Bowden, which was directly contradictorP The magistrates decided that the case proved, and fined defendant ss. and costs, ' days. The Chief Constable then applied fora rant against the accused for perjury. This I granted, the man was arrested.</p>
        <p>When leaving the Exchange Station, Man! ter, on Monday afternoon, on her return Frodsham, a Mrs. Jack, of Bradshaw-8 Higher Broughton, collapsed and died.</p>
        <p>The Chester Fire Brigade were engaged hours in extinguishing a fire at Church Farm, Waverton, which destroyed a quanl hay and did damage estimated at £4OO.</p>
        <p>Mr. • .Kelsall was chairman 0 Ashton Bench of magistrates which 0 " distress in seven days," against his Mr. Tom Kelsall, J.P., who is a passive ri</p>
