      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>BUNTY SENDS WEELUM FOR THE MINISTER.</head>
        <p>BUNTY SENDS WEELUM FOR THE MINISTER.</p>
        <p>What .should prove a most popular engage- pent is the visit next week at the Royal Court 'Theatre, of Mr. Cyril Maude's Company in the Scottish comedy, " Bunty Pulls the Strings." The piece has taken London by storm, having been played over 460 times in one year. The scene is the Scottish village of Lintiehauglv the period '1860; and the characters—the shop- keeper and his family, the joiner, the police- man, and so on. Fashions change, but human nature remains constant, and one could fiad "just the like" of the author's characters to- day, though one might have to go some distance from the railway to find them. Tammas Big- gar is a persistent character. As the shopkeeper of the village he is a person of consequence, a magistrate and an elder of the Kirk. He is a jocose and Pawky person at times. At other times he is very, very dour, and his pious sever- ity has already sent the on to Glasgow—and the bad. He is a bit of a hypocrite, too, though he hardly seems to be aware of the fact. Bunty, as his daughter is called, is a clever and attrac- tive girl, who has the wit to play one character off against, another,. and one circumstahce off against another., She extricates her father from more than one tight place. She fools and foils the scheming, • vindictive woman who would marry 'him; and brings about his much more promising engagement with Ellen Dunlop, an old .flame -whore he deserted in his youth. The.</p>
        <p>only other Biggar in the play is flab, 1311: brother, who has seen " Rob Roy theatre, cannot learn his Catechism, ,:ei (hymn tunes) on the Sabbath, and is ti to the thrashings from his father, and wants life in ,Glasgow. A comic creation' of the vater is Bunty's 'fiance, the gloomy joille:..l ise who know nothing of Scotland, t11:10 11 be most instructive. They will Se', notions of the rigours of the Old Seettlo`loji bath. "Bunty Pulls the Strings" is a teDie able play—perhaps the most remark° op recent times. Anyone who wants a realtoti ing ,evening should go to see the P` players.</p>
        <p>A rainfall of 2.11 inches was regiotel Accrington during the first five daY5 ° Present month.</p>
