      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <p>to the hotel at one o'clock, Inspector Stuart and witness found the place in darkness. I In answer to Mr. Jordan, witness said that I when he saw the defendants in the street they were "jolly." Mr. Jordan: I am told they were singing rag- time songs. Witness: I cannot say what they were sing- ing. They were making a noise. THE DEFENCE. Mr. Jordan said the men were on the premises during closing hours, and it was his duty to satisfy the Bench that they were not there in contravention ,of the Act. According to law, a licensee might have on his premises after clos- ing hours private friends, bona-fide entertained at his own expense. He contended that the cir- cumstances of the present case came within that provision. There had been a billards tourna- ment between teams representing the Golden Lion and the Old Ball, and on the evening in question the players held a supper at the Old Ball. The defendants were all either players in the Golden Lion team or on the reserve list 'to assist, if occasion arose. The licensee of the Golden Lion was at the supper. Shortly before 11 o'clock he took Critchley and Locker home asvehe a would drink havewih h said m with him in his trap, and told the other defend- antsa to call rouenaide , just s th a on their way h if he had been inviting them to a private house. The Chairman: A most unwise thing to do. Mr. Jordan: We are all wise after the event. The Chairman: It was such a wholesale invi- tation. Mr. Jordan : Only to the defendants. It waf. not a wholesale invitation to the ve ople at the inn. James Kay, the licensee, said he invited the men in the house. Mrs. Key deposed to serving the men, but there was no suggestion'of payment whatever. The Chairman said the Bench were of the opinion that very serious indiscretion had been shown by all the parties. They did not accept Mr. Jordan's definition of the friendship supposed to lye existing; the affair was too big altogether. In a case like that, the licensee should, at least, have intimated to the police beforehand that he wished to invite a large number of men into his house, even in the circumstances referred to The cases would be dismissed on payment of I costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. Before Mr. R. T. Fairclough (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion, Messrs. T. Pemberton and A. J. Jolley. The Private Mark.—Gertrude Fleetwood 06), 36, Old-road, was .charged with stealing a pail of boots, value Gs!lld., the property of Benedict Tyrer, 100, Buttermarket-street.—The Chief Constable said the girl stole the boots from the shop doorway and the theft was discovered when she tried to pawn the boots for Is. to an- other pawnbroker, who recognised Mr. Tyrer's private mark. She then said she was pawning them for a lady in Wellington-street Furthei taxed she went away. The girl was blind and lame and alleged that she was badly treated at home. Mr. Laws had endeavoured to get her in Dr. Barnardo's Home, but she was not eligible now. Mr. Laws was trying to get her in Mr. I Broome's Home at Stepney, where they made I artificial flowers. The father of the girl did not appear to have taken that interest in the girl which he should have done, and the step-mothei did not treat her well.—Mr. Laws said the girl had been knocked about from pillar to post and had had a hard life. The father was willing to pay something for her.—The girl was remanded for a week. MONDAY. Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Bolton Councillor Dr. Joseph, Messrs. S. _ _</p>
        <p>Wright, W. Roberts, and W. Furness. ve‘r•-street, idwaist, ctianirgVed'—j:-initheshia' an aggravated assault on his wife, Sarah Jane, hvionmgpcsoomn,m6,itStedil- on February 28th.—It was stated that prisonei• went home drunk, and his wife went out, leav- ing him asleep on the sofa. She locked the door, and on her return prisoner asked her why she had locked him in. She did not argue with him and prisoner went for some beer. Whilst the wife was putting some things on the shelf he came up behind and struck her on the head with a hammer. The wife wrested the weapon from him and fainted. On recovering he was coming at her again, but in desperation she again got the hammer and screamed. Two daughters came in and subsequently the woman was at- tended at the Infirmary. P.C.'s Harrison and Moody found pools of blood about the premises, and the woman was absolutely saturated. The Chief Constable said it was a most brutal assault on the woman, who worked hard to keep the house going, whilst the man went out drinking.—Mrs. Thompson said her husband had not done much work in five years.—Prisoner Did I use that hammer F—Witness : Yes, Jim; you did.—Prisoner Arethere any blood marks on it to certify I did it? You went on the car- riage, lass, and cut your head.—Witness: No; you hit me with the hammer.—Prisoner ,had admitted to Annie Thompson that he hit his wife because she nagged at him.—Prisoner I am very sorry it has happened at all. I cannot think how I lost my memory.—The Mayor: You seemed to know something about it when you said she had nagged at you. You will go to prison for three months, with hard labour.— Prisoner (over the dock rail, to his wife): "Goodbye, Sarah !"</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes and T. Lecker. A Row and a Sequel.—Thomas Kelly, labourer, 5, Stanley-strfet, was charged, on remand, with assaulting Matthew Carr, 9, Beatrice-street, in the Packet House Inn.—The evidence was to the effect that complainant was trying to stop prisoner from quarelling with another ',an. Prisoner turned on Carr and strawt him a lent blow on the nose, and broke it.—Prisoner alleged Carr hit him first, and that he only retaliated in self-defence.—The Chief Constable said prisoner was a public-house "sponger," who went to public-houses getting what beer he could for nothing, and when he failed to do so he acted as he had done in the present case. —A fine of 20s. and costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>9, Cl,:iii 4.'",",TN :::: 7: P •••••'‘."</p>
