      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>SOME CRITICISM 14 T THE ANNUAL MEETING OF THE LII3ER2HL ASSOeIRTION.</head>
        <p>SOME CRITICISM 14 T THE ANNUAL MEETING OF THE LII3ER2HL ASSOeIRTION.</p>
        <p>Hearty Support for the Government.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Liberal Association, held on Tuesday night, as notable for three things— A discussion on the proposed concessions to the Orange section of Ulster upon the Home Rule Bill, during which it was made clear that the Government have the undivided support of the party; The vitality every section of the party is show- ing in Warrington, and the high hopes that are entertained as to future prospects; and The large attendance and enthusiasm displayed throughout the evening.</p>
        <p>admit the strength of this contention, but I am rather in doubt myself whether it would not have been wiser to have ,allowed the practical disadvantages of a divided Ireland to work their own cure, as I think they would have done, rather than to have held suspended over the heads of North-East Ulster the threat of com- pulsory inclusion after six years. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>THE OTHER PARTS OF IRELAND. I don't often disagree with any leaders, but I think it would have been a better plan, instead of suspending a sort of sword over the heads of the Ulster people to let Nature take its own course, and common-sense to take its course, and I am inclined to think they would have come in by agreement We have to be careful in think- ing of this question, not to lose sight of the other parts of Ireland, in thinking about Ulster. (Hear, hear.) I think we on our side ought to have a solemn league and covenant declaring that we will be loyal to the Irish demand, doing all in our power to solve a difficult problem and remaining loyal to,the,principlee .of which we consider the Irish demand to be the embodi- ment. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles E. Parker (president) was in the chair and he was supported by Mrs. Osborne (secretary of the Women's Liberal Association), Councillor Locker (hon. secretary), Mr. Clegg, Mr. Delaney, Captain Gordon Roberts, Mr. E. A. Sanders (agent), and others. The simul- taneous meeting of the Town Council prevented many members from attending.</p>
        <p>SPLENDID RECORD OF A YEAR'S PROGRESS BY ALL SECTIONS OF THE PARTY.</p>
        <p>average attendance was estimated at 1,000. They had a fine open-air meeting in Orford Village in June, and it was. intended to have some form of social gathering in,Orford Schools. when a committee would be formed to carry out propaganda work; with Mr. P. H. Brown as hon.. secretary.. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>, The Juniors have also helped us considerably in carrying out successfully 11 open-air meet- ings in the different wards, three mass meetings in the Co-operative Hall, the speakers being Mr. Francis Neilson, M.P., Mr. J. B. Martin- dale, Mr. W. A. Barton, M.P., Mr. Jos. Davies, Sir George Toulmin, M.P., Mr. P. M. Oliver,l and others, and by holding a garden fete at Capt. Crosfield's, in Lodge-lane, on September 6th, which was a huge success. The Peter Peacock Challenge Cup Committee have also had a -very busy summer and winter, 605 entries being received, and bowling took , place every Saturday from May to September.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>HOME RULE CONCESSIONS. Some Disappointment, But Support for</head>
        <p>THE HOME RULE CONCESSIONS. Some Disappointment, But Support for</p>
        <p>Government Solid.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that during many visits to Ireland'le had spoken to all sorts of men on the Irish question. On his last visit he met ten Ulstermen and one Home Ruler, and that Home Ruler impressed him a great deal more than the ten. He was an employer of labour, and a .Wesleyan. He said, " I have faith in human nature, I have known the Irish people, having been with them all my life. I am an Ulster- man; have mixed with them all my life, em- ployed them, and know them from top to bot- tom. I know if you treat the Irish rightly in the same way as you treat other people rightly they arise to meet the promise of you eyes." He was of the opinion, continued Mr. Parker, that Home Rule would work well. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>PRINCIPLE OF DEMOCRACY.</head>
        <p>THE PRINCIPLE OF DEMOCRACY.</p>
        <p>I should like to refer, continued the speaker —as you will, perhaps, expect me—in a more definite way to the question of the hour, which must have been in all our thoughts to-day as we read our papers. I mean the Home Rule ques- tion, and the proposal which our great leader, Mr. Asquith, with the approval of the leaders of Ireland, laid before the House of Commons last night. As a party we believe in the prin- ciples of democracy, namely, that the people, by majority vote, should decide. (Hear, hear.) For nearly one hundred years Ireland has re- turned to the House of Commons a very large majority in favour of 'cal self-government, and it ought to be granted. But a difficulty has come to the front, not a new one by any means, but it has of late come into special prominence. In the North-East corner of Ireland there are four counties out of the thirty-two, which are resolutely opposed to Home Rule, and the Liberals have been challenged on the ground that they are not loyal to the principle of democracy, in not allowing the majority in this section to determine the authority by which they are to be governed. Of course, we have to recognise that every principle must be limited by practical considerations; you could apply the principle of majority rule to such small groups that any rule at all would become an absurdity. We have to recognise that this minority opposed to Home Rule is, in their own . .</p>
        <p>disfrict, in a respectable majority, that it is distinct from the otheckpait of Ireland in racial characteristics and :in religious ideas. Now this has been recognised in the compromise which was last night proposed by Mr. Asquith by the option of voting themselves out of the national scheme by majority. A LARGE CONCESSION AND DISAPPOINT-</p>
        <p>ME NT. This compromise introduces many inconveni- ences, and to some extent spoils the complete- ness and harmony of the plan. (Hear, hear.) It is undoubtedly a large concession, and at the same time a great disappointment to those who hoped for a complete and undivided Ireland. But circumstances have rendered it necessary, and this part of the conipromise seems to meet with general approval. The whole difficulty centres round what is to occur at the end of the six years' limit. The counties that vote them- selves out are, at the end of this period, to come automatically in, and Ireland, in its local gov- ernment, is to become a complete unity. No doubt as the matter is furthe9 discussed, we will ,have good reasons put before us why this inclu- sion should be compulsory. But, speaking for myself, and only for myself, I consider that it will be a grave reflection on British- statesman- ship if this, what is after all a minor.,point, should stand in the way of a settlement by con- sent. (Hear, hear.) The difficulty is that when two parties are in disagreement and approach each other by way of compromise, in the last move towards a settlement each party believes he has gone as far as possible, and hesitates going an inch further. A small point then assumes an importance which it otherwise would not possess, and prevents a reconciliation. This is the danger at the present hour. But it will not be creditable to British common sense if this minor point should result in a rupture. (Applause.) A BAR TO DEVELOPMENT. _ .</p>
        <p>At the same time I think we have to recog- nise what a serious bar to the development of Ireland the permanent existence of two forms of government will produce. We will have the new local Irish Parliament and a continuance of the old control by the Irish Boards in the non-included counties. (Hear, hear.) We have to remember that these two authorities will be concerned entirely with local affairs. They will have to deal with the material pro- blems which arise out of the physical conditions of the country, one in itself, but split up in control. Cast your eye of imagination over the map of Ireland. Suppose, as is probable, that the four counties, Antrim, Down, Armagh and Londonderry, elect to keep out of the national settlement. You will notice that a railway connects Belfast and Dublin. This railway serves towns in both districts. How difficult it will be for the Irish Parliament to iniroduce reforms in the control of this railway, when two governing bodies have to be consulted. Or take the canals. Here is a canal going through Tyrone and Monaghan, two Catholic counties, but also going through Antrim and Down, two Protestant counties. Suppose that the Irish Parliament decided to widen this canal; it would be useless to do so unless the Protestant counties agreed to widen their part. Take the question of roads. The Protestant section might desire to improve a certain road, but unless the Irish section were at the same time agreeable, and had funds to deal with their portion the improvement would be blocked. Follow out in your thought all the questions that come up in regard to county government, in physical things, like rivers, canals, water- sheds, drainage, railways, which minister to the needs of Nationalist as well as Unionist. Any alteration, improvement, &amp;c., suggested by one interest is apt to be blocked by the other interest in disagreeing, or not having the funds to carry out their part. When you think of these things in detail you begin to realise what a tremendous disadvantage it is to cut the country in two, and have different controlling authorities for each part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>NATIONALIST SACRIFICE.</head>
        <p>THE NATIONALIST SACRIFICE.</p>
        <p>God 'lath joined Ireland together as a whole, and it is a tremendous pity that racial and reli- gious difficulties should be allowed to tear it asunder. (Applause.) If you follow out this thought you will realise what a sacrifice the Nationalists have made in agreeing for a time to this separation, what a disappointment it is to their hopes, and also their anxiety, that the ,separation should only be for a limited time. I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>DISCUSSION.</head>
        <p>THE DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>Mrs. OSBORNE proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the Chairman for the able manner in which .be had conducted the business, and his inter- esting address on Home Rule. Mr. SHERBERN seconded. He said they all knew of the great work the Chairman did in connection with that Association, and the ad- mirable manner in which he conducted the business. (Hear, hear.) OPPOSITION TO CONCESSIONS. Mr. GEORGE WALKER, supporting, said that he felt his Liberalism very much strained by the concessions made. He was of the opinion that whatever concessions were made, the Opposi- tion would not accept them. (Hear, hear.) They were only weakening their own position by seeming to accommodate them. (Hear, hear.) Mr. DELANEY said he agreed with Mr. Walker, If they asked him if he agreed with the move- ment taken by the Cabinet of the day he would have to say "No." That was the belief not only of himself, but of the whole of the Irish. speaking race throughout the world. Mr. John Redmond had said the previous day that the present concessions would be the last to be made in Ulster. (Applause.) lie (Mr. Delaney) did not know in the histoi'y of Liberalism when the time was more opportt.ne for them to be united in one solid body, fo:. they did not know what might happen in the next few days or months. He believed that with wisdom, discretion and proper management, the Government would be returned again. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>GOVERNMENT KNOW THEIR BUSINESS. Mr. B. HALL thought they were placing them_ selves in a high position in criticising Mr. Asquith, who had the inner workings and the greater knowledge of the situation at his dis- posal. He thought the reason why the conces- sion had been made was that Mr. Asquith knew that after the vote was taken it would be decid- edly in favour of Home Rule, and for that reason they were given the opportunity of accepting it, which he did not think they would. The opportunity would be lost altogether, and Home Rule would be forced in; the, way Mr. Delaney expected it. The Government knew their busi- ness better than those present could teach them. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>PREPARED TO FOLLOW PREMIER ANYWHERE. _</head>
        <p>PREPARED TO FOLLOW PREMIER ANYWHERE. _</p>
        <p>Captain ROBERTS said that if the concessions were not accepted the country would be able to pronounce and say with no uncertain voice as to who wonld be responsible for any bloodshed in Ireland whet' the Bill was passed. (Ap- plause.) They had to trust their leaders, and for his part he was prepared to follow Mr. Asquith anywhere. • (Applause.) Mr. ISRAEL was sorry there had been a dis- cordant note. It would go forward that there was a split in the Liberal party with regard to the proposals of Mr. Asquith. (Voices: No, No.") He suggested a vote of confidence in the Government. (Hear, hear.) Ireland best and what w Mr. E. LLOYD JONES said that for his_part he , would rather the Cabinet had gone straight ahead. (Hear, hear.) But they had to consider what was best, and he agreed with a previous knewspeaker h e thatulB those Ulster wh o knew counties best Were thel Nation- alists,wereparnade practically whofore wigenreerlseatdoinlgretlhaeid a nd in Ulsterites consequence of their lack of knowledge did not understand—they he position.ndt—the yonly judged m hearsay what He was not one to consider HOMO Rule for Ireland sim- ply from the Irish standpoint, but to confer it on the broad grounds of Liberalism—on demo- cratic lines—and Liberals said they should have it because the people demanded it. (Aptlause.)</p>
        <p>tEhNe Tirish odf, with the forwarded to the Government. SUPPORT FOR THE GOVERNIN a vote of confidence be spien3ociprelr.eeDsfteoLrmAtiwoEtYhi,ov met 0, tlsilhemotswp otkhee, 11)0 ryoapl toys e A N—Prhause.) Mr. ISRAEL seconded. beh alf of the , The CHAIRMAN said the, proposition had his Mrs. Women's O sr! Liberal support A ssoe iaetdi 0 n !) n entire and cordial support. The trouble, he said, was that Ireland was now governed by 38 different boards, and it was a most clumsy form of government anyone could conceive. Some of the things done by the boards u-ere very good and some were bad, but whether good or bad, they had this crowning demerit: they did noi secure the co-operation of the Irish people in their own government. The vote of confidence with the Cr'overnment and the vote of thanks to the Chairman were carried unaninmusly.</p>
        <p>THE BUSINESS MEETING. i IMPROVED FINANCIAL POSITION. ' Mr. PARKER, in presenting the treasurer's re- port, mentioned the increase in subscriptions, ' which manifested the interest that Liberals took and their recognition of the obligati°, to sup- port the Association. He thought they might take that opportunity of congratulating Mr. Sanders for his energy in collecting such a sub- of Idi etahre bal The balance . ) satgaani tnisatl t ial ins mo umn, t a s. - £4 them the previous year was .273 Hsance against. 1(1., so that it had been reduced by about ..t69 or ../;70, which showed they were in a better financial position' now. (ApplauSe.) On the proposition of Mr. P. DELANEY, sec- onded by Mr. IlusitTos, the balance sheet was adopted.</p>
        <p>A BUSY AND SUCCESSFUL YEAR. The Secretary's report, submitted by Council- lor LOCKER, stated that all sections of the Pro- gressive party had had an exceptionally busy time during the year. The year's work began with a tea and concert in the Parr Hall on January 16th, in celebration of the coming into operation of the Insurance Act. The founda- tion stone of the new Memorial Hall was laid by Mrs. John Crosfield on May. Bth, and Mr. A. H. Crosfield presented the spoons and photo- graphs to parents who had received maternity benefits on the first day under the Act. They had listened to excellent speeches made by Sir Alfred Mond, M.P., the Right Hon. A. Birrell, K.C., M.P., Mr. J. E. Swift Mac Neil, M.P., Mr. Tom Wing, M.P., and others.</p>
        <p>_ House to house visits resulted in many new voters •being added to the list in the Revision Court, and the party could claim the honour of placing on the voters' list for the first time, tenants of the stalls in the Market. (Applause.) The Progressive Labour League had had the most successful 12 months since its inception. Eighteen open-air meetings were -held, and the</p>
        <p>~,r51411M1014,0,1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Women Create Enthusiasm.</head>
        <p>Women Create Enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>The report. continued : • The Women's Liberal Association have also had a very busy time. In addition to the con- tinual round of ward teas and socials, the ladies successfully carried out a sewing class picnic to Southport, the annual picnic to Belle Vue on September 29th, and the annual fancy dress ball in the Parr Hall on October 23rd.</p>
        <p>As a result of the co-operation between the men and the women, inaugurated during the year, new members are being made, the Ward Committees are taking a keener interest in the work, and last, but not least, the ladies are in- fusing new life in the whole party by their enthusiasm and their readiness to take any part which may be allotted to them in the work of organisation. Various duties have been given to them to carry out at the General Elec- tion, which will be of inestimable value to the party. In fact, there never was a better under- standing between the ladies and the Senior Association.</p>
        <p>On November 10th, for the first time for many years. a Liberal Mayor was appointed, and we all offer our heartiest congratulations to Coun- cillor Peacock on the way he is worthily up- holding the traditions of that very high office.</p>
        <p>Past Mistakes to be Wiped.., Out. We appeal to all our leaders and workers to assist again to carry out the splendid pro- gramme we have before us, and to put in all they can, from now onward to the General Elec- tion, when we have not the least doubt the mis- takes of the past will be wiped out entirely by the return of a Liberal member for Warring- ton by a substantial majority. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>In conclusion our thanks are due to the workers of the wards for the magnificent back- ing they have given and are giving to the Liberal cause; to the Ladies' Association for their readiness at all times to assist; to the speakers at all our meetings; to Mr. J. B. Mar- -tindale and Mr. P. M. Oliver for their excel- lent addresses on many occasions, and their kind offers of assistance in the future; to the Progressive Labour League and the Junior Liberals for their excellent propaganda work; to the artistes who have so kindly helped in making the functions we have held such a pro- nounced success; to the ,Cup Committee for their self-sacrificing efforts to make the sum- mer months pass so pleasantly for our ward workers; to the auditor, Mr. J. G. Chappell; and last, but not least, to the Committee of the Liberal Club for their kindness at all times in granting us the use of the rooms, and to the members of the Liberal Club for their generous assistance on all occasions. (Applause.) Mr. CLEGG moved the adoption of the report, and said they were really a live institution The report showed the great progress being made, and he had every hope for a prosperous future. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Captain ROBERTS seconded, and the report was adopted.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR LOCKER CONFIDENT.</p>
        <p>In reply Councillor LOCKER said there had never been a year in Warrington where Liberals had been so much alive. (Hear, hear.) The interest and enthusiasm displayed showed that they were all determined to return theiz 'esteemed friend, Mr. Crosfield, at the next elec- tion. (Cheers.) Both he and his wife were full of fight, and they hoped both of them would maintain the attitude they showed on the occa- sion of their recent visit. Their visits always gave an impetus to the party, and would mean that there would be no doubt as to the ultimate result. Everything seemed to be moving in the world of Liberalism for the rank and file to put their backs into the work, and with the body of gentlemen they had behind that association, and with the cordial assistance of the ladies and the Junior Liberals, there was no reason to doubt that they would render a good account of themselves at the next election. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>MR. PARKER RE-ELECTED PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Captain ROBERTS proposed that Mr. Parker be re-elected President. (Applause.) He did not think they could do better. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Parker had held that office for a number of years, and he did an enormous amount of 'Work for Liberalism in the town—a good deal mom than maly of them were aware. Mr. CRITCHLEY seconded, and said Mr. Parker was a worthy representative as president of the association.</p>
        <p>The proposition was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF "THE EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>Mr. PARKER, replying, said he had held tha office for eleven years, and any Person who had been in harness that long was likely to get a bit rusty, but he did not feel so. (Hear, hear.) As soon as he did show any indication of rust he hoped they would turn him out, and put in a more active man. (Laughter.) On the last occasion he was there he made an, announcement which was intensely interesting to the party in connection with certain changes then being made in their Liberal newspaßer. He asked them to do what they could individually in support of that paper, because he thought that everyone would realise what an important factor it was in educating, developing, and con- centrating the thought and enthusiasm of the party, that they should have a well-conducted, successful, and energetic paper to lead them, as far as literature could lead them. (Applause.) "I made that appeal at that time," continued Mr. Parker, "and I want to thank you foi responding to that appeal. / take it for granted you have responded, because the increase—the weekly increase—in the circulation of 'The Warrington Examiner shows it. (Hear, hear.) It is scarcely for me, and yet I do not know but it is for me, in your name, to thank Mr. Weston for the energy, intelligence and improvement he has put into that paper. (Applause.) The increased circulation and standing of the paper have brought in advertisements freely, because if anyone in the town wants to be known at all now he cannot help but advertise in The Examiner.' (Hear, hear.) Still there are a great many small advertisements you might send—if you want to record the death of a mother-in-law, for • instance." (Laughter.).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>VICE-PR ESI DENTS.</head>
        <p>VICE-PR ESI DENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Huorms moved, and it was seconded, that the following gentlemen be re-elected vice- , presidents: Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Mr. Ziba Armi- tage, Aldermen W. Pierpoint, James Evans, D. Tinnion and Thomas Burton, Councillors Henry Roberts and J. Halliwell, Messrs. W. Bibby, G. Walker, Joseph Wilkinson, E. Wynne Jones, W. Russell, J. Jarman, J. G. Withinehaw, 3.</p>
        <p>inimENNIMIN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Two more Warrington People's Experience with Doan's Backache Kidney Pills.</head>
