      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Sciatica.</head>
        <p>Sciatica.</p>
        <p>On March 21st, 1911, Mr. C. W. King, of "Cheshire View," Manchester-road, War- rington, sold: "1 caught a severe chill which I think must have affected my kidneys, because I had an attack of sciatica afterwards, which laid me up for three months. A sharp, cutting pain used to start in my left hip, and shoot right down my knee. I was in dreadful pain night and day. "At times I was almost helpless. I could not get any sleep at night, and was confined to the house for !tree months. "I tried remedies before I used Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, but was unable to get any relief. This medicine, however, soon did me good, and by the time I had taken five boxes I was quite cured, and able to return to my work. " I am only too pleased to give this account of my experience for the benefit of other sufferers. I have every reason to speak highly of Doan's Pills, and now I never miss an opportunity of recommend- ing them. (Signed) "C. W. KING." On November 22nd, 1913—oven TWO YEARS LATER-Mr. King said: "1 heartily confirm all I said for Doan's Backache Kidney Pills. I shall always be grateful for the lasting good they have done me."</p>
        <p>Acute Pains in the Back.</p>
        <p>On September 11th, 1905, Mr. T. fligby. of 121, Church-street, Warrington, said: "Through having to work out ill all</p>
        <p>weathers I became subject to kidney trouble. For several years I was a sufferer from acute pains in my back, which were sometimes so bad that I could scarcely get about. I was almost afraid to stoop, for it was a diffi- cult matter to straighten myself up again. "For three years I took different medi- cines, but with no good results. It was different, however, when I started with Doan's Backache Kidney Pills; they did me good from the first, and brought me back to health. I am thankful for the benefit I de- rived from Doan's Pills, and shall do all I can to •make them wider known. (Signed) "T. RIGBY."</p>
        <p>On November 21st, 1913—OVER EIGHT YEARS Li,rea—Mr. Rigby said: "1 still keep very well indeed. I have often recommended Doan's Pills. They have done me lasting good."</p>
        <p>These patients had only a few of the symptoms of kidney trouble, for no patient suffers from them all. Other symptoms are :—Backache, Urinary Disorders, Gravel, Dropsy, Rheumatism, Sciatica, Nervous Ills, Irregular Ileart, etc.</p>
        <p>Doan's Pills are solely for kidney and bladder troubles, which explains their suc- cess. It is not safe to delay in treatment of any signs of kidney trouble. A book on the subject will be sent free to anyone. (See address below.)</p>
        <p>219 a box, 1319 for 6 boxes, of all chemists. or from Foster-McClellats Co., 8, Wells Street, Oxford Street, London, W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>DOAN'S %fig," PILLS.</head>
        <p>DOAN'S %fig," PILLS.</p>
        <p>Dodds, H. G. Roberts, William Clegg, M. Howarth, T. Dale and Jos. Ainsworth. The resolution was carried unanimously. A GOOD WORKER.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. GEORGE WALKER had pleasure in moving that Councillor Locker he the honorary secm- tary again. They sympathised with Mr. Locker in his ill-bealth—(hear, hear)--hut hoped he would soon be well and strong again. He had put in a good deal of work for the association, and he had a good assistant in Mr. Sanders They hoped that by their joint efforts the busi- ness would be conducted just as successfully in the future as in the past. Mr. B. HALL seconded, and the proposition was carried. _</p>
        <p>Councillor LOCKER, in reply, said nothing would give him greater pleasure than to serve the Liberal party. (Applause.) He was encour- aged in his duties by the enthusiasm displayed by the members of the party. He would like to see a favourable result at the election before leaving office. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>• HONORARY TREASURER.</head>
        <p>• HONORARY TREASURER.</p>
        <p>CRITCHLEY proposed that Mr. Parker be the honorary treasurer of the association, be- cause he did not think they could have the office in better hands. (Hear, hear.) Mr. GEORGE WEAVER, in seconding, said they could not do a better thing. Councillor LOCKER supported. Mr. Parker, he said, had rendered invaluable. services to the party. (Hear, hear.) The proposition was carried unanimously. Mr. PARKER agreed to serve, but hoped they would find 'someone else in the party to take the office, as it was not good for one to hold i. too often.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE. It was unanimously decided that the follow- ing be the co-optive members of the Finance Committee: Alderman Tinnion, Councillor Wil- kinson, Councillor Hawthorn, Councillor Halli- well, Councillor Lindley, Messrs. G. Weaver, W. Bibby, G. Walker, P. Delaney, E. W. Jones, J. Critchley and T. Pemberton.</p>
        <p>EXECUTIVE OFFICERS. The executive meeting was then held, and the Cuttraivor said the last chairman of the execu- tive was the Mayor, but he thought that Coun- cillor Peacock had wisely decided that whilst he held his office he ought not to take an active part in politics, and had retired from the posi- tion. (Hear, hear.) The deputy-chairman Was Councillor Wilkinson. Mr. DELANEY proposed that Councillor Wilkin- son be the chairman, and this was agreed to unanimously. Mr. CLEGG nominated Mr. George Walker for the deputy-chair, but Mr. Walker, whilst thank- ing them for their confidence, said it was im- possible for him to accept the office. On the proposition of Mr. WEAVER, seconded by Mr. A. EDWARDS, Capt. Roberts was ap- pointed deputy-chairman. Capt. ROBERTS said the position was a respon- sible one, and he would like time to think over the matter and let them know as early as pos- sible. It was agreed to await Capt. Roberts's deci- sion. --- - - -</p>
        <p>THE HELP OF THE WOMEN LIBERALS. Mrs. OSBORNE. as secretary of the Women's liiie work h bewraolAsse had donetion,t n haonkteidlei Mr rb.ehSaalnfders(_3p for t_ plause.) Continuing, she said: It must be one of us present to hear report of the past year's work gratifyingsca splendid e t nod iedv ery of the Liberal forces. (Applause.) Personally, I have been much encouraged by the large at- tendances at the ward meetings, and by the en- thusiasm displayed both by men and women. We women have taken our share in working towards a better understanding in the organise,. tion of the wards. (Hear, hear.) Ward cap. tame and auxiliaries are doing spade work and taking a great and active interest. A good deal of educational work in a quiet way is done by our Women's Liberal Association—apart from the ordinary routine of meetings—"helping the men" and all that that Involves, which does not make much of a show in the local papers, but which, perhaps, does more than anything to make Liberalism a living force. (Hear, hear.) We Liberal women intend to form up solid be- hind our friends in the House of Commons. They need our backing, just as much as we need their leadership; and if we all here to- night make up our mind to band together for the purpose of working for the return of Mr. I Crosfield to Parliament, I say, in all sincerity; we can do it. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>CLEAR SKIN.</head>
        <p>CLEAR SKIN.</p>
        <p>DIMPLES, Blackheads, Blotches, guaranteed 1 Cured. A healthy Clear Complexion ensured by using " PIMOTIO, ' a lotion prepared from pre- scription of a London Physician, post free, includ- ing full original instructions, is. 6d. and 2s. 6d.. from PIMOTIO PREPARATIONS CO..</p>
        <p>8, Argyll-place, Regent-etreet, -London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>ADULT SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>THE ADULT SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Incidents and Addresses at the Sunday- and Other Gatherings.</p>
        <p>MUSIC INFLUENCE ON LIFE.</p>
        <p>At the Academy-street Women's Class, pre- sided over by Mrs. Hill, the Bible-lesson was on "Training in light Judgment" The discussion on this subject took up the most of the evening. The rest of the evening was spent in a social manner. Mrs. M. Cox recited the "Cottage by the way," and Mrs. Hill recited "Berlin Wool," -and were much appreciated. The Secretary is anxious that the women of the district should take advantage of these pleasant Wednesday evenings, and they will be heartily welcomed ' from 7.30 to 9 p.m. BEWSEY MEN'S. - -</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning Dr. J. S. Manson gave a very interesting lecture on "Eugenics." The speaker pointed out that the term was founded on two Greek words, and was originally coined by a former student of the old Warrington Academy. In the course of his address he showed that there was nothing distasteful in the right advocacy of Eugenics, but all would tend towards the production of a robust, virile race of sons and daughters. He showed the relative values of heredity and environment in producing a healthy state of affairs, demon- strating clearly that with right heredity and environment a most excellent state of affairs would be produced. He pointed out that it was not to the leisured classes we should look foi the building up of the race, nor, on the other hand, to the under-fed or improvident, but to the middle portion of our race, who carried out steady, arduous daily toil, and lived clean, moral lives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>SANKEY _ BRIDGES MEN'S.</head>
        <p>SANKEY _ BRIDGES MEN'S.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning Mr. William Landon gave the second of his lectures on Shakespeare, tak- ing as his subject " Shakespeare the Man." STOCKTON HEATH MEN'S.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance on Sunday morning to hear Mr. George Fleet, a member of the school, give an excellent paper on The Wonders of Nature." The speaker based his remarks chiefly from trees grown in our forests ttad parks, the trees specially noted being the oak, beech, elm, birch, weeping willow, ash, horse chestnut, acacia, cypress, pine, myrtle, eucalyptus, cork cedar, palm, and others. The special features of each tree and the uses of its timber based in its history were well defined by the speaker. Several useful and ornamental specimens were on exhibition, and the school tendered its warmest approval and thanks for the interesting talk.</p>
        <p>ACADEMY-STREET MEN'S. " Music and its influence upon life" was the title of a nicely composed paper given by Mr. C. R. Muir at the Academy-street Men's Adult School on Sunday morning. Plato said "Music as moral law gives soul to the universe. wings to the mind, flight to the imagination, charm to the voice, gaiety and life to everytting." For a descriptive piece the speaker portrayed in words both eloquent and lucid the various scenes and effects indicated in the "Storm." Kingsley compared music to the speech of angels, but the speaker would frequently sub- stitute "the speech of God." As an aid to memory there is no gainsay. Accent in speech is a sort of chanting, and almost every parish has its peculiarities. Music is really ennobling, but it is often employed as a bait to abuse and degradation. If, however, it is used in coin. junction with higher arts, sciences and motives it can be a means of obtaining greater unison and harmony in our lives. ADULT SCHOOL CONFERENCE. The yearly meetings of the Lancashire and Cheshire Adult Schools Unions were held at Morecambe on Saturday and Sunday. Nearly 100 delegates were present. Mr. John A. Ran some (Warrington) presided, and was accom- panied by Mr. H. Ward (Birkenhead), the union secretary. Reports were dealt with respecting the work accomplished in 1913, including those bf the Rest' House at -Heys Farm, Clitheroe, and the co-operative holiday held at Bentham. Reports were also given concerning the propaganda work of Mr. D. P. Boyd, the national secreta7, and the lecturer to schools (Professor G. Currie Martin). Mr. Ransome allnded to the -eneral a ork of the movement, which comprised 2,000 schools with a membership of over 100,000 men and women. Its main object, he said, was educa- tional, and the schools were neither r?litical nor sectarian, and thus persons embracing all. shades of opinion could meet together and (iis- cuss fully and freely matters of common inter- est. Social problems also received a very large share of attention. Mr. G. A. Dunlop (Warrington) said the Adult School movement was the only movement which he knew to be capable of establishing an on- sectarian haFis for Christian effort and unity.</p>
      </div>
