      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>CONCESSIONS FOR PEACE.</head>
        <p>CONCESSIONS FOR PEACE.</p>
        <p>On Monday night Mr. ASQUITH made his statement on the Home Rule concessions, and they are now being considered by the country. The Government plan for bringing about a settlement of the present strained situation may be summarised thus :</p>
        <p>(1) On requisition of, say, one-tenth of the electors of an Ulster county, a poll will be taken to decide whether the county (Derry and Belfast ranking as counties for this purpose) shall be excluded from Home Rule.</p>
        <p>(2) An .excluded county will be placed outside the legislative authority of the Irish Parliament .and the executive authority of the Irish Gov- ernment, and will be represented, as at present, subject to changes involved by any measure of re-distribution, in the Parliament at West- minster.</p>
        <p>(3) The vote will be exercised by persons now entithxl to vote at Parliamentary elections.</p>
        <p>(4) The ill decidetended.' ( ex period of exclusion will be six years. 5) The imperial Parliament w whether that period shall be lion, but the few who, like The proposals have met .withthae a victory for vlalndieedpernecedenPi i Nationalists, regard them as AiQurnfs aim was to bring about "Orange bluff," must remember t athahteal Mr. measure. So far as the friends of the Govern- ment are concerned, there is a general belief that the Government have made a worthy bid for a peaceable and honourable settlement, and, like the members of the Warrington Liberal Association, are prepared to place their confi- dence in the Ministry to the fullest extent. On the Unionist side there has been a commend- able desire to treat the offer seriously, and Sir EDWARD CARSON'S offer has been regarded as of good omen. "If," he said, "the exclusion of Ulster was made permanent, he would go over to Ireland, call a convention, and leave it. to the Ulster Covenanters whether, if they would be in perpetuo free from any legislative or admin- istrative control of an Irish Parliament, they would allow, without fighting, an Irish Parlia- ment to be established." This Is not far removed from the opinion of Nfr. CHARLES PARKER, who told the Liberal Association that he agreed with the suggested exclusion, but would have preferred an unconditional exclu- sion, leaving the natural course Of events to teach Ulster the wisdorn'and need of inclusion. When we get so near agreement as this Home Rule for Ireland appears to be only a question . of measurable time,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <p>_</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>AN OTHER ' DE,P GTAT I 0 N .</p>
        <p>- - (tour friends the enemy,•have set us a poser. litr. W. 11. Milan .has written us to say that before his letter is in print "another deputa- %ion" will be ".preparing to leave for Ireland." Why, we *wonder:! We thought that the first deputation had finally, and for all time, de- cided that the Home Rule Bill was a "blunder and a crime," and that the matter was at an end. We have even heard it suggested that Mr. Asquith% concessions were the natural out- come of their celebrated " Report " and tele- grams, though, to be candid, tve have heard . .</p>
        <p>it whispered -that some of the members of the' deputatiou have voted Carson a traitor if he accepts the .Government scheme! We further wonder if the reason for this .second .picnic is contained in the concludrhg •portion of Mr. Bl- lian?s j 116/Iter. "I am anxious," he winds up, 1 "that ne effort shall be spared to help us to make a report that cannot be challenged as , being an :honest effort to get at the Truth." Was the first report not the Truth—with a: capital T—and is that why a second bite at the same cherry is necessary? Or was not every "honest °effort " made on that rollicking occa- sion that might have :been made to get at the truth? We had, and expressed, grave doubts our- selves as to the 'extent and honesty of those, efforts:; but there:seems Lobe cause•to fear that the same fundamental faults will lie with this trip as with the last. Who will compose the: party? Who will finance it? Will there be "Liberals" in it? Will they be the same "Liberals" as before, or fresh ones? We have made inquiries of the officials of :the Liberal Association, but they were as surprised as we were to hear of this second venture. The only: internal evidence the letter produces on this asME.Wße,e,lliwanh ;Let indicates re: again. But he also says Hayes will•send them on in-1 pointptt hhr ate that tt e hNn4ietsr confusing.ur bbt ace tt itiohnHe formation from the Conservative 'Club, which suggests that Mr. Hayes will.not be there. But; after all, why a second deputation, when the first had so satisfactorily settled matters? It seems to be nothing more nor less than a re- flection upon the first batch, which seems un- kind. It was enough that opponents heaped ridicule upon them; but this'throwing over by their own Cfriends is positively cruel. One word more. Mr. Bellian says: "I am writing this on the eve of our departure, and with no idea as to the conclusions the party will come •to." Did his previous deputation come to conclusions before they departed? -We' hinted that they did. In any case, Mr. Bellian has already come to a conelusion—see his tele- gram and 'The Report." The Report" is too long for reproduction, but Mr. Bellian's vidual opinion, so urgent that it had to be wired, said:—</p>
        <p>"humanity demands that we should oppose Hi:4lla Rule. Bellian (C).."</p>
        <p>OUR POPULAR MAYOR. The %Or iscertainly Setting the pace, as Mr. S.'W. Jeffery stated at the Children's Bulb I( is difficult to foretell where he will appear next, but we shall save ourselves a deal of trouble if we content ourselves by reflecting that before he lays down his robe of office he will have done everything that may become a man—and a Mayor. Last week he gave a frarged photograph of himself to Wycliffe Sch6ol, and this week the Mayoress has given a framed photograph of herself to Silver-street School—lastinglreminders that both have come from the people, and are proud of the fact. It is good to have a full-time Mayor, who has no distractions to take some of his attention from his official duties, The.best testimony we have 'heard to "the. manner in which Councillor Peter Peacock is serving the town is the declaration by a„responsible citizen that it would he a pro.fltable thing to appoint him per- nianent Mayor at a salary.</p>
        <p>EL, DORADO WITHOUT AN ORGAN.</p>
        <p>Councillor Wilkinson, chairman of the Finance Committee, who, as local chancellor of the exchequer, ought to know, told the Council on Tuesday riight that Warrington is a little El dorado—one of the wealthiest towns in England. "And one of the stingiest," said the Mayor, who was evidently more than ordin- arily upset by the fact that he had been unable to find a wealthy man sufficiently generous to give an organ to Parr Hall. This comment to&amp; place on the passing of the rates, but it palpably had reference ,to the organ, :for his Worship made some pointed remarks on that subject when it was under discussion. That the Parr Hall stands in urgent need of a reli- ,able :instrument is. admitted, but meeting the , need is another matter. The Town Council have matte two starts upon what has proved to be the wrong tack, First the Local Govern- ment Board refused to sanction the borrowing of .the requisite £1,600; then the General Pur- poses Committee declined to make the sum a direct charge upon the rates. The Mayor ap- proached "several of our wealthiest gentle- men," and no doubt hinted to them an easy way of providing that their names should be linked with the sending forth of sweetest music for generations to come. None wanted the pleasure at the price and the Mayor made some remarks about doing something "while we are on the earth." Presumably, his Worship had exhausted his sweetest style before he felt im- pelled to adopt the opposite attitude. He, how- ever, seemed to imply that there was some hope still, and it would be well if this source of hope revealed itself quickly. A suggestion is made that the organ should be provided by public subscription, and if any of the gentlemen con- sulted by the Mayor would "head the list," there could be little doubt that the amount re- quired would be forthcoming. We need an organ in place of the apology for one that now plays when and how it likes.</p>
        <p>RATEPAYERS SATISFIED. The rates of Warrington are to remain un- changed. This is news which must satisfy the ratepayers. The Council also adopted the Budget this week, the result of which is that the rates remain as they were, at Bs. in the X. More money was required, but this is met by a further visit to those profitable "hen roosts," the trading departments run by the Corpora- tion. The tramways and water have been! called upon for more, chiefly to meet the re- quirements of education, which asks for £2,000 more. Councillor Osborne objected to the tram- ' ways department being asked to contribute a larger quota hi view of the condition of the permanent way. The gas department he also thought should not bear a larger call Until it had met the customers' claims for cheaper gas.</p>
        <p>WORKS BOWLING. With the spirit of independence born in each one, of us, that bids us rely upon ourselves rather) than lean on oars, the Works Bowling Association have determined to have greens and rooms of their own. If this ambition is to be achieved, there must be no "slacking" on anyone's part and all must stand resolutely by the officials. The advantages \of the scheme fire numerous, and .trundlers cannot but ap- prove.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PLAYGROUNDS,</head>
        <p>PLAYGROUNDS,</p>
        <p>At the Mayor's banquet on Thursday night, Mr. Charles E. Parker introduced the question, of providing playgrounds for children in differ- ent parts of the borough. It is somewhat, re-1 markable that this week we produce a letter' from -a Grappenhall correspondent saying how badly off the suburbs are in this respect com- pared with the town. The fact is that in both instances facilities lie close to hand, if we woultl only embrace them, to do a great deal in this direction at very small cost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION.</p>
        <p>It will be reported to the Education Com- mittee on Monday night that in view of the delay in the building of certain new schools and the lack of accommodation at certain e,xisting old schools, deductions from the grants of the latter have been made by the Local Govern- ment Board. With regard to Wycliffe Tempo- rary Council School, the Board have asked for the date upon which the new school in Lovely- lane will be ready for occupation. These are serious .questions,, and a deptitation has been appointed to interview the Board of Education accom- modation 0` in the 'borough generally." Coun- cillor Pemberton is to be One of the deputation. Will he ,mention "worms" don? theory in Lon-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>CHILD BULB GROWERS.</head>
        <p>CHILD BULB GROWERS.</p>
        <p>The cult of bulb growing has firmly seized upon the school children of Warrington, and the show of the result of their efforts redounded to their cre6it. Practical gardeners, with Lample glass accommodation at their disposal, :,..haye found, the season for bulb culture a bad one, yet the school children were able to pro. - , duce excellent .res'ults, as the bloom at the Drill Hall show amply :demonstrated. There were some 600 entries, and amongst these were many fine blooms. The idea of interesting the chil- dren in this class of culture is an excellent one, and should be fostered in every possible way. The Beautiful Warrington Siciely deserves all the help that the citizens can give.</p>
        <p>LITERARY AND PHILOSOPHICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>---- -- The reported decrease in the membership of the Warrington Literary and Philosophical ' society, although :not -so serious as in kindred societies elsewhere, causes one to ponder. The public to-day are not so interested in subjects of weight and importance as they were a couple of generations ago, and this lack of interest does not augur well for the future. There is.a spkit of flippancy abroad which is not ,good for the nation. Amusements have seized upon the people quite out of proportion to the value derived, and in consequence sueh societies as the Literary and Philosophical suffer. Still those who guide the fates of the society should not lose hope, and take their hand from the Plough they have steered to such usefUl pUP Pose. A reversion is bound to come—when. , none of us dare predict—and the society .will then come into its full inheritance as a guide of public opinion once more. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>LATE MR. GEORGE SIMPSON.</head>
        <p>LATE MR. GEORGE SIMPSON.</p>
        <p>An Impressive Funeral Service.</p>
        <p>Brief intimation was given in our last issue of the death of Mr. Geo. W. Simpson, eldest son of Mr. Charles Simpson, of Bewsey-road, who Passed away after a short illness at the age of 38 years. Deep sympathy and feeling were shown at the funeral, which took place on Sunday afternoon at the Warrington Cemetery. Relatives and mourners present were: Mr. and Mrs. C. Simpson, Mr. C. F. Simpson, Mr. T. A. Simpson, Mr. and Mrs. C. 4.Larson,Mr. J. Smith, Mr. G. Hudson, Mr. T. Hudson, Mrs. L. Larson, Mr. and Mrs. F. Hudson, Mr. .1. Atkinson, Mr. Wilkinson, Miss Ethel Shaw, Miss H. Esba Shaw, Mr. Harry Gaunt.</p>
        <p>A deputation from the Liberal Association consisted of Messrs. W. Heyes (chairman of the Billiards Committee), Messrs. F. MuHey, A. T. Clay (manager of the club), G. Shaw, W. Critch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <p>• , , ov*,„ k . . • • -.10( 4 •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>'LATE MR. SIMPSON.</head>
        <p>THE 'LATE MR. SIMPSON.</p>
        <p>ley, F. Walker, W. Patrick, A. Jones, R. Wilson. James Smith, C. Jordan, W. Langley, F. Plurapton. IT_ Sh- aw, T. Newnes and A. Crow. Rwulad` rhdbe, r ibjidt 7 y B ee James rap; 0 Carried?d and nsvdb William l aiaarmr y companyvitooyblien. so ,f the Bert who were his fellow-appientices—Messrs. Robt. Mr. Arthur Mounfield conducted services :u the chapel and at the gravesido. Floral tributes were reoeived from: Mr. and a Mrs. J. Smith, sister and brother (Garston); Mr. Alex. Smith, nephew (Garston); Mr. and Mrs. F. Hudson, uncle and aunt, and family s( ot (Garston); (Wigan); Mr. and 'Mrs. J. Hudson, uncle and Larson,aunt, c nudfl e family aunt(Gar C. H. Larson, brother-iil-law and sister (Gars-, )s;t o n Mr. and Mrs. Mr. and Mrs. ton); Mr. and Mrs. T. Hudson, uncle and aunt (Garston);members and friends of Liberal Club; his fellow-shopmates; . members of Bewsey Adult School; Mr. and Mrs. Pemlerton, Mrs. mu Atkinson .(Oldham), and Mr. J. .- Itkin (New York).</p>
        <p>Engineer Students Debate' CONVICT OUTWITTED BY GOVERNESS. At a meeting of the Warrington Engineering A convict who was found in the College one Students' Association, held at the Technical and explained his. presence night this week, Institute on Saturday night, a debate was held there by saying he wished to present religious on "Will internal combustion engines supersede periodicals to "the orphanage," was sent to steam engines?" a Mr. A. Light led the _r debatese ' combustion bu s stion engines. and gaol for sic months, with tile prospect of some faaliitiintr iltr unexpired "time" being remembered against engines. SomeM very interesting lantern slides w shown, and him. The smartness of the governess who dis- these were kindly lent by Messrs. Tangye, of covered the intruder, lured him into the College Birmingham. There was a good attendance, and v. U y Museum, and then . locked him in while she some very sound arguments were heard. Mr. N. 'phoned for the police, is a credit to the insti- E. France, the engineering lecturer at the tution. I Institute, presided. ,</p>
        <p>,tt *ll4 4 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>MAYOR'S BANQUET.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S BANQUET.</p>
        <p>" Lions and Lambs" Who Dined</p>
        <p>Together in Peace.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S PLAYGROUNDS SUGGESTED.</p>
        <p>Councillors did not wrangle over certain points in municipal government, no heated argu- ments or unseemly interjections were heard, no need for the chairman to ring the bell for order. Instead, the merry chink of glasses, good-humoured banter, witty and humorous speeches were the order on Thursday evening, when the Mayor gave a banquet in the Town , Hall.</p>
        <p>On the right the host had his Chaplain, the Rev. Canon Willis, and Councillor Lewis (Mayor of Widnes), whilst immediately on his left were Dr. Limont (Mayor of Southport) and the Deputyt'Mayor of Warrington (Councillor Dr. Joseph). The Revs. Father Rockliff and T. T. James were also amongst those present, and there was a full attendance of aldermen, coun- cillors, members of the Education Committee, and Corporation officials.</p>
        <p>An excellent menu was submitted, and Mr. and Mrs. Mitchell are to he congratulated on the admirable manner in which they catered.</p>
        <p>"BISHOPS, CLERGY, MINISTERS."</p>
        <p>Councillor lIEmsnALL proposed "The Bishops, Clergy, and all Ministers of Religion." He spoke of the time when he attended the Sunday school connected with the Parish Church, and also the Parochial School. In Sunday school the Superintendent proved himself most orthodox by apostolic blows and knocks. It was bad enough to be whacked from Monday to Friday when they did not deserve it, but to be whacked on Sunday when they did deserve it was more than flesh and blood could stand, so he went to St. Paul's, where they did not give the cane but barley sugar and all sorts of prizes. (Laugh- ter.) He thought they had considera.bly im- proved in regard to indiscreet Sunday school superintendents. In matters of education they had also improved wonderfully. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MINISTERIAL REPLY.</head>
        <p>A MINISTERIAL REPLY.</p>
        <p>Three of the clergy present replied, and the Rev. Canon WILLIS said he could not imagine a more anxious time than the present for all the Bishops. There were very important matters which would come before them very shortly. There was the government of Ireland, which was a religious as well as a political question, and other important matters. The Rev. Father ROCKLIFF, on behalf of his co-religionists, said the Mayor was a man amongst men. He had those characteristics which led invariably to success. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. JAMES said that the Free Churches did not claim to have any monopoly of the actual truth, but they stood for the con- victions which appealed to them, but they did claim to have history, tradition and memories which inspired them, and no less than members of other churches, they stood by their convictions and sought to be true. The Mayor had extended his sympathy to all classes of church life.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS proposed the toast of "The Imperial Forces," and Lieut.-Colonel FAIRCLOUGH replied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>"JOY OF PROSPECT."</head>
        <p>"JOY OF PROSPECT."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. UPSON proposed the toast of "The Chairman." The Mayor, he said: was in the somewhat unusual Position of having, not the satisfaction of retrospect, but the joy of pros- pect. Usually when a man. became Mayor it was the termination of his life's work, and after he had passed the chair he went into the lean and slippered state of life. The Mayor had been called to that office when, in this age of somewhat selfish materialism, a man gave all his time to the accumulation of wealth. It spoke volumes for Councillor Peacock when he ' had given up such a year of his life for public service. During his five months in the Mayoral chair he had justified the high expectations formed of his thoroughness, efficiency and sin- cerity in his work. Up to the present it had been for him, in the words of Browning, "Roses, roses, all the way," and that night they crowned him with a laurel wreath and offered him their floral tributes. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>TO FULFIL PROMISE.</head>
        <p>TO FULFIL PROMISE.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, in reply, said he would endeavour to fulfil the promise he made when elected to that office to do his duty fearlessly and with- out favour to all parties. (Hear, hear.) He returned thanks to all those who had extended their kindnesses to him. One of his characteris- tics, he might say, was that he had a nasty trick of saying what he thought, and meaning what lie said. At the same time he did not do it to hurt anyone, but life was too short to mince matters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>"THE LIONS AND THE LAMBS."</head>
        <p>"THE LIONS AND THE LAMBS."</p>
        <p>Councillor Dr. JOSEPH proposed the toast "Other Municipalities," and dealt more par- ticularly with Widnes and Southport. It was very important that various municipalities should be on the best of terms. In Warrington they heard about the "lions and the lambs," but if they were to ask him which were the lions and which were the lambs, lie would have to say, like the showman, whose menagerie was poorly stocked, ," Whichever you choose, my dears." (Laughter.) The MAYORS of Southport and Widnes replied.</p>
        <p>PLAYGROUNDS FOR CHILDREN. Mr. G. E. PARKER proposed " The Co_Tora- Hon " toast, and made an admirable suggestion. He said:</p>
        <p>Warrington is pretty well provided with parks, bowling greens and other forms of recreation for the older members of the community, but there seems to me to be a very great want in this town of playgrounds for children within easy access of their homes, and where the parents can exer- cise some control over them. There is much land in Warrington which could be got at a very small cost, and which would be useful for that purpose. Swings, &amp;c., could be put up in four or five places, so that the children who have no other playgrounds than the street could have little places fenced off in which to play. In some places there is organised play for the children. Perhaps this is outside our possibili- ties at this time, but I say if you only give the children some little accessory, some little help for their play, they will develop their power of play, and that has a wonderful effect on the children tho have those privileges. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>"A WORTHY MAYOR." Alderman BOLTON'S speech, in reply, was full of humour, and he led off with the statement that Councillor Peacock was a worthy Mayor, because he had voted him into the position of alderman. (Laughter.) As regarded the mem- bers of the Town Council, they had all sorts of brains and dreamers. They had financiers, and some of the best brains in the town on the Town Council, but sometimes they were a bit peculiar. (Laughter.) Nine cases out of ten they tried to be as obnoxious as they could, but still they were better than other town councils. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>OTHER TOASTS. Capt. 'CROSFIELD proposed The County Bor- ough Magistrates," and said the Warrington Bench always tempered justice with mercy. Alderman TINNioN replied, and said the War- rington Bench did its work seriously, fearlessly and absolutely honestly. (Hear, hear.) The toast of the "Town and Trade of War- rington" was honoured, on the proposition of Alderman BENNETT: and Mr. W. P. ELANDS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>EIGHT-HOUR SHIFTS FOR BLAST FURNHCES.</head>
        <p>EIGHT-HOUR SHIFTS FOR BLAST FURNHCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield's Picture of What Is and- What Might Be.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>NEEDED REFORM in 'CONTINUOUS' INDUSTRIES</head>
        <p>NEEDED REFORM in 'CONTINUOUS' INDUSTRIES</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield continues in to-day's "Examiner" his demand for the replacing of trie twelve-hour shift in continuous indus- tries by an eight-hour shift.</p>
        <p>He urges that constant labour is good neither for tue worker nor the employer, and urges that in the best interests of the State reform should be insisted upon, and that England, that already knows the benefit of a partial adoption of the eight-hour shift, should lead the rest of the world into proper channels.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>In my article published in your issue of February 14th I described the policy of the International Association, for Labour Legisla- tion with reference to the efforts it is making to improve conditions of employment for the shift workers, first of all in the iron and steel trade, and subsequently in all other continuous industries. The object is to substitute three shifts of men working eight hours a day for two shifts of men working twelve hours, wher- ever it is necessary to carry on manufacture continuously right through the twenty-four hours. Let us now consider the results that may be .anticipated when we have achieved that object. We have some substantial data already to guide us, and if in the first instance we seek to investigate the effect of such a reform on the operatives engaged in one of these continuous industries in their capacity as citizens of the State and burgesses of their own locality, or its influence upon them, their wives and their families, throughout the home life of the com- munity, we cannot wish for a better illustration than we get if we take an industry where the two systems are at the present time in opera- tion in different districts of the same country. Let us glance for a moment at those districts in the North of England, where the eight-hour shift has been universally adopted for men engaged at the blast furnaces, and then turn our eyes to Northamptonshire and South Derby- shire, where men are still groaning under the execrable, discredited and out-of-date twelve- hour shift.</p>
        <p>A Dismal Picture. As this industry is neeessarily continuous, and cannot be stopped at week-ends, these men are compelled by the thousand to work an average of 84 hours a week—that is to Say, 72 hours one Week and 96 the next. It is necessary for them to work 96 hours every other week, because they have then one shift of 24 hours' continuous work, that double shift being required in order that men may change from the day shift to the night shift, and vice versa. Not only are these hours of work outrageously long, but the work itself is for a considerable part of the time done in great heat, besides which men inhale a certain amount of noxious fumes. Work of such a character notoriously makes men old long before their time, as a result of being constantly and systematically worked to the point of exhaustion and beyond it. Instead of working to live such men literally and un- happily only live to work. The all-round civi- lised life open to men nowadays who have their fair share of leisure is for them a sheer impos- sibility. They have neither the mental nor the physical energy left that would enable them te pursue it, What, for instance; is literature, with all its varied delight and inexhaustible treasure—(" The hoarded spoils and legacies of time")—to such a man? It is little more than a I sealed book. What chance has he to stimulate ' his imagination and refresh himself with that other "most enduring form of refined pleasure" (to apply to music what has been said of litera- ture), which is to be found in the world of art? To 1p found and enjoyed there, true, but only when) some patient preliminary study makes it possible to appreciate and profit by what it offers. A man physically and mentally played out has no mind to give to such study. Or again, what about the magical fairyland and wonderland of modern science? He scarcely gets a glimpse of it.</p>
        <p>In public affairs, in the politics of his part', in the focal government of his own town, and so forth, he cannot play an eteient part; while even of those field sports and forms of outdoor recreation, which, to city-bred folk are nowa- days such a God-send, and, indeed, positive necessity—even of those he is scarce able to take advantage. In point of fact, it is too often found that such men, jaded and wearied, and toil-worn as they are, turn to liquor or sensu- ality as the only kind of change which they can appreciate as a respite from their life of ' monotonous drudgery. Obviously they are not the only sufferers by this; the wife, the family, the entire home life suffers, too—and the State with them.</p>
        <p>elusively commercial point of view, he will find that the gain in the long run largely exceeds the sacrifice. 1 do not say that is always the case. It is not, and, happily for the credit Of British employers of labour, there are plenty of them who would gladly enough make sacrifices. and large sacrifices, too, to assist in bringing about so beneficent a change in industrial coildtions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Economic and Social Value of Leisure.</head>
        <p>Economic and Social Value of Leisure.</p>
        <p>Turn from this dismal picture to the cheerful aspect of those districts where the eight-hor usilift is in force. It is in now the language of exaggeration, but simply a statement of fact, that the contrast between the two is just the difference beween industrial barbarism and in- dustrial civilisation. Where a man has 16 hours out of the 24 for leisure, rest, the home life, study, culture, recreation, public work, or what net, practically all the amenities of a civilised environment are available for him, and, of course, the entire family shares in these advan- tages.</p>
        <p>But are they too dearly purchased? Has the State to pay for the increased cost of production more than it can afford? Far from It. For a thousand reasons—and I have already referred to some of the most important—the State gains, and gains enormously, by the more enlightened and more scientific system of work. With re- gard to employers of labour, the results of such a change will vary greatly in different kinds of work. Generally speaking, such a change will involve some sacrifice, even a considerable sac- rifice, on the part of the employer at the outset, and that is why the employer often hesitates. He sees the extra shift to be manned; he sees the very large addition to the wages bill. He may not see or foresee—the speeding up of work which, without excessive and injurious ' stimulation, will naturally follow, and will give him a largely-increased output from any given plant; he may not realise that besides this largely-increased quantity he will also get better quality of work. That will be partly the result of the fact that improved conditions will attract better men to his factory, and that those same men, fresh, alert and 'vigorous at their work, will be able to follow it with an attention and an intelligence for which the employer will look in vain among a staff that are chronically wearied and stale. For precisely the same reason there will be less risk of accident! Then the health of the employes will be better; an epidemic will less easily take hold of them, and so, year in and year out, there will be less 41)- sence from work through sickness and less irregularity in the way work is done. The very plant itself will be better cared for, and last, but not least, men will work cheerfully and willingly and take some interest and pride in their work; as a consequence of which there will be considerably less likelihood of the dis- location of the work of the factory through industrial unrest. So that on the whole these advantages are calculated to prove a very sub- stantial off-set to the increase in the employer's wages bill; and not seldom, even if lie looks at the thing--as he ought not to do—from an ex-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Co-opiration to Prevent Sacrifice.</head>
        <p>Co-opiration to Prevent Sacrifice.</p>
        <p>But the International Association for Labour Legislation is not making such sacrifices on the part of employers of labour a condition for the furtherance of this great reform. On the con- trary, it urges international co-operation so that employers and lending industrial. countries which compete with each other May all be placed on a level footing, and then any tempor- ary increase there might be in cost of production. would be borne by the conimunity. I should like, however, to emphasise the fact that the evidence so far available justifies one in main- taining strongly the opinion that in the long run the purchasing community will be no loser by this reform, but will stand to gain by it, along with the individual worker. On the Continent the chief objection of em- ployers of labour is their apprehension of diffi- culty in .finding men to work the extra shift. • That objection can be perfectly well met by ,giving several years of notice to employers of labour—such a pe ri od, in fact, as would he amply sufficient o enable them to prepare for the change.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>England Must Lead.</head>
        <p>England Must Lead.</p>
        <p>In England the movement makes, and, wilt . or without international co-operation, will cony tinne to make, steady progress, so that lon before another generation has passed the twelv .'. hour shift will have been abolished in ever , iron and steel works in the country: But it i admitted that this result will be the mor speedily attained if we co-operate with ou neighbours across the ocean and across th.. Channel. . It is true that in matters of inch trial and Social reform there is an unhapp spirit of re-action prevalent just now in man of the countries of Europe, nor is the cause . it far to seek. Excessive rivalry in armamen !extended periods at compulsory military servi these . things are, as they have ever bee , inimical to progress. On the other. hand. 0.. have palpable and striking evidence of the deeP. dissatisfaction this causes in the minds of lat•ge sections, of the communities of our great neigh- bours 'across the water. Is there any reason. then, to doubt the response that would be given in many an influential quarter if England gives a lead in this matter? It is for England to d° • It, because it is England, and England alone. which .has, already proved on a large scale the manifold advantages—commercial and. indus- trial, moral and social advantages--;which are the certain result of this wise and just reform. Therefore the members of the British section of. the International Association for Labour Legis- lation. approached . the Home Secretary rather more than a year .since and asked him to signiti: his willingness to send delegates to Swi to consider..</p>
        <p>„ .</p>
        <p>(a) the desirability; and (b) the feasibility of this reform in the iron and steel trade when- ever Continental powers were prepared to do the same. Need I add that this request wag modest in the extreme? It was, in fact, the irreducible minimum, unless we are weakly to fold our hands and do nothing. I am sorry to say, however, that although we have communi- cated time and again with the Home secretary on this all-important question, we have so far met with at the Borne Office only the barren• obdurate, unreasoning, sterilised and petrified opposition of a stone wall. No valid excuse for. or intelligible explanation of this persistent inaction has been given us, nor has any singh" objection to the course we recommend beeo pointed out. The exigencies of official life n° doubt have to Le borne in mind. Cabinet Ministers have many demands on their time. and it may be, and I would fain hope it is the fact, that Mr. McKenna has not yet grasped. either from the national or the international point of view, the full significance and impor- tance of this reform. But now the time has come when we bold that we are entitled to request his serious attention and practical assistance in the prosecution of our work, and We feel also justified in appealing to public opinion in Elie confident hope that we shall receive a strong and whole-hearted backing for the demand we have made, and which we non. emphatically repeat in the universal interest of industrial democracy. I have laid stress, in the course of these , articles, on the immense national advantages to be reaped by allowing enlightened, rational and scientific principles to govern the whole question • of hours of work and conditions of employment' Before I conclude, perhaps I may be allowed in add a word on the deeply significant and encour- aging international aspect of the question.</p>
        <p>A. 11. CROSFIELP,</p>
        <p>*Statistics hare made it quite clear that—as one would expect—where long hours of work are in force there is most risk of accidents towards the close of the day, or of the shift, when fatigue begin'' to tell most on the workers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>DIU W FOR THE WORKSHOPS'</p>
