      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <p>Freedom Fellowship. Nun Nicholas, Cardiff, will address the Free- dom Fellowship in the Rooms, Market Gate, on Friday, upon IThe futility of political action."</p>
        <p>Knutsford Division Liberals. Mr. P. M. Oliver and Alderman J. Middleton, Bolton, will address a Liberal meeting in the School, Higher Whitley, on Thursday, at 7.30 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Preachers for To-morrow..</head>
        <p>Preachers for To-morrow..</p>
        <p>Hospital Sunday ;ill be celebrated in most Warrington churches to-morrow, and the preachers will be found in the advertisement on Page 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>"South African War."</head>
        <p>"South African War."</p>
        <p>_ The members of the St. Paul's C.L.B. listened with interest on Thursday night to a lecture , upon the South African War, given by Lieu- tenant Bottomley, who was recently transferred to the company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>Butchers' Football Match Proceeds.</head>
        <p>Butchers' Football Match Proceeds.</p>
        <p>The Honorary Secretary of the Working Men's Infirmary Committee gratefully acknow- ledges the receipt from Mr. Ed. Stirrup, Market- street, of £3O Sc. 3d., being the proceeds of the football match, Thursday, 12th February, Warrington Butchers versus St. Helena Butchers.</p>
        <p>Industrial Insurance Agents. The annual social of ale National Association of Industrial Assurance Agents was held in the King's Cafe on Thursday evening. A company of 65 took tea, and an excellent concert fol- lowed. All the artistes provided first-class turns, and amongst those who contributed were Miss Carrie Hastings, Messrs. S. T. Brockle- hurs-t, Moon, W. J. Hopkins, Walker, G. Jack, Whitehouse, the Excelsior Glee Party, and Messrs. P. W. Smith and Trow were able accom- panists.</p>
        <p>Pitilomathie Society. At a meeting held at the Old Parlour, Cairo., street Schools, on Wednesday evening, Mr. R, M. Monk opened a discussion on the question "How could the present political machinery bu improved?" He submitted that there was something wrong with our present political mechanism. While condemning the hereditary principle of the House of Lords, he gave in- stances also to show that, even in the Hous€ of Commons, the government of the country was largely a family affair. Proportional repre- sentation would be the death of the party sys- tem. Mr. Monk's remarks were keenly criti- cised, Messrs. Walsh, W. B. Forshaw, H. Dutton, W. H. Tickell, A. H. Rathbone, D. j Dunn, H. Sherburn, and H. H. Taylor taking part in the ensuing discussion.</p>
        <p>F Russell's Div Mend. Theannual e rhe r , report of Messrs. Fletcher, Rue. l sell and Co., Ltd., shows, after the usual deduc- tions, a profit of £21,145 25., to which is added the balance brought forward from last year of £3,057 38. 4d., making a total of £24,202 .58. 4(1.,0ut of which the Preference dividend for the half-year ended 30th Tune, 1913, of .£1,500 lihs been paid, leaving a balance of .222,702 4d The directors recommend that a final dividend on the preference shares for the six month,- ending 31st December, 1913, be Paid, absorbing .41,500. They recommend that a dividend of 5 per cent. be paid on the ordinary shares foi the year ending 31st December, 1913, absorbin.. £5,000. They also recominend the payment of a bonus of 5 per cent. for the year on the ordinary shares, absorbing £5,000. They fur- ther recommend the transfer to the reserve fund of the sum of £6,919 16s. 6c1., as per the articles of association, and to carry forward .C 4,282 B.s. 10d. to next year's account.</p>
        <p>payment of Committeemen. At a meeting of the Finance Sub-committee of the Insurance Committee the following particu- lars were given by the Clerk in connection with the proposal to pay members: Aggregate amount available to the Commissioners for pay- ment to members of Insurance Committees foi subsistence allowance and compensation for I,.ss of remunerative time, £.30,000 per annum. If divided equally between 236 Insurance Com- mittees this would equal £127 2s. 4d. p,,r com- mittee. Taking the average attendances for the past three months as a basis, the amount re- quired to pay the members of the Warrington Insurance Committee, at the rate decided upon at the last meeting, viz., 7s. per attendance at all meetings of the committee and sub-commit- tees, would be £252. Wigan, Birmingham and Blackburn Insurance Committees proposed to pay ss. per attendance at all meetings. Man- chester and Kent proposed 6s. per meeting; St. Helens proposed ss. for the General Com- mittee and 2s. for the sub-committee meetings; Rochdale proposed ss. for meetings before six ' o'clock and 2s. 6d. for meetings after six o'clock. Nthe C Commis- sioners mvirmai rs rsii ono ng te replyersnwith Insuranceh a Al regard been received Cteointhm eitt ee f proposalrotom ptaohlfe,7e per meeting. The matter will be discussed at the meeting on Wednesday. 1.8.5. A. These initials stand for the International Bible Students' Association, a society incorpor• ated for the promotion of Christian knowledge. Its headquarters in this country are at London, but branches exist all over the world. Entirely unsectarian in character, its object is to assist Christians generally, and others, regardless of denominational distinctions, to a better under- standing of the divine plan concerning man's past, present, and eternal future, as revealed in the Bible. The work of the society is truly enormous. Last year tracts, printed in many languages, to the extent of between 40 and 50 millions, were circulated free. The lectures. which are given the world over by speakers of the association, are also free of charge, and no collections are taken. The work is supported Apebyoseprlveioelwsuhonfotamiepretiencogiasnti theeilbauvt Leions from Christian bße.nS.Aa r. raanndged it sf er work.en Spuanladee, ay eveningssankystear o at cinnamtheencPinag s Park undaPicturey, March 15th. The subjects will be "The end of the world—and after," "The divine plan of the ages," and "Beyondthegratvrie,"seartesspLce- tively. All are heartily welcome; free, and there is no collection.</p>
        <p>St. David's Day at Warrington. The annual social and concert arranged by the Welsh Literary Society in connection with St. David's Day celebrations were given in the Schoolroom adjoining the Welsh Chapel, Cros- field-street. Mr. D. J. Price (Lovely-lane) occu- pied the chair. There was an excellent attend- nce, which, if it did not constitute a ,record, was patedF much II a wibenyg theon teaa the astptleanaddaindc entertain- ment t earntatiicai: ment was provided, including songs, duets, Pianofortess E T aynsloalr, 0,. Miss Mr. pianoforte solo, speeches, and competitions. The programmet w; ass() songs s follows:M i Percy s Smith; Taylor (Arpley-street). Miss Caryrie Mr. E. Walker, Mr. Willie Hughes, Mr. William Davies; duets by the Misses Taylor and Miss Carrie Hastings and Mr. Willie Hughes, and Miss Carrie Hastings and Mr. E. Walker; speeches—" St. David, Patron Saint of Wales,- ' the Chairman; "Our language, our nation, our country," Mr. H. 0. Hughes (Liverpool); "St. David's Day," Mr. William Jones; toasts—" Our King, Prince of Wales, and all the Royal Family," Mr. Robert Thomas; " Crosfield-street Welsh Literary Society," proposed by Mr. E. E. Hughes, Ms. J. H. Jones responding. Competi- tions: Solo, "All through the night" (Welsh air), Miss Thomas (Buttermarket-street); two verses to " The Welshman from Home," Ist prize, Mr. Evan E. Hughes; 'Welsh reading, unpunctuated," Ist prize, Mr. Willie Hughes. The adjudicators were the Rev. R. Parry Jones (pastor) and Mr. H. 0. Hughes (Liverpool). The accompanis •ts were Mr. Percy Smith, Mrs. Draper, Miss Bibby, and Miss Blodwen Parry Jones. A vote of thanks was proposed at the close by the Rev. R. Parry Jones (the society's president), seconded by Mr. William Jones, the proceedings terminating with the singing of "Hen Wlad fy Nhadau" ("Land of my Fathers").</p>
        <p>Tanor CARS are world renowned. 4-seater complete, with dynamo electric lighting outfit, £4lo.—Sole agent for Warrington and district, J. S. G ARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warring- ton.</p>
        <p>Territorials Camp. The Warrington Territorials are to hold a week-end camp at Altear during the Easter holidays, starting on 9th April. Will of Late Councillor Birties.</p>
        <p>The will of the late Councillor Thomas Birtles, who died at his residence at Fearn- head, over two months ago, has been proved at £5,254, of which £4,871 is net personalty.</p>
        <p>Working Men's Infirmary Committee. The annual meeting will be held in the Town Hall this afternoon at three o'clock. The annual meeting of the Governors of the Infirm- ary takes place at the Infirmary on Friday, 27th March. • Warrington Chamber of Commerce. At the annual meeting of the Associated Chambers of Commerce, held in London this week, the Warrington Chamber of Commerce was represented by Captain if. 11. Crosfield (the President) and Alderman Bennett (the secre- tary).</p>
      </div>
