      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 14, 1914.</p>
        <p>Serious Anthrax Case.</p>
        <p>Suffering from anthrax, William Richardson (30), of 113, Wellington-street, and employed by the Bishop's Wharf Carrying Co., lies in a serious condition in the Infirmary. Other patients admitted during the week were: James Dunville (74), 64, Mersey-street, fractured arm; Annie Hough (60), 40, Algernon-street, fractured leg; William Powell (16), 12, Samuel-street, scalds on the hands. The last three patients are doing well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Deported Labour Leaders.</head>
        <p>Deported Labour Leaders.</p>
        <p>- ---- Warrington trade unionists and others will, on Wednesday evening, have the opportunity of hearing the real story which led to the deportation of the South African Labour leaders. Mr. R. B. Waterson, general secretary of the South African Labour party, and Mr. D. licKerrell, Transvaal Miners' Association, two of the deported, will address a mass meeting in the Parr Hall. Mr. H. Wardman will' take the chair at 7.30 p.m., and the other speaker will be Mr. Charles Dukes.</p>
        <p>Warrington Rechabites. The quarterly meeting of "Charity" Tent. No. 11, was held in the Friars' Green School. room, Cairo-street, on Monday. The meeting was presided over by Bro. Edwin Weir, Chief Ruler, there being a fair attendance of members, and also several visitors and district officers! present. After the ordinary busim2ss of the ' tent had been brought to a close there were two items of an interesting and unusual character. Bro. Cou.ncillor James Shaw, District Trustee, on behalf of the members of "Charily" Tent, made a presentation of a valuable fountain pen and black ebony walking-stick to Bro. T Massey, the late treasurer of the Tent.' the walking-stick, with silver mount, containing the following inscription: "Presented to Bro. T. Massey by the members of ' Charity ' Tent, treasurer r ci ati on f eorf the ser vi pastce services 2Nrleorly.deelallied:'l;tooßjlte T riol9. Ta.se.Snahtnawaas preferred to the sacrifice made by Bro. Massey, both on behalf of the Tent and district, for the furtherance of the principles of the Reche.bite Order. He was glad the Tent had recognised the services in the true spirit of brotherhood, which was essential ! to the prosperity of the Order. Bros. B. Walker, C. S. Fellows, D. Hamblett and William Wright (district officers) supported. Bro. T. Massey, in responding, said that in accepting the pre- sent he was touched and impressed with the brotherly spirit that lay behind the presenta- tion, and in retiring from the position of Tent Treasurer he had done so, not to get out of: i the work, but n order that he might devote more of his time to district affairs. lie should do all lie could to assist in the continuity of the success of the Tent, and thanked the members for the appreciation of the services he had ren- dered. Bro. Edwin Weir, Chief Ruler, then presented to Bro. Percy Cook a P.C.R. framed emblem of the Order as a recognition of having passed through the various offices of the Tent. Bro. Cook suitably responded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>STRIKERS' MANIFESTO.</head>
        <p>STRIKERS' MANIFESTO.</p>
        <p>Fletcher, Russell's Men Set Forth Their Case.</p>
        <p>WERE THE UNIONS ONOE RECOGNISED ?</p>
        <p>The strike at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell's works at Wilderspool continues, and the men are showing the same spirit of unanimity and determination which marked the earlier stage of the dispute. Works gate meetings have been held in the dinner hour at various works in Warrington during the past week, and these have elicited a tlarge amsympathy for the strikers, and hs has taken ofnthe very practical form of hand.. some donations to the strike fund. Trade strikers. strongly on the side of the union support is stron</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>MEN'S MANIFESTO.</head>
        <p>THE MEN'S MANIFESTO.</p>
        <p>The Strike Committee have issued the folloW- ing manifesto : " Owing to statements being circulated which are apt to convey a wrong impression regarding our case, we desire to make, through' the columns of "The Examiner," an official state: ment regarding same. "About two years ago, the.men at Fletcher, Russell and Co. were on the eve of a strike, our contention being that some of our comrades were being victimised. On this occasion the men were re-instated by the firm, who further agreed, in order to avoid trouble in the future, to meet the representatives of the unions affected. This course has been faithfully ad- hered to until just before Christmas. At that time the secretaries of the unions affected, con- sisting of the Stove and Grate Union, Gas Workers and General Labourers, Central Iron. moulders, Sheet Metal Workers and United Pat- ternmakers, were in negotiation with Messrs. Fletcher, Russell's and Messrs. Richmond and Co., with a view of having both works declared "Union shops." The negotiations failed, after two conferences, because the employers refused to consent to such an arrangement, and there- upon the unions let the matter drop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Arrangement Changed. 1</head>
        <p>Arrangement Changed. 1</p>
        <p>"Immediately following this Messrs. Fletcher Russell's posted a notice at the works, which in effect stated that while they did not object to a man joining a trade union, they refused ocfatsheetlmieen affected, to put pressure on any individual to be- come a trade unionist, and that if anY man had a complaint to make he should make it to the foreman of the particular shop in which he worked, and it would receive due attention. „since that notice was posted several egerosiaveriyr- ances materially affecting the wages of the men iheafli-eseciocctioi occurred, and deputationi in every firin have consistently but insist that the men make their complaints individually. This is in direct contradiction to for the what the firm have done two years. In view of this action, it is singular that at the conferences mentioned above the employers never once challenged the right of the men to bargain collectively. Their only concern was for the conscientious objector ' (sel- dom heard of, and never met). The nature of the work in this industry results in a constant changeto iiateoaf prices, and committeei t i sab l absolutely inside revision of these prices.</p>
        <p>the men men t hat time Conciliation Exhausted. f ore everytaking conceivablethe methodadopted, 0 f ::::: exhaustedße ciliation. On January' 15th a mass meeting was held in the Cozoporative Hall to discuss the firm's notice, and on that occasion the men's leaders taking , extremeundoubtedly action, becausera in ed thea t they were under the impression that the firm would again meet them in conference in an correspondence settlement. A. month elapsed,deavolir dtouraiargrivwhich Passed between the officials of the firm and the joint committee, but the men were refused repre- sentation. "Then came the culminating paint. There was a wholesale rejection of castings. which e seriously affected a great number of th men's wages. A ' shop ' meeting was held and a depu- tation of the men affected approached the man- agement, but were refused a hearing, although the men could have explained where the fault lay, to the benefit of the firm and of themselves. Two days later, when the patience of the men was quit fi exhausted, they held a mass meet- ing, and agreed to instruct the officials to write demanding that the deputation be met. The reply was, 'The firm could not brook interfer- ence with the management.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Brook No Interference .</head>
        <p>Brook No Interference .</p>
        <p>"It is obvious then, in their desire not to brook any interference of the men with the management, the firm are prepared to deny the men common justice—the right to state their case when it affects their pockets. "The firm want to he judge and jury in nu cases, and the workmen must submit, no matter what the verdict is. This we contend is an un- thinkable attitude to take up. We desire to state fully and frankly that we have been ever desirous of meeting the representatives of the firm.` ` n behalf of the Joint Committee,</p>
        <p>". CHARLES DUKES. "A. BcsNrrr. "H. SINCLAIR."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>MAYOR'S REGRETsisIDELIGHTs op THE ipisy PROBLE)YI</head>
        <p>The MAYOR'S REGRETsisIDELIGHTs op THE ipisy PROBLE)YI</p>
        <p>Councillor Peacock Comments Upon Warrington's Wealthy Men.</p>
        <p>NO GIFT OF ORGAN FOR PARR HILL.</p>
        <p>Are Warrington's wealthy townsmen un- patriotic? This was the question raised by the stinging reproach made by the Mayor at the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday. He had endeavoured to get the gift of an organ for the Parr Hall, and, having failed, expressed his opinion with candid fervour. With regard to the organ for the Parr Hall, said the MAYOR, I regret that the Local Govern- ment Board have declined to allow the Corpora- tion to borrow £1,600. The only alternative was to take it out of the rates. That idea was brought before the General Purposes Committee and defeated. In view of this difficulty, I per- sonally approached several of our wealthiest gentlemen in the town, and suggested that War- rington would welcome the presentation of an organ. I regret, although I had put the matter in the best possible manner, they came forward with various excuses and got out of the position as well as they could. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>While "On the Earth."</head>
        <p>While "On the Earth."</p>
        <p>I suppose there is only one other alternative now, and that is a subscription list. Personally I shall not initiate a subscription list under any possible circumstances, although, if one is , started, I shall give my mite along with others— and perhaps more generously than some. But I 'am very sorry that Warrington is in such a dis- credited position. We have a lot of wealthy gentlemen, who have earned the bulk of their money in Warrington, but who cannot see their way to do what is done in other towns all over the country and make handsome presentations. Personally, I am very sorry. We cannot hope for very much when they die, but I had hoped that some of them might do something while they are on the earth. (Laughter.) Even now we may have an offer from some gentlemen, and I hope that for Warrington's sake, even at the last hour, someone may come forward and do what he ought to do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>OUT OF THE RATES.</head>
        <p>OUT OF THE RATES.</p>
        <p>Dr. JOSEPH (the .Deputy-Mayor) said he was the originator of the scheme for a municipal organ, and he deeply regretted that it was doomed to failure. He had ventured to move. a resolution in committee that the money should come out of the rates of the current year—not that he had any less regard for the ratepayers than other members of the Council had, but simply to be consistent to the end. He had no hope of carrying that proposal, in fact only two members supported him. If the rates had not been so high he should have persisted further in his resolution, and he had no doubt he would have been successful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>:MUSICAL SOCIETY SUGGESTION.</head>
        <p>:MUSICAL SOCIETY SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>- Who Will head the List?</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>- --•—• - Sir,—lt was with great regret that I read that Part of last Tuesday's Council meeting report relating to the Parr Hall organ. Seeing that the urgent need of a first-class organ was fully recognised by a large majority of the Town Council, and that an advantageous tender had been actually accepted, it appears to me most unreasonable and unsatisfactory that the pro- vision of such an organ should be vetoed on some quibble or other by the officials of the Local Government Board, who can know noth- ing of the wants of the town. I quite recognise that everything possible was done by the ex-Mayor (Dr. Joseph), the Town Clerk, and the Parr Hall Committee, who had the matter in hand, and it is most disappoint- ing that their efforts should be rendered of no avail in such a way. I I suppose, however, there is no appeal from ' the decision of the Local Government Board, and some other means must be found to pro- vide the money required, for the provision of the organ still remains a crying necessity, and if there is no other way of obtaining it, there is every bit as much justification in charging the cost of the organ on the rates as in provid- ing bowling greens and other recreations and institutions for the benefit of the public. I note with great satisfaction the Mayor (Councillor Peacock) is determined not to let the matter drop, and I need hardly say that I shall be ready to support his efforts in any way within my power and means. Surely there should be no difficulty in rais- ing the £1,600 or so required, but when one looks at other towns one cannot help thinking that Warrington has benefited by very few private benefactions of any magnitude. Lancaster has public baths, the large Storey Institute, a Children's Hospital, the splendid Williamson Park and buildings, and the fine Town Hall, containing a fine large organ, all of which were, I understand, given to the town. Rochdale has recently had p, magnificent organ, costing about -0,000, presented to the town by Alderman Turner, who also, I believe, gave a public park and buildings. St. Helens also, I am told, has had several parks and buildings presented. Many other instances could be cited if space permitted. Now there must be in or connected with War- rington many to whom the gift of £lOO (or to some even the whole amount required) would be a very small matter, and if only ten or fifteen would generously come forward and head the list, there should be no difficulty in obtaining a substantial sum by public subscription. If this were done I would gladly and willingly do my best to obtain a fair share from the mem- bers and subscribers of the Musical Society. I remain, yours truly, JOHN P. REYNOLDS, Hon. Treasurer of the Warrington Musical Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>WAKES WEEK SCHEME.</head>
        <p>WAKES WEEK SCHEME.</p>
        <p>The Mayor ' Moving in the Matter.</p>
        <p>TOWN'S MEETING MAY BR CALLED.</p>
        <p>Developments have taken place in connection! with the proposal to establish a "Wakes Week" in Warrington on lines similar to those which are so popular in other Lancashire towns. The scheme, as was exclusively announced in "The Examiner" Jast week, originated at the meeting of the Trades and Labour Council, and the Secretary, Mr. Charles Duke, was asked to communicate with the Mayor, so that the pro- posal might be laid before all the parties con- cerned.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes accordingly communicated with the Mayor during the present week, and evi- dently the Mayor has been interested in the scheme proposed.</p>
        <p>1 THE MAYOR'S REPLY. The Mayor, after considering the scheme, wrote this week to Mr. Dukes as follows:</p>
        <p>"In reply to your letter of the 6th inst., with regard to the proposed Wakes Week, the Mayor is communicating with the different emploYers of labour, and he will, if he deems it advisable, call a town's meeting."</p>
        <p>Since the subject was published in "The Ex- aminer," it has aroused a large amount of in- terest in all circles in the town. The workers , would welcome a good holiday, in preference to ' the present patchwork system, and the em- ployers, The Examiner" representative was informed, may also approve of the scheme, as the present system of holidays interferes con- siderably with their works. "The Wakes system works admirably in other Lancashire towns," said one interested in the question, "and there is no reason why it should not prove so in Warrington."</p>
        <p>:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>LAWLESS "ORANGE BANDITTI."</head>
        <p>THE LAWLESS "ORANGE BANDITTI."</p>
        <p>BY C. E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>The first Orange Lodge was formed in the obscure village of Lougbgall on September 21st, 1795. The immediate cause which gave birth to Orangeism was in connection with the planting of colonies of Protestants on the farms, or tene- ments of Catholics, who were forcibly ejected simply because they were Catholics. Orange- men were first called the Break-of-Day party; this title was afterwards changed into Orange- ism. In a Parliamentary Report (1835), from which most of these facts are taken, 8e i:ave the evidence of a Mr. Christie, a member of the Society of Friends, who tells us "that he heard of twelve or fourteen Catholic houses wrecked in a night, that this commenced in the neigh- bourhood of Churchill." Numbers of Catholics dispossessed were seen wandering about the country hungry and half naked. This Churchill was the residence of Thomas Verner, Esq., who, in 1798, became Orange Grand Master. This family seat near the foot of Lough Neagh is midway between Dungannon and Portadown, in the County of Armagh.. It is bordered by the River Blackwater, which forms the boundary between Tyrone and Armagh. Lord Gosford,. then Governor of Armagh, submitted a plan , for the consideration of the leading magistrates. of the county for the checking of the enormities which disgraced the country in connection with this Orange organisation. He says: "It is no secret that a persecution accompanied with all the circumstances of ferocious cruelty, which . have all ages distinguished that dreadful i calamity, s now raging in this country. Neither age, nor even acknowledged innocence as to the late disturbances, is sufficient to excite mercy, much less afford protection. The only crime which the wretched objects of this Merciless persecution are charged with is a crime of easy proof; it is simply a profession of the Roman Catholic faith. A lawless banditti have con- stituted themselves judges of this species of delinquency, ond the sentence they pronounce is equally conc:,f,., and terrible; it is nothing less than a confiscation of all property, and immediate banishment." He speaks "of more than half the inhabitants of a populous country deprived at one blow of the means as well as of the fruits of their industry, and driven in the midst of an inclement winter to seek shelter for themselves and their helpless families where chance may guide them."</p>
        <p>" Ascendency." This was the condition of the country in the early days of this "lawless banditti" called Orangeism. Part of the oath taken was:—</p>
        <p>"I, A. 8., do solemnly swear, &amp;c., that I will do to the utmost in my power to sup- port and defend the King and his heirs so: long as he and they support the Protestant ascendency."</p>
        <p>The Orange movement then was an organisa- tion for exterminating the Catholic faith, and its loyalty to the King was then, as now, pro- visional, i.e., only as long as he supported the "Protestant ascendency." As years went on this movement became more respectable; the aristocracy and nobility joined it. Its oath as modified; it became a kind of Brotherhood. The qualifications and characteristics of an Orangeman were supposed to be " faith, piety, courtesy, and compassion." He was to "love rational society and hate swearing." But what- ever its rise in respectability, its refinement in speech and manners, it has always retained its hatred, and, as opportunity permitted, exer- cised its intolerant attitude to the Catholic faith. I have no doubt that the Orange lodges like other friendly societies, served useful pur- poses in creating a feeling of brotherhood and mutual help amongst the members. By 1835 the numbers had increased to two hundred thousand and included some of the nearest tp the throne, as well as the poorest peasants. The purposes of the organisation. as set forth by the Grand Master at the Grand Orange Lodge of the County of Tyrone, 27th April, 1632, was embodied in resolutions of which the following are samples:—</p>
        <p>"That the support we speak of means to encourage Protestant tenants on the one hand, and to defend Protestant landlords on the other; to preserve a Proteitant popula- tion and to keep at its head an aristocracy truly Protestant." " That we as Protestants reprobate the new system of National Education, and that we will not listen to any pastor whom we see to encourage it, or whom we know to approve it" Sample of Orange "Ascendency."</p>
        <p>I do not know why they condemned educa- tion, except that it was national, and therefore meant to benefit Catholics was well as Protes- tants. Near Churchill, the residence of the Verners, is a Catholic village called Maghery. Once an Orange procession with drums and banners passed through this village, and one of the usual party fights between the villagers and the procession arose, in which both human heads and drum heads were damaged. This was an illegal procession, such having been prohibited by law. The Orange party, who resided across the Blackwater, in County Tyrone, afterwards determined that they would be revenged; they collected their forces and crossed the Black- water by the iron bridge near Churchill, and marched on Maghery, with muskets, bayonets, swords, &amp;c.; Colonel Verner, as magistrate, made a feeble slick- of keeping the peace. The inhabitants of Maghery, hearing of the coming raid, left their houses in fear, and took refuge in the adjoining bogs. Disappointed in finding nobody to fight, the attacking Orange party proceeded to wreck the houses; 28 of these were practically destroyed, the furniture broken, the few inhabitants who did not escape were beaten and abused. The Lord Lieutenant sent Justice Perrins to investigate the affair. A chief point of the investigation centred upon the conduct of Colonel Verner, the Orange Grand Master, himself a magistrate. Justice Perrins reported that "Colonel Verner appears not to have performed his duty as a magistrate, and that he is liable to be prosecuted at suit of the Crown by information of such (as it seems to me) criminal neglect of his duty." The curious thing about this is that Colonel Verner instead of being prosecuted was selected as one of the two magistrates to whom the "informa- tions" were specially forwarded by the Crown. Still more curious, the outcome of the proceed- ings was that all the raiders were acquitted, while for the offence of the broken drums the Maghery Catholics were tried, convicted and sentenced to three months' imprisonment. Colonel Verner was, of course, exonerated. A few years after this Colonel Verner was dis- missed from his position of magistrate for his violent Orange partisanship. And now it may be asked why I recount the details of an incident which happened in an Incidents]now yobeascrusreagpoar. tlofrelprlyelabneed aeuigsehttyhiosr ()inns:ll:nen:etch:l:d: typical of the conduct of the Orange party, 1-wherever it can establish its ascendency and typical of the powerlessness, or the disincline- teiNo.enn intimatelyo f justice. t he th e ProtestantlAoenai tysrecu inloinnwdgh. cboiew: eatriuisest:et o happened. It has been my lot for many years to be connected with the development of a peat bog. about 200 acres in extent, forming the tongue of land which separates the Verner estate from this village of Maghery. I might withe:hr than an rt isns:ednetpsu everytan against a)i hoi nna ss canet of hfl opt ionol ui see- here add that I have been associated in business with Ireland and Irishmen for over thirty , years, have frequently visited the country, made many friends, both Home Rulers and Anti-Home: ghl ctlhaeim. country, nktnryow, and —ealt sdtaeanldnifoor, re ha:ipmeert Irish question. I also know several of the men idqscuuaelsssltediotnh interviewed by the Warrington Deputation, and have gathered together in their scam ' Rulers, Ihrie:dr hthaed Rule.stßhi urel3i!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <p>be of interest The Churchill tenants sur- rounding this peat bog are, as I have said, mostly Protestants and Orangemen, and from these have been drawn the labour to dig the bog. Each man takes a section and employs h's family or friends to work with him. A man applied for work and was allotted a section. No sooner had he got to work than we had a depu- tation from the other head workers, with an ultimatum that if we did not dismiss this man they would throw up their jobs. "What is the matter?" was our inquiry. They replied: "This man is a Catholic and we are Protestants, and we are not going to work with him." Our reply, of course, was: "We recognised no dif- ference of religion in our works; the sole con- dition was that they did their work properly, and if they did not care to work for us they might go elsewhere." The opposition, of course, subsided, and now they work harmoniously together. The Origin of "Intolerance."</p>
        <p>, - Now to come to what ;nay be looked upon as the natUral result of the Maghery incident described. I have heard within the last year or two the recent conduct of the men of Mag- hery given me as evidence of Catholic intoler- ance. The facts stated are these. The vill Age of Maghery lies between the Churchill estate, and the Protestant Church, which many .of the tenants attend. There are two roads from Churchill to this church, one through Maghery, somewhat the shorter, the other more cir- cuitous. The Catholics at Maghery have since the raid described maintained a claim, uncon- stitutional, of course, that no Protestant pro- cession, even if it be a funeral, should come through their Catholic village, but must take the circuitous route. This curious claim, up to a few years back, has been acknowledged by the Protestants. Some years ago, in the case of a funeral, it was decided to put this claim at defiance. The procession was met at the entrance of the village by the men, women and children of Maghery, who, with the proverbial blackthorns, disputed the right of passage. I believe a few blows were struck and the funeral cortege turned back and made to go the longer way round. The, putting forward of such a vil- lage right would be unthinkable in this country. It is Irish all through. It is really a remnant of communal right, the idea that the village and its road belonged to the villagers. But my point is that this is now brought about RP an instance of Catholic intolerance. Ido not deny the intolerance, but seek to show that its origin and continuance was the direct and natural result of the Orange raid, and of the favouritism shown to this party by the ruling party of the time. There are probably those living to-day in Maghery whose parents' houses were un- roofed. Like incidents I have heard of in the last few months (and they may be perfectly true), but my own investigations, extending over many years, and my reading of history, has led me to the conclusion that the Orange party has, in almost every case, been the aggressor, and that the Irish Catholics are a peaceable, peace-loving folk, who, if they were treated justly, would not be aggressors, or oppress their Protestant fellow countrymen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>ANOTHER DEPUTATION.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER DEPUTATION.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner." • Sir,—By the time this appears in print an- other deputation will be preparing to leave for Ireland, to judge for themselves what effect the ' present Home Rule Bill (if passed) is likely. to have on the country. I have been waiting to hear from Mr. Griffin, who recently suggested subscribing; &amp;c., towards a further deputation, and towards which I offered to assist. For reasons known only to himself, he has apparently decided to drop the idea, seeing he has wit accepted my offer. It has just occurred to me that probably the writer of the article in The Examiner" call- ing himself "Ulster Protestant" would not mind arranging to meet us in Belfast, so that we can discuss the matter directly, and also know to whom we have the pleasure of con- versing.</p>
        <p>I should also greatly appreciate the address of the "Friend and Protestant" mentioned in Mr. O'Brien's letter, who makes such sweeping statements, so that we may arrange for an interview if this is at all possible. If this can be arranged, and the particulars sent to Mr. Hayes at the Unionist Central Office, Sankey-street, Warrington, he will see w. get them at once, so that we may have un- restricted opportunity of getting to the bottom of this important question.</p>
        <p>I am writing this on the eve of our departure, and with no idea as to the conclusions the party will come to. But I am anxious that no effort shall be &amp;Pared to help us to make a report that cannot be challenged as being an honest effort to get at the Truth.—Yours faithfully, W. 11. BELLIAN. The Spinney, Grappenhall, March 12th, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>CROSFIELD'S OPERA.</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S OPERA.</p>
        <p>A Good Balance Towards Buying Steriliser for Infirmary.</p>
        <p>One incident connected with the indur named, and bearing oft or present theme, may</p>
        <p>intry• I</p>
        <p>SELF-SUPPORTING NOW.</p>
        <p>We have received the following letter from Captain George Crosfield, president of the Crosfield Operatic Society :</p>
        <p>It may interest your readers to learn that the Crosfield Operatic Society has cleared .1.'31 10s. 10(1. as a result of the performances of "The Old Guard."</p>
        <p>The society has decided to hand £3O (arrying forward the balance) to the Infirmary, with the suggestion that the sum should go towards the buying of a steriliser (cost, about £5O), the balance to be paid off next year. Last year and this year are the only two years in which the Orosfield Operatic Society has run on its own financial basis. In former years the cost of scenery, orchestra, dresses, &amp;c., was defrayed privately, thus enabling a good balance to be shown although the society was not sup- ported by the public in the way that it has been latterly.</p>
        <p>Considering the way in which the public sup- Ported the performances this year, it may seem surprising to your readers that we have not a bigger balance to hand over, but only those who have had to run an operatic performance for one week can thoroughly appreciate how heavily im i)ro in improve these upon mtahtete firns,anancidal we es the expenses bear upon the receipts. Every year, however, the society gains fresh experi- encel should like to take this opportunity, on behalf of our society, of thanking the Warring- ton public for their kind support. noefatthyise to this. r hope</p>
        <p>ACKNOWLEDGMENT. The Honorary Treasurer (Mr. Roger C. Parr) , gratefully acknowledges the receipt of cheque value £3O, from Mr. B. S. Townroe, being the proceeds of the recent performances of The Old Guard," by the Crosfield Operatic Society. The society desire this sum to be devoted to buying a steriliser, and hope to be able to con- tribute a further sum to the expense next year. _ •</p>
        <p>PREMIER MOTOR CYCLER. Light-weight model, 1:26.—501e agent, J. S. GARIAND, 51 to 55. Bridge. street, Warrington.</p>
