      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Lenten Lectures.</head>
        <p>Lenten Lectures.</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at the Lenten services at the Parish Church yesterday, both in the afternoon and evening. At the former service the Rev. J. C. Coop, vicar of St. Cather- ine's, Liverpool, gave a discourse to the women, and at the later service the Rev. J. G. McCor- mick, of London, was the preacher. The preacher at both services on Friday will be the Rev. B. Heywood, vicar of Swinton.</p>
        <p>The Tanning Trade. Trade in the home centres is improving, says "The Leather World" about Warrington, and orders have come along more numerously; this was especially the case with bends, the trade in which during the week was much brisker than usual. At Manchester Fair tanvers had matters very much in their own hands, for buyers were flitting from one to another in the endeavour to place orders; that their success was very limited was due to the opinion held by many tanners that a sharp rise is imminent in leather values. As it was, some record prices were made foi rough leather, and parcels of rough bellies were booked at Id. up on previous record figures.</p>
        <p>Fire Chief Resigns. For over half a century the "roll book" of the Warrington Fire Brigade has contained the name of Mr. Frank Turner, first as fireman, and then successively as sergeant "deputy" and superintendent, but he will not be present offi- cially at any further fires, for at the meeting of the Fire Engine Committee on Monday hie resignation was accepted with regret, and they passed a resolution placing on record his valu- able services. During his connection with the brigade he had been present at Warrington'e most memorable fires, and on several occasions had narrow escapes. He was appointed a ser- geant in 1873, and six years later he was made "deputy" to the late Superintendent Matthew Chorley ; whilst in 1882 he was chosen by the committee to fill the post of Warrington's Fire Chief. In the year following his appointment as sergeant Ile had his narrowest escape. It was at a fire at the Liverpool Sire and Glue Works, when, following the collapse of the walls, he was removed apparently lifeless from undei a burning beam. He was unconscious for two hours, but he received no great injury. In the world of sport he was most prominent as a bowler, and at competition open to all Eng- lavd he won the championship silver cup.</p>
        <p>Temperance Women. Up to the present time there has been no sePnrate branch of the British Women's Tem- perance Association in Latchford, and those who have been anxious to forward this great movement among the women of England have gone to the Warrington meetings. During the last few years Latchford has much increased, and it has been thought that the time has comel for a branch in this part of the town. A meet- ing was held on Tuesday afternoon in the Wes- leyan Guild Room, under the presidency of Mrs. E. Lloyd Jones. Miss Cherry, of the Cheshire County Union, spoke on the beginning of the! movement, and its inauguration by Frances Willard in America, and Margaret Parker at Newcastle, in England, with the result that 32 members were enrolled and a branch formed. The movement in Latchford is to be uns.&gt;-...tar- ian, and this first meeting included representa- tives from most of the different Churches, in- cluding Church of England, Baptist, Wesleyan and Independent Methodist. The meetings are to be held monthly, and speakers will addrese them on subjects of interest to women generally, and for the furtherance of their welfare. The next meeting will be held on March 31st in the Independent Methodist Chapel.</p>
        <p>Mission at "Our Lady's." The Mission which is being held at Our Lady's Church, Latchford, by the Jesuit Fathers, is being well attended, the instructions on the Commandments and Sacraments by the Rev. E. Buckland, of Romney, and the Mission medita- tions and sermons by the Rev. H. Day, attract- ing large • and appreciative congregations. Preaching on Monday evening on the subject of " Eternal punishment," Father Day said that if the existence of Hell were denied, then he not see how it was possible to explain the words of Holy Writ, for in more than one place in the 1 Bible distinct references were made to the ever- lasting flames of Hell. In very beautiful word pictures the preacher depicted on the one hand the sufferings of the God Man and Saviour, who had done so much to prevent any soul from ever being condemned to everlasting punishment, and the awful state of those who, by following the example of the rebel angels in their cry of "Non servam " (" I will not serve"), find themselves for ever in the company of those same fallen spirits, longing for a death which will never come, to free them from their torments. While dwelling on this truth of the Catholic religion, Father Day made very fine contrasts between the joys of the elect—a happiness within the reach of all—and the misery of the lost souls, whose greatest suffering must be the knowledge of their own folly in practically refusing to take the means- of beilig saved, while yet the preciousisto be lfot sedo f ttiom. me owr-raosw t heirs evening, he hwe s sen tin ohe Apostolic Blessing of the Holy Father will be given and baptismal vows renewed.</p>
        <p>Shakespearean Players. The Warrington Amateur Shakespearean players aim high. It is rather unusual for amateurs to attempt comedies of the "Bard of Avon." However, there is every prospect of the productions "Much Ado About Nothing" and "Merry Wives of Windsor" being a big suc- cess, and in order to bring the performances within the reach of all it has been decided to adhere to the ordinary prices of the Royal Court Theatre for the whole of the week com- mencing April 20th. On the first two nights scholars will be admitted at half-price, thus bringing the price of witnessing Shakespearean plays to the level of the picture palaces. The performances will be given on alternate nights, "Much Ado About Nothing" being staged on the Monday. There has been some difficulty in obtaining a gentleman of physical propor- tions to undertake the role of Falstaff, but this has been overcome by the acceptance of Mr., Ryder Boys, of the New Theatre, Manchester, The following is the complete cast:—"Much Ado About Nothing"—Don Pedro, Mr. W. H. Brown; Don John, Mr. D. Travis; Claudio, Mr. J. Lowe; Benedick, Dr. Law; Leonate, Mr. C. Daintith; Antonio, Mr. A. C. Hodgson; Balthazar, Mr. J. H. Draper; Borachio, Mr. W. Ashton; Dogberry, Mr. H. M. Ashton; Messen- ger, Mr. C. W. Vernon; Second Watch, Mr. W. H. Forster; Oatcake, Mr. R. Pemberton; Sea Coal, Mr. B. Madeley; Hero, Miss M. Truslove; Beatrice, Miss G. Adams; Margaret, Mrs. Ella Royston; Ursula, Miss Hutton; Comrade, Mr. D. Heyes; Verges, Mr. G. Starkey; First Watch, Mr. H. Beaman; Francis, Mr. P. Newbold. "Merry Wives of Windsor"—Shallow, Mr. Alf. Grounds; Ford, Mr. L. Langford; Dr. Cains, Mr. 11. M. Ashton; Host, Mr. C. Daintith; Pistol, Mr W. Ashton; Nym, Mr. A. C. Hodg- son; Robin, Mrs. Law; Simple, Mr. A. N. Ches- ter; Mrs. Ford, Miss Gladys Adams; Mrs. Page, Miss Brundreth; Mrs. Quickly, Miss B. For- shaw; Sir H. Evans, Mr. W. S. Edwards; Bar- dolph, Mr. B. Caldwell; Rugby, Mr. W. S. Fur- ness; Falstaff, Mr. Ryder Boys; Fenton, Mr. Lawton; Slender, Mr. R. Dickson; 'Page, Dr. Law; Ann Page, Miss M. Ellison.</p>
      </div>
