      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>PRUDENTIAL ASSURANCE CO.'S RECORD PROSPERITY.</head>
        <p>PRUDENTIAL ASSURANCE CO.'S RECORD PROSPERITY.</p>
        <p>The sixty-fifth annual report .of that gigantic undertaking, the Prudential Assurance Com- pany, is printed in to-day's issue of "The Examiner," and shows that another record year has been experienced. The assets of the com- pany amount to nearly .197,000,000, an increase f nearly x 2,500,000, and the details of the several departments approach the marvellous. Twelve months ago, in dealing with this company's affairs, attention was called to the energetic manner in which the directors had worked the Insurance Act, six approved societies having been formed, in which nearly three mil- lion members had been enrolled at the end of 1912. The report now issued states that these societies have done important work during the past year and the membership continues to in- crease, while during 1913 a sum of no less than ..t1,401,360 was distributed in connection with this matter on account of sickness and mater- nity benefits. The total income for the year amounted to £16,567,600, an increase of ..t549,700, the expansion being attributable to every source from which the company derives its income. £1,000,000 from Increased Values. At the annual meeting the Chairman, Mr. T. C. Dewey, stated that, as was the case in 1912, there has again been a noticeable increase in the amount of business ranging from .tl,OOO up- wards, and it looks as though policies of this class will continue to be in considerable demand.</p>
        <p>At the end of 1913 there were over 20,500,000 policies in force, of which 917,091 related to the ordinary branch, and 19,778,135 to the industrial section. . .</p>
        <p>As regards the company's investments, Mr.l Dewey stated that since the end of 1913 the re- covery in Stock Exchange values has improved the company's position to the extent of ovei £1,000,000, while at the low prices ruling during 1913 a sum of £7,500,000 was invested at an average yield of 5 per cent. In the meantime full provision has been made out of the 1913 accounts for the depreciation which occurred during that year, while the bonus distribution has been maintained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>Annual Bonus Distribution.</head>
        <p>Annual Bonus Distribution.</p>
        <p>Industrial branch policyholders are admitted to a share in the profits of the company, and the following table shows the policyholders' bonus under the company's profit-sharing scheme: 1908, £140,000; 1909, £200,000; 1910, .1:260,000; 1911, £300,000; 1912, .C 360,000 1913, ..C.400,000; 1914, £400,000. This total of .C 2,060,000 is in addition to a sum, exceeding £5,850,000 which ha* been provided by the company, in respect of the increased benefits given to policy- holders prior to the year 1907. The bonus for 1914 will be paid by way of addition to the sums assured on all industrial branch policies of over five years' duration which become claims either by death or maturity of endowment from the 6th of March, 1914, to the 4th of March 1915, both dates inclusive, as showli in the advertise- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>Remarkable Warrington Figures.</head>
        <p>Remarkable Warrington Figures.</p>
        <p>We belie the Warrington district, of which Mr. S. Broeklehurst, 69, Sankey-street, is the superintendent, had a most successful year, and helped considerably in the company's prosperity during 1913. On going into figures it is surpris- ing to find the indirect good a company like this is doing locally, for no less a. sum than 0817,000 was paid during the past year in claims in Warrington. Further, since the commence- ment of the payment of benefits in connection with the new Act, in January, 1913, a sum of £4,000 has been paid by the company's appfuved societies to members in this town, which sum includee 328 maternity benefits. It may also be mentioned that the Warrington district enrolled considerably over 5,000 State members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>MR. BONAR LAW'S "FACTS." Truth About Prudential Company's</head>
        <p>MR. BONAR LAW'S "FACTS." Truth About Prudential Company's</p>
        <p>Assurance.</p>
        <p>A very interesting and significant illustration of the manner and the methods by which the Insurance Act is attacked was provided by the debate on the Act in the Commons on Thursday .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <p>night, says the "Daily News." The gravity Of the discussion lay in the attempt cf Mr. Bonar Law to drag the Prudential Assurance Company into the position of seeming to support the scare as to the solvency of the Act. As reported, the Opposition Leader said:</p>
        <p>It is my belief that the financial provi- sions of the Act have entirely broken down. There is a very widespread feeling thatithat is the case. As an illustration of the e*tent to which it exists, I am told that only to-day at a meeting of the Prudential Assurance Company a shareholder asked the Chair- man : "Is the society bound to pay the minimum benefits?" and that the reply was: "Certainly we as a society are not bound to pay them." Then the shareholder rose up and said: "Is the Government bound to pay them?" and the reply was: "Ask Mr. Lloyd George." That is a view which is becoming general throughout the country. What view?" asked some Ministerial mem-</p>
        <p>"That there is a danger of the minimum benefits not being paid," was the reply, "in the case not of one or two societies, but of a large number of societies."</p>
        <p>What Really Happened. There is a great deal of confusion in Mr. Bonar Law's description of what occurred at the Prudential meeting. The following is what happened.:</p>
        <p>jr. Allanson (to the Chairman): Do we understand that the minimum benefits of the Prudential insurance societies for laundresses are guaranteed by the shareholders? The Chairman: Certainly not, sir. Mr. Allanson: Are the minimum benefits of the approved societies of the Prudential Assur- ance Company guaranteed by the State or by the shareholders?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: You had better ask Mr. Lloyd George. (Laughter.) I cannot answer the ques- tion. They are not guaranteed by the share- holders.</p>
        <p>In explanation it should he pointed out that the Prudential manages half-a-dozen approved societies (one of them the laundresses' society mentioned above). It receives no pecuniary re- ward for doing so, but neither is it liable for any Joss._</p>
        <p>i..?Vhat Mr. Bonar Law did was to substitute society for shareholders and to make it appear that according to the Chairman of the Pruden- tial the approved society was not bound to pay the minimum benefits—a very different thing.</p>
        <p>Full Benefits Paid. A representative of "The Daily News" has the authority of the Prudential Chairman for stating that he expressed no views on the possi- bility of benefits not being paid by approved societies. Nor could he do so, having regard to the fact that no actuarial valuation has yet been made. As a matter of fact, he added, every ono of the six societies in connection with the Pruden- tial is paying the so-called minimum benefits. They have actually paid out fully 1/ millions in cash, to say nothing of the benefits received in ' other forms of the cash value of at least 11 millions. Moreover, the societies ItTZ vontinu- ing to pay' benefits at the same rate. One-fifth of 4.:h people insured the rnited Kingdom, it may be stated, are members of one or the other of the Prudential approved insur- ance societies.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>, r or.•!! - 1, , 1!!.! - A 0 , :,,,..:.... ir -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>METAUPOLISHt</head>
        <p>METAUPOLISHt</p>
        <p>;#**#.**'.#,,Y</p>
