        <p>SPIICIAL TRIAL a . i. 8 FRILLING Sprinkler Tina A /,6 1 Carnage Paid. Tine tee. 4/6 Free simple on retains of card oyeue, dealers °aria and address. Agent. *ranted. ' PYlrsre Bros.. Maratinaten St Deirtnil _A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>hers.</head>
        <p>hers.</p>
        <p>ATTRACTIVE WIDOW.</p>
        <p>Amusing Episode at Runcorn Board</p>
        <p>of Guardians.</p>
        <p>THE HALF-YEAR'S RATE,</p>
        <p>Whilst Mr. Morris was proposing the engage- ment of a certain nurse at the Runcorn Guard- ians on Tuesday, he spoke of her qualities, and. concluded, "and, gentlemen, she is a willow." Mr. Linaker, sen.: I would like to know whati Mr. Morris meant by saying she is a widow.l (Laughter.) Mr. Morris: We were very much struck with her looks, and we engaged her iiramerlfidtelly. (More laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <p>LOSS IN RATES. At a meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Boundary Committee, held on 4th March, Mr. C. E. Linaker presiding, the Clerk reported upon the loss in rates that the Board of Guardians and Rural District Council would sustain in the event of the boundaries of Lancashire and Cheshire being altered in accordance with the representations of the Lancashire County Coun- cil. After a long discussion it -was resolved that Mr. James Diggle, of Heywood, be engaged as an expert witness on behalf df the Guardians and Rural District Council, and that the Chair- man of the Rural District Ctouncil and the Clerk and certain ofvners and occupiers of property within the parishes be ago ,called as witnesses against the proposed alterations. It was also decided that the Parish Councils of Grappen- hall and Thelwall be communicated with, to ascertain whether they proposed opposing on their own behalf, or desired to join with the Rural District Council. The Clerk • was also authorised to engage a leader and junior coun- sel. ,</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr. Lister Kaye stat- ing that the Parish Council of Grappenhall had appointed him to attend the inquiry. The report was agreroa to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>"DIDN'T WANT TO DO IT."</head>
        <p>"DIDN'T WANT TO DO IT."</p>
        <p>It was reported that at a special meeting of' the Visiting Commitbee the provisions of the Poor Law Institutions Order, 1913, were consid- ered, and a number Of recommendations made. Under this order much extra work falls to the lot of the Guardians. Mr. Morris, as chairman of the Special Com- mittee, moved the adoption of the report with mingled feelings. There were a lot of things that they would have to do which they would not do if they had their own way. However, they had to do it— Mr. Pilling: " And we didn't want to do it." (Laughter.) Mr. Morris, continuing, said they had in- creased duties. "Members of Parliament are getting paid," he joeularly remarked, "and I think we should be paid." (Hear, hear, and laughter.) The Chairman: Will you give notice of motion? (Laughter.) Mr. Morris: Not at the present time. (Laugh- ter.) The report was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>HOSPITAL RECOMMENDS.</head>
        <p>HOSPITAL RECOMMENDS.</p>
        <p>How many recommends are the Board of Guardians entitled te for the hospitals and in- firmaries to which they subscribed? This was a question asked by Mr. Morris. Mr. Pilling said they were entitled to send patients when they had them, regardless of numbers. Mr. Morris: I don't think so. _ . .</p>
        <p>The Clerk: The Guwa;ii-L; -I;;:e not limited to any certain number of cases. When we have a case we send it and it is accepted, provided, of course, there is a vacancy. We don't have so many recommends. Mr. Morris: You will find out that you can only send so many, according to the amount of the subscription. The Clerk: No doubt if you send a large num- bei they will ask you to increase your sub- scription. The matter was left in the hands of the Clerk, who is to report later.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday, Mr. E. L. Bent presiding. The Clerk submitted his estimate for the half. year ending 30th September, 1914, and he recom- mended that a call of £15,340 be made on the Overseers. The estimate for in-maintenance was £.3600 for the Werkhouse, and £9OO for the schools, and for out-relief £2,900. The estimate for lunatics in asylum was £2,700. Mr. Jolley said the matter had been carefully considered, and the actual increase in expendi- ture was only £240. It wag quite impossible to call for any less with safety. There were sub- stantial reductions in regard to several items, but there was an increase in expenditure for the repayment of loans and interest, the amount being £2,310, as against £1,678 7s. 7d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>HALF-YEAR BUDGET.</head>
        <p>HALF-YEAR BUDGET.</p>
        <p>The Clerk submitted an estimate of the ex- penditure for the half-year ending 30th Sep- tember, 1914. The total receipt's for the cur-1 rent half-year was £21,128, probable expendi- ture £16,730, leaving a balance at the end of the current half-year of £4,398. The probable expenditure during the half-year ending Sep- tember 30 next was £21,482, which included common charges £5,320, county rate £12,970, balance and contingency £1,192. The amount required from the overseers was £17,084. A rate of Is. 4d. in the .6 on the assessable value would produce .617,026, and it was recommended that a call for £17,084, being an increase of one halfpenny in the .6 for the corresponding period of last year, for general expenses', and £1,048 for special expenses, be made upon the over- seers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>WONDERFUL HEALING. Zarn-Buk Grows New Healthy Skin.</head>
        <p>WONDERFUL HEALING. Zarn-Buk Grows New Healthy Skin.</p>
        <p>CLEARS AWAY .DISEASE AS IF BY MAGIC.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk has brought the treatment of skin affections and injuries to a point of perfection never before attained. Skin magic, in fact, is how Zam-Buk's wonderful healing feats are fre- quently described. Nothing else known to Science has such a wonderful influence on an injured or diseased skin. Zam-Buk, indeed, reminds one of the rare and wonderful herbal balms of the ancient Gladiators. Zam-Buk is a rare and unique combination of the purest vegetable oils and herbal extracts, highly refined and concentrated, and is manu- factured under the most scientific and hygienic conditions by skilled chemists. It is in its unique herbal composition, and its freedom from lard and all animal fats, that Yam-Buk differs so completely from common salves and oint- ments. And its repeated successes show how different it is in medicinal action. Every box of Zam-Buk placed on the chemists' counter IS A C'ONCENTRATION OF THE WORLD'S MOST WONDERFUL HEALING PROPERTIES READY FOR INSTANT</p>
        <p>L'SS. When Zam-Buk is applied to the skin, fester- ing is stopped, inflammation and irritation allayed, sore places disappear like magic, and new layers of healthy tissues are made to grow. Equally invaluable is Zam-Buk for poisoned wounds and sores, which need the corruption drawing away and new skin creating; those troublesome ringworms and scalp, sores that cause mothers so much worry; and aggravating piles that tell their tale in their own weakening way. All are relieved, and the surgeon's knife evaded by the marvellous healing power of Zam-Buk. At this time of the year Zam-Buk is particu- larly valuable in clearing the skin of those troublesome blotches, pimples and rashes. Zam-Buk is so refined that its active medicinal essences are absorbed deep down beneath the outer layer of skin. ZAm-Bux HAS REAL norenvAt which toilet creams ond powders do uot</p>
        <p>possess., MMints of !amities at home and abroad find Zara-Buk the quickest and surest healer for cuts, bruises, sores, burns, scalds and knocks. Zam-Buk gives such benefits as make a box of it a -wise investment in every home. When buying Zam-Buk you can tell it is the genuine article by seeing the name " Zam-Buk " on the package and on the Government Stamp by which the package is fastened up. All others are worthless imitations.</p>
        <p>iXO Kills Rats &amp; Mice (registered). Government testimony with ever 1 y box. The World's greatest. No danger or smell from dead vermin. Safe, Sure and Quick. Chemist agents: Harold Smith, TT, Bridge Street. Warrington; Joseph Taylor &amp; Co., Radcliffe; J. Dowson, owns. ikl., 64.,1P,21-, post 24. Makers, The " Ester- mino" Chemical Co. Ltd., Dundee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>DEPUTATIONS COST.</p>
        <p>The Bev. A. M. Mitchell's Complaint at Board of Guardians.</p>
        <p>ALCOHOL IN WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>A MAN OF PEACE.</p>
        <p>- _ The Rev, A. M. MitOhell drew attention to the fad that the deputation expenses were £5O, as against £ll ss. 6d., and that, he said, was a substantial increase. He would draw attention ' also to the fact that there was no estimate for alcohol in the house for medical treatment. He would be told that it was included in the main- tenance, but he thought there ought to be some information given as to the amount. Mr. Jolley: If you like to quarrel with -the item for deputations and suggest it be reduced to £2O it won't affect the real expenditure. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell • J don't want to quarrel with anyone; I am a man of peace. (Laughter.) Mr. Jolley said the estimated profits -were practically the same as the previous year. By a stroke of luck the profit was more than the previous year.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: That is right. Mr. Jolley: I am glad I have satisfied Mr. Mitchell on one point. The estimate for alcohol has never been shown separately. (Hear, bear.) The minutes were confirmed.. THE NEW OFFICES.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Office Sites and Buildings Committee Mr. Owen submitted plans of the proposed new offices and Board-room in Museum-street. The new building would pro- vide reasonable office accommodation for the Guardians, Rural District Council, the Super- intendent Registrar, Assistant Registrar, and waiting-rooms. The committee approved of the plans and instructed the Clerk to take such' steps for the work to be proceeded with as sOon as possible. • The Chairman said they were of the opinion that the plans were in perffet order and ought to be carried out. The minutes were confirmed. INFIRMARY SUPERINTENDENT.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. Mather it was decided to appoint Miss Lizzie Ernerton super- intendent nurse at the Workhouse Infirmary, at a salary of £65, with emoluments. The Clerk read extracts from a report by Mr. W. H. Young, Board of Education Inspector, who said that general educational work at the institution had been well, maintained by the new superintendent of the homes. Strenuous efforts had been made to make the instruction more human and less mechanical, which was more for the benefit of the students. The report was adopted and referred to the Homes Committee. A vote of condolence was passed with Mr. and Mrs. Simpson on the death of their son.</p>
        <p>When is the Parish Meeting to be held? This is the question some "Heathens" ask. It is six months since the last. In the interval grievances have accumulated. The next meeting promises to be lively. What about the Fire Brigade's feats now? Will their traducers give them a Teeny hit of praise for promptitude? Warrington is jealous of Stockton Heath. At least this was evident from what town planning confab. ors fear that the -Heath aaarstit.saiizicdttoinn ctloieuntoc will ~ The artisans to the Cheshire side of the water And The borough is waiting to see what the "Heathens" them the very Poorest folk. , Do in the way of town planning before they In Warrington attempt to do anything at all. We have heard a lot about the dignity of The ancient borough of Warrington, but .is not This a marked lapse of that boasted "dignity" ? Six ships were gathered at once at the Bridge. Some foTk wondered if the new clock had been made.</p>
        <p>The new cook lad arrived at a Heath house. Ahe mistress and'maid started to talk cooking. suppose you are a fine cook?" quoth the maid. " No, I'm a very poor cook," answered the mistress.</p>
        <p>"So am I. but then we will get along fine." So came the startling remark from the new cook. • The local'ra'tes next half-year promise - To remain at the same figure, at least So far as the Guardians' call is concerned; but whet Will the County Palatine ask for in rates? Amateur gardeners are now hard at work. Is it dread of a deputation from the Garden Society Which has sent them on the tidying-up track?</p>
        <p>No Licensing Matters.—The adjourned licens- ing sessions were fixed for Tuesday at the Police Buildings, Stockton Heath, but there were no applications to be heard.</p>
        <p>"Proceeding Satisfactorily."—The Walton sewerage works are proceeding satisfactorily, so it was reported at the meeting of the Runcorn Aural District Council at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday.'</p>
        <p>On the Bench..—At the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions on Tuesday the magistrates were Messrs. 11. Talbot (in the chair), Roger C. Parr, J. llepherd, (I. Dakin, T. Baxter and W. Pletcher.</p>
        <p>No. Dogs, No Licences.—Thcre were numerous applications for exemptfons M dog licences at the Stockton Heath'Petty Sessions on Tuesday, when Supt. Owen successfully opposed a num- ber of- them on the ground'that applicantA had no dog. As there was no dog there was no ex- emption.</p>
        <p>Firefighters' Promptitude.—A fire occurred at Stockton Lodge School on Saturday afternoon. and in a few Minutes after the alarm was given the local fire brigade were on their way to the scene. A quantity of bedding in a room was found alight, but the fire was promptly checked and filially extinguished before much dama,ge was done.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Choir Sunday.—Choir Sunday was celebrated at the Stockton Heath Wesleyan Church during last week-end. On Sunday there were good congregations, and the special preacher was the Rev. E. Murphy, of Atherton. In the morning the choir splen- didly rendered the anthem "The Lord is my Shepherd," and in the evening "Judge me, 0 God," and "Sing unto God" were given a first- class rendition. The soloists in the morning I were Miss Webb and Mrs. T. Brereton, and At the evening service they were Mrs. E. A. 0 Sanders and Mr. H. Cliffe. On Monday evening Ia concert was given in the schoolroom. The programme was provided by the following: Misses E. Turner, Horton, Webb, M. Webb, E. Whitfield, and Taylor, Mrs. Gould, Messrs. J. White, J. Turton, Whitfield, Williams, H. Cliffe, T. Brereton, A. T. Hughes and Potter. The choir, under Mr. T. E. Penketh, gave a number of glees. Mr. J. White and Miss E. Turner accompanied.</p>
        <p>Passed the Time of Day.—" He got hold of his gun and said he would knock my brains out," was the allegation which William Moore, road- man, Little Leigh, made against Bold Robin- son, gamekeeper, of Whitley, who was sum- moned at the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions on Tuesday for using threats.—Prosecutor said on the afternoon of February 23rd lie was work- ing on the higher road, Lower Whitley, when he saw the defendant. He thought he stag- gered a little, and when he got to where witness was working he asked if there was any fighting to do, to which prosecutor replied, "If you go about your business there will be no trouble." It was then alleged that defendant used the threats.—Prosecutor, asked if he was afraid of defendant, replied he thought he was in great danger when a man stood over him with a gun. He said there had been trouble between them foi: some time.—Defendant said he only "passed the time of day," and never stopped walking. He strongly denied the charge, and the case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>St. Thomas' Band of Hope.—There was a good attendance on Thursday evening at St. Thomas' Band of Hope, when a very instructive address was given by the Hon. and Rev. C. F. Cross, the Rural'Dean, on "Seeds and Sowing," showing how small beginnings often resulted in great things. Prizes for the competition, " What I think about our Band of Hope," were very kindly given by Miss Heath. It was also object. lesson night, and altogether a very pleasant evening was spent. On the motion of Mr. Stan- nard. seconded by Mr. Hough, hearty votes of thanks were passed to the Rural Dean, to :qrs. Milling, and to Miss Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>ahurth</head>
        <p>ahurth</p>
        <p>LEGH-STREET CHOIR FESTIVAL,</p>
        <p>The annual choir sermons of the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church were preached by the Rev. A. J. Chessum on Sunday. In the afternoon Maunder's cantata, " Peni- tence, pardon and peace," was rendered by the choir, whose singing in the choruses left nothing to be desired, the attack being both vigorous and well sustained. The solo parts were taken by Mrs. A. E. Hoyle (soprano) and Mr. J. W. Warburton (bass), both acquitting themselves with credit. They showed fine judgment in their singing of the various items which fell to them. At the evening service Madame Alice Wood- land sang in a most sympathetic manner "0 Divine Redeemer." The other soloists were Mrs. A. E. Hoyle, Miss Jessie Woodland and Mr. 11. Hamilton. The choir sang "I waited for the Lord" (Mendelssohn) and "The day is past and over" (Marks) in a veil' tasteful manner, responding readily to the baton of Mr. A. Spruce, who con- ducted with his usual ability and judgment and deserves credit for the evident care with which the choir had been trained. Mr. F. Banner presided at the organ and per- formed his duties with much skill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>'WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</head>
        <p>'WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>" The unrest around us" was the theme taken by Dr. Mellor at the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday afternoon. The revolt they saw devel- oping all over the world tended to a change from the customs of the past. In some respects the revolt was a religious effort, which it h hoved them to foster. To-day they required militants in religion as well as in labour. What was needed was a better realisation of individual freedom. freedom. Men became sick at heart because of their surroundings, and they felt that a radical change in the mode of life was neces- sary. Then there was the women's unrest,' which aimed at the attainment off higher and , nobler ideals, and they asked not simply fox respect, but for something higher. They were crying out for more liberty and freedom than ! they yet possessed. Great civilisation would bring about a greater change than was ever before known in the world's history. The con- ditions of the past would not do for the present, and far less would they do for the future. This rest concerned religion, and religion had noth- ing to fear from the revolt. Mr. Payton read the lessons, and Mr. B. Farrar (the secretary) led in prayer. A solo, "The Way, the Life," was admirably rendered by Mr. 11. Hawthorne. Seven new members re- ceived a hearty welcome. A vote of sympathy was passed to Mr. Ball and Mrs. Simpson on their great bereavement,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH</head>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH</p>
        <p>The pulpit was occupied on Sunday morning by Mr. T. 11. Gregson, of Cliff College, who is a candidate for the Wesleyan ministry. ' Mr. Gregson chose as his text the words, "I am the Light of the World." This, he said, was one of the seven claims which Christ made while on earth. It was spoken after the scene between the Saviour and the Pharisees, who had questioned Christ con- cerning the woman taken in adultery. Tht question received a sharp and crushing answer, as Jesus issued the challenge, "He that is with- out sin among you, let him first cast a stone at her." Then came a statement which had caused men to ponder in adoration, because of the richness of meaning contained therein. Christ turned to His disciples and said; "I am the Light of the World." This claim had caused men to be struck with the self-assertion which it displayed, and which, in any other, would have been re- garded as presumption. Three things commanded the attention: first, the statement; second, the promise which fol- lowed the claim; and third, the conditions which had to be observed before the promise could be fulfilled. The light of the Gospel was effective to-day in spite of pessimistic forewarnings of the doom of Christianity. Both history and ex- perience served to prove that all opposition and all adverse argument had failed to extinguish the great Light of the World.</p>
        <p>••i</p>
        <p>New Rating Basis.—A letter was read at the meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians on Tuesday from the Cheshire County Council in- timating the revised county rats basis, and this included: Stockton Heath £17,206, assessable £17,146; Walton Inferior .28,614, assessable value .28,404; and Appleton 028,882, new assess- able value 026.957. The new rate basis is for the purpose of meeting the partial changes that; had occurred in the rateable value of the pro-' perty,</p>
        <p>After the Hare.—At the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions on Tuesday, Joseph Shaw, of 26, Rose and Crown-street, Warrington, was charged, along with another man, who failed to appear, and for whose arrest a warrant was issued, with trespassing for game at Keckwich. —Two gamekeepers in the employ of Sir Richard Brooke stated that they witnessed the two men attempt to catch a hare by means of a net. When the men were questioned they refused to give their names until they were taken to a policeman.—Shaw was find 40s. and costs or one month.</p>
        <p>Third Man Necessary.—A fine of ~t 1 and costs was imposed upon Patrick Collins, jun., show- man, of New Brighton, who was charged at the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions with failing to have a sufficient number of men accompanying a heavy motor in Grappenhall-road, Stockton Heath, on February 13.—Inspector Breese stated that there were only two men in charge of the motor, whereas there should he a third man to render assistance to horses. &amp;c., on the road.— Defendant stated that he had been unable to get about because of an injury to his foot. He said he had 27 traction engines, and that was the second case against him, the other being two or three days previous to this, when he was also ill.</p>
        <p>Suspicious Landlord's Plan.—Herbert Wood, 15, London-road, Stockton Heath, was charged at Warrington Police Court on Saturday with obtaining food and lodgings to the value of 14s. from Mr. W. S. Roberts, 57, Wilderspool-cause- way.—lt was alleged that prisoner said he was a clerk at Crosfield's. The previous morning, Mr. Roberts, being suspicious, accompanied thc. man from the house on the way to work. They</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. Gould.—Writing in the Parish Magazine, the Rector says: "I cannot close without giving expression to the general, and widespread sympathy that the deplorably sad accident to Mr. William Gould has drawn from the whole neighbourhood. Mr. Gould was not a Churchman, and it is only during the past two or three years that I have known him at all intimately, as a brother member on the District Council. I trust I may not be exceed- ing my province in any way by stating here the deep appreciation that all associated with him must be formed of the soundness of his judg- ment and the high integrity of his character. His sudden death has been a real sorrow to his host of friends and admirers. The sincerest sympathy of all goes out to his poor wife and child and to his brothers, who are so well known in the neighbourhood, and to whom he was bound by ties of true affection."</p>
      </div>
