      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>Will of the Late Mr. F. Holt.—The will has been proved of the late Mr. Frederick Holt (72), of Rose Bank, Lymm, for over fifty years con- nected :with the Bridgewater Canal Company. The estate is valued at</p>
        <p>Methodist's Death.—Local Methodists will he sorry to hear of the death of Mr. James Manley, late of Hale, who passed away in his 7Dth year at his residence, in Avenue-crescent, Leeds, on Monday. The interment took place at Hare- hills Cemetery, Leeds, on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>Departures.—With the approach of the March quarter day, Lymm is witnessing several impor- tant changes in its inhabitants. Many persons will learn with regret that during this week two well-known families have removed from the dis- trict—Mrs. Hampson and her son, Mr. R. Hamp- son, from Beech House; and tha Misses Norris and Mr. IL Norris from Brookfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODIST SOCIAL. A Successful Entertainment.</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST SOCIAL. A Successful Entertainment.</p>
        <p>The annual tea, prize distribution and con- cert in connection with the Broom Edge Primi- tive Methodist Sunday School was held on Wednesday. After the scholars had given ample evidence of their appreciation of the catering exertions of the teachers and friends, a public concert; which was well patronised, took place. The programme, though a long one, was full of good things, and devoid of a single clull moment, a fact which the audience marked with earnest approval. _ _</p>
        <p>-Talented recitals were given by Miss Thomp- son, "The man with a single hair"; Annie Blease, "The new housemaid"; Oswald Percival, "The little fireman"; Joe Blease, "Lazy Tom"; Mr. 11. Perrin, and Mr. J. Perrin, the items of the latter two gentlemen affording a striking contrast of pathos and humour, each being well received. The above contributions were inter- spersed with an assortment of capital songs by Miss Astal, "Come, sing to me," and "Till the sands of the desert grow cold"; Miss Thompson, "Little grey home in the West," "Sympathy," and "Roses"; Mr. G. Oakel, '.When we went to school together," and "There's no friend like an old friend"; and Mr. S. G. Warham, "The Gift." Variety was supplied .in the form of some clever selections on the violin by Master Fred Rutter, and an excellent performance of the dialogue, "My Lady Haughtrington," by the Misses Astal and 0. Warham, whose efforts evoked roars of laughter. Mr. Chorlton was a proficient accompanist. A large number of the scholars were awarded books for meritorios u attendance, Miss Priestner gracefully making the presentations. _ .</p>
        <p>On the proposition of the Chairman (Mr. A. B. Edwards) a hearty vote of thanks was accorded the artistes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>COUNCIL ELECTION.</head>
        <p>COUNCIL ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Promised Contests in Several Wards</p>
        <p>The appearance of the notices announcing the date of the Urban District Council elections as Saturday, April 4th, has given an official im- petus to the events which usually surround such an occasion. This year there are three vacan- cies.</p>
        <p>In Booth's Hill Ward the retiring member is Mr. G. L. Welford, who has served the district loyally for over twenty years. We understand that he does not contemplate re-election unless the ratepayers are specially desirous that he should continue to represent them. Mr. C. E. Cowper has signified his intention of being a candidate for this ward. Mr. 0. G. Smith, the retiring councillor for Malley Ward, does not seek re-election, but several gentlemen have been mentioned as pro- bable candidates, namely, MeSsrs. W. G. Jones, J. Radcliffe, and W. 11. Finch. Mr. F. E. Evans was spoken of as the most likely successor to the late Mr. W. Gould as the representative of Lymm Ward, but he informs us that while he would have been pleased to do his best for the locality as a councillor he feels that his father, who is unfortunately not very strong at present, has first claim on his time, and he therefore regrets his inability to stand for election.</p>
        <p>Messrs. A. Mclntyre and S. H. Davies are described As prospective candidates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
