        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Co-tperation.</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>L_Tte reference to the Labour M.P.'s "co-op." boots reminded me of a meeting years ago at Which an old man proudly boasted that every rag and stitch he had about' his person repre- sented co-operative production. If all asso- ciated with the movement were so loyal the weaker side of co-operation would be exceedingly Strong, and one of the most pressing social napriosbulnedinesrst-uaonudld be practically solved. Let none I am by no means a thick-an']- thin supporter of the system which the Roch- a" Pioneers inaugurated four score years ago; but I believe (and I wish I might argue this at lengtli) that its application to agriculture would enormously benefit, not merely the farmers, but the , , -ountry as a whole. I have seen it at work 'll a small way, and I am sure that nothing but rlod could come of its extension in that direc- , ,wie,'!• The particular phase of co-operation t cri which we in these parts are most familiar : does not strike me as altogether lovely. The aver- ,ile Member who is scrupulous about purchasing , at the stores strikes me as a "slivi-hunter " , illirlY that and nothing more—and it seems I; i tfuo it were hoPeless to talk to him or her 1 (espetutiy • her) of the ideals which inspired the 1 "dere of the system which has in many '' litters eventuated in selfishness and I narrow- -1.-"edness. "Te and genuine co-operation as t . °,l,:ksinally preached is a beautiful thing; butin the distributive side of it as developed in ncashire has some rather ugly features. - Menace by Rooks." _ , i. was surprised by the avowed experience of . oe ' itillenlber of the Cheshire Chamber of Agri- '' tuh,,U lii,re who at last week's meeting declared saw on his farm a rook which was nOt a,„„seetntuitting some depredation. I have ch- at the rooks are always busy in fields - ,ittel.e Ploughing is proceeding, and I have been tnler and over that they do a great deal of 11 devouring the wireworms as they are te-fs,,tnrried up. I think, however, even their . ;,'Nends mild allow that they are capable of of '4.el4ief in the poultry yard, and for the sake coCle Many readers interested in the fanciers' . tireuill.n 1 may reproduce the following illnstra- (tee incident from the pen of an old acquaint- : iecres,cfelni own:—" The other morning, before oe,,4ere attired in suitable raiment for leaving ssing-room, a duck politely deposited a ma resh egg about a hundred yards from our, in the middle of a small paddock. atur ii , • ild a Y our thoughts turned breakfastward, Ilya- we began to hasten over our toilet, but al- Pi with one eye on the egg, not because we , ftte, especially hungry, but rather owing to the totL that as an old fancier we kne\w that it is rein w‘ise to leave eggs about. In perhaps two ces.„ll.`es from the time the egg had been laid, th-'lnlY not longer, a couple of rooks flew over B field, and with scarcely'an instant's hesita- „ n they pitched down by the egg. Our heart Yet it was impossible not to be interested, -"•1 so we watched. The birds walked in that Nid , way rooks have up to the egg, examined it :al'efully,, and then set to work. They obviously "PPreciated a breakfast egg!" • . 1t,.4 Smallpox HOSpital Needed? 0114 note that some £6,000' or so is to be spent th a Smallpox Hospital for Warrington. Is tflete not a much more economical and not less tiZtive way of providing for emergencies in s resPect? I once lived in a district in which elonatbreak of smallpox occurred. To supple- ile llt Permanent accommodation a canvas mar- ilowes Was erected. This happened to be blown 41411: In a gale. Thereupon the wiseacres in tiatit.°l.iti said one to another—Go to, let us Atte: such a mishap impossible for all future nli b. And to ensure that they added to the t,hlillding a handsome and substantial block several thousands of pounds. That is ani ably more than a dozen years ago, and I "at there there has not been a patient under: hay -'hent there since, Meanwhile the caretak e ilesse."4l(l t keep the plaCe heated and in readi._ Use at any moment. . l '-me ° - - 'Twice during, Ow , , th officer has Strongly urged e , the medical Of 'eral committee to adapt, it for the porpoSeist, he San"- .. im for plith i isis, the site ieirq, o rezy °lll- - in -ll respects suitable; but they a rel‘sistently turn a deaf ear to the eminently 40p:table sio,gestion, although it has been eteali Plain that if there should be a case of the , 'Pox-_a remote possibility in these days— _lol tileslatal could be made available without an War's delay. In view of such contingency, the A rrington Corporation might have a tempor- -met, ..v, a ' strUcture of light materials which ould be „ t'qed after use. Such are easily obtainable. sei..-- "„,, ,- 4Tic Methods. At the Philomathic Society's meeting, Mr. ti:tinan Nield spoke profitably of the applica- ' b,ll of scientific methods to modern manufac- !, :re. &amp;c. Illustrations in support might be " t'uonoltiplied almost indefinitely. Take the famous stm j the educated bricklayer. It was re- ' pris 'm in " Harper " some time ago, and I may extract : "Bricklaying, one of our oldest to-day carried on, except where, ';ehlentific management has transformed it, with . e same materials, implements, and methods wh • .ic 11 u-ere employed before the Christian era. s : cleace promptly inquires why an intelligent . Iliall should lower a hundred human pounds to 61er t a e a four-pound brick, and receiving no satisfactory answer, supplies the bricklayer with , a simple scaffold which keeps the pile of bricks always at the proper height. The workman tskes the brick in his hand and tosses it twice ?I' thrice to find the best face; science has the ~ hricks laid upon the scaffold with the best face °Alt, eliminating lost time and motion. The /;Lerkman uses mortar of varying consistency, t"hinli requires tapping with the edge of, his tl3wel; scienee provides for mortar alwabs of te-e same consistency, and does away with the i?-I,l)infl. The workman assumes any position to 14,7'01 he may accidentally become accustomed; t:i_lleet studies out the exact position for each in relation to the wall, the mortar-box, and lii I„Pile of bricks, and teaches the workman to nauj,lll) a brick in his left hand at the same ht that he takes a trowelful of mortar in lelight. The net result of all this is that ,itete, requires five motions to •lay a brick, he workman averages eighteen, and the bricklayer can now lay 350 an hour, as ' 120 for the workman who remains out- beneficial zone of scientific .manage-</p>
        <p>Th l. Lii, "Stab Wary Inspector. tl'esitit,enent-Colonel Eden, .11. M. Inspector of Vv,,4°lllary, who paid his annual official visit . 4tke blgton last week, is in great favour allitihs ot;'ive wherever his duties take h 44 always reminds me of anotheini. rd istin- Inember of the family—his brother, the °f Wakefield. The latter officiated .at i Wedding which I had to "do" seve,le ,_ brid: 840. and I followed to the house NatZli DerehtS to verify a quotation in the the tlits`sa given in church. Making my way among 4.4441:v.ct1i :efderedgueste, I button-holed the Bislrhl 0 red my e) request. "Well," was N, _ the manuscript is in my .bag, and 1 11 %%he „4t2a a moment; but first of all let us have 1/f_ bri:l4"." Fresently he returned with a plae Ity ~,7,t4i-cake, and this we discussed together. a,°iser„llt la that Lieutenant-Colonel Eden is pie- hat a sort of man, and he has Won not only 4 L evetion but the affection of all with whom '4B to do. of mira, Th "HMIS Escape." . )141t : incident narrated under this heading in Itt l?,k'd " Table Talk" brought to my mind 11'lli:i i" "to others. There was that of the negro 04241 l front a scaffold in like manner, and, 4 e *itli ti.ng the pavement by the, violent contact lit'lB skull, at once protested against respon- Y for the cost of repair. We will treat -- Continued in next column. 44141„1vT Loo,r kict 13 OLD —geeptheravages of time at bay by 141Pn ;Fey etreakewithLocnynn'sbu LP Htla HILIR er i'''44.!elk deepens to ainaturalcolourin a few clays. int, ~,.arltaina a youthful appearance. Luge bets. '4-90 tree 116 Bedford Laboratory,L madon.BE</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST —continued.</p>
        <p>that as apocryphal, if you please. A borough in which I fotherly resided had as Mayor a builder who was wont, to keep a close personal oversight on his men. He would constantly walk to and fro along the parapet 'of a block of houses in course of .erection, and, although ad- vanced in life, apparently had no fear. One day, howev,er, he slipped frpm the edge of a high roof, and fell to the ground level. Horrified eye- witnesses hastened to the scene of the accident, expecting to find him dead or fatally injured; and they were naturally amazed when he rose without assistance and declared that he was little the worse. He had had the rare good fortune to alight on a 'heap of soft rubbish, and after a short rest was able to go about his work as well as ever.</p>
