      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>PETER RYLANDS : AN ADDITIONAL OiAPTER.</head>
        <p>PETER RYLANDS : AN ADDITIONAL OiAPTER.</p>
        <p>The Early Days of the Temperance Movement and a Pioneer Warrington Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>BY 'ARTHUR</head>
        <p>BY 'ARTHUR</p>
        <p>There is a ch.apter in the life of the late Peter Ry-lands which merits a more careful telling than we have given. rt. is worthy of note, because it joins his name in 11 very interesting way with a nOtable movement which was to make itself felt far beyond the borders of the town. 1</p>
        <p>The first Total Abstinence Society was formed in, 1830 at Stockton Heath, and Friars' Green Chapel followed a short time afterwards. For a year or two the movement was in the hands of the enthusiasts: Its advocates were obscure but zealous men, who were discovering in their lives the value of abstinence. Enlightened ones in the churches were the next to take up temper- ance advocacy. Then came the day when it seemed necessary to have a periodical, and "The Liverpool and Warrington Temperance Herald" saw the light. Though the earliest to be planned, it narrowly missed the honour of being the first journal devoted to temperance, being forestalled in point of time by the "Manches- ter Temperance Journal." " The Herald" appeared.. on May 4th (inad- vertently dated April 4th), 1836, and was the joint responsibility of the teetotalers of, Liver- pool and Warrington. Its Editor was Mr. H. T. Turner, of Ash Cottage, Penketh, and the print- ing was done by J. Pannel, of 24, Byrom-street, Liverpool. But the printer does not appear to have given satisfaction to the Editor, who apologises in dignified terms for typographical errors arising from his distance from the press, and expresses his pleasure at seeing the work removed to Warrington. The issue of June 29th was printed by C. Malley, 15, Horsemarket-street. In July the printing passed to T. Hope, Buttermarket-street, and befbre the end of the nionth was in the hands of J. Haddock, the successor of William Byres. These details are perhaps worth extracting as preserving the names of Warrington printers of eighty years ago, the more so as only one copy has survived of the first volume of the " Her- ald." This is in the possession of the present writer, and bears the autograph "Peter Rylands, Bewsey House, 1836." The Prospectus of the journal, after announc- ing that it would be published every Wednes- day, says:— It will occasionally contain Reports of Speeches delivered on the Subject of Temperb once, and especially at Temperance Meetings, a p Extracts,ers Essays oth religious and moral, Letters, Strictures, Precepts, Maxims, Occasional Memoirs, Biography and dying hours of Reformed Characters, Anecdotes, Poetry, Useful Hints, Epitaphs, Valuable Recipes for Reformed Characters, particularly adapted to the Reformed Housewife, Temper- ance Hotels, Coffee Houses, B:c., In religion it will neither know the Episco- nor Baptist; and, as it will not be palian, Quaker, Methodist, Presbyterian, In- for • Political Information, it will kaduePeveilhdiielneitther Tory, Whig nor Radical. The work is intended to be a useful and Miscellany,interest- heu ca s cause particularly and adapted to itios t rpurcot- of Temperance, and edify as well the Moralist as the 'Re- claimed Drunkard. minogte t Each weekly issue bore the 'legend : All Communications to be addressed for- warded to the Editor, and t b pro edeposited in "The Herald" letter-box, Mrs. Flint's. nn site Islington Market, Liverpool;°- or o 91 Eridge-strect, Warrington. b * y post the</p>
        <p>musiThe paid', or they -will 'not he taken in. The Editor gave each week a list of Temper- ance, Societies and their meetings, from which it appears that there were three societies (at Stockton Heath, Friars' Green and Brick-street). conducted by the Quaker Methodists, and no at Wintick-street Wesleyan• Chapel." -:• '</p>
        <p>MR. RYLANDS ON "MODERATION" HYPOCRISY. • It was in "The Temperance Herald" that Peter Pylands first appeared as an author. :lEs ' contribution was printed in the issue of June 15, 1836. He had not long passed his sixteenth birthday, and wrote the following modest cover- ing letter to -the Editor:— "Dear 'Sir,—The publication of your periodical has given us much pleasure (for I can speak for others in addition to myself), and it is with feelings of sincerity that I wish it, every possible success. "If you think the following commirnication is worthy of a place in your pages, and that any good ends would be answered by publish- ing it—by granting your magazine as a vehicle for so doing, "You will oblige, dear Sir, "Yours very respectfully and truly, &amp;c." Here is the article! Philanthropy. Who has not heard of virtues;om HOWARD? Who has not extolled glowing with fervency in his praises, and offered supplications to the Omnipotent to raise who would glory in pursuing his Pa 'ho thus emulating his actiont? But do those w adise and dheirils the standard imitate his character an re act, feel any wish that dtheyacthhemselves may of his laudable virtue? "Deeds, not wares!' have been the motto of the wisest, and to use our Saviour's expression, "By their fruits ye shall know them, for what must we say of the man who, after uniting in the applausz of the Great Philanthropist, could view the most pain- ful suffering and the most abject misery with hardened indifference, or reward with a scornful laugh, the prayer of the destitute? HOWARD entered the most filthy and dis- gusting gaols in order to relieve the necessities of the prisoners, Would any of us delight in such an occupation? Are we desirous to be bene- ficial to our species? If these queries must be answered in the negative, then will all those plaudits which have escaped our lips, in the , praise of Him who underwent so much for the ' benefit of others, stand in awful array against ' us on that day, when we shall have to render our accounts, as the vain effusions of a hypo- critical and misled mind. But if, on the con- .trary, we can elevate our voices in a loud and heartfelt affirmative, there is an ample field for us to prove the sincerity of our assertions, and for the fulfilment of our resolutions. t Who is that being whose face is disgraced by a filthy and for-long-uncut beard—whose pallid and wasted cheeks bear the stamp of advancing disease—whose half-closed eyes swim with water —whose body is covered with rags—and whose walk displays weakness from the want of due nourishment and the feebleness of old. age? Surely this is a picture well worthy of your compassion—a picture, too, but very faintly painted and feebly delineated, when compared to the original, hundreds of whom are of daily occurrence' They are men driven to the last stage of human misery by a wicked, but over- whelming love of intoxicating liquors; brit no one, so how faint the description will mistake the portrait of the poor drunkar,d ! Would it not., then, give you pleasure to benefit his condi- tion and allay his misery? Drunkards consti- tute principally the tenants of gaols !, Judge, then, which would be the best and wisest plan, to visit and comfort them when in prison, or to prevent their incarceration, and all the indi- , gence and wretchedness concomitant, by destroy-</p>
        <p>• •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
