      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>MOUNFIELD.</head>
        <p>MOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>jag the cause. Delay not therefore; there re- mains but one alternative, either resign in tote kallin dc claim b m troi bring e consideredyour mind at s an efer friend resoluten t determination. Dispel the dark and threatening clouds of drunkenness which have so long m a n- obscured . the luminous and illustrious star of temperance ! Let the PASSWORD OF ABSTI- NENCE resound more loudly throughout the ' kingdom, and the white pennant of TEE- TOTALISM play gracefully in the breeze, while , its benign influence shall be felt throughout Great Britain! Hail back the fell MONSTER ALCOHOL to his den! Release from his grasp the emaciated drunkard and raise him to com- petence and happiness! Then, and then only. will England be a nation of the free; then and then only will the voices and songs of her happy and industrious sons and daughters rise as sweet fragrance to Heaven; then, and then only, will churches and chapels be crowded, and thousands of the poor bear the glad tidings of salvation. All these inestimable blessings are to be gained by prevailing upon the drunkard to put away, and totally abstain from, that filthy liquor which causes drunkenness. But deceive not Yourself with thinking that this purpose can be accomplished while you are not an abstainer. If this was the cas:, might not the drunkard' justly inquire "Why must I, a hard-working man, be deprived of my glass, if you are allowed to indulge yours?" Shake of then, immediately, if you are so fettered, the trammels of "Moderation." Moderation has never, nor will. it ever, save the drunkard, therefore you must not be a moderator. But surely I need not dwell long on this point: for his feelings must indeed be hardened, and his conscience seared. who, when by giving up a very slight sensual gratification (his " little drop." which custom, perhaps, alone renders palatable), might prob- ably prevent it. Who, I say, could calmly and withindifference view one (how much more hundreds of, his fellow creatures) close an un- happy life in a premature grave, and then con- template his spirit's entrance into that state which is as miserable as it is everlasting, and to the suffering of these pains which are as acute. poignant, and tormenting as they are eternal! PETILARCHES• Space will not allow the quoting of a rather facetious essay on "Pop," which " Petrarches " dated from Bewsey House on June 23rd, 1836. Beginning in July he contributed seven articles under the title "Hints to Reformed Drunk- ards," which urged the formation of reading societies and a teetotalers' library, and gave advice upon books and 'reading.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>USEFUL KNOWLEDGE-AND TEMPER-</head>
        <p>USEFUL KNOWLEDGE-AND TEMPER-</p>
        <p>ANCE.</p>
        <p>Before the end of the year another step had been taken by the temperance advocates. Its significance will be grasped if we quote from the minutes of a meeting held in the Theatre at Warrington on November Bth, 1836</p>
        <p>Formation of an Association in loc-arrington for the Promotion of Useful Knowledge in Connec-</p>
        <p>tion with Temperance. 'At a meeting, called for the above object, on Tuesday evening, November 8, 1836, Mr. P. Rylands, secretary, having read a letter from John Cropper, Esq., of Liverpool, who was ex- pected to preside on the occasion, stating the reasons which prevented him attending, on the motion of Mr. Thomas Eaton, carried unani- mously,. John Rylands, Esq., was called to the chair, when, On the motion of Mr. Samuel H. Broadhurst, seconded by the Rev. H. T. Turner, supported by the Rev. C. S. Sevier, and carried unani- mously, it was resolved : " That this Meeting, convinced of the benefits which have accrued from the operation of Tem- perance Societies, and of the importance of em- ploying every means of insuring the perpetuity, and of improving in other respects, and with referencelo higher objects, those who have been reclaimed through the adoption of abstinence principles, determine on the formation of a soelety,,Which,„while it shall acknowledge those .t.h.ougli„Tithout requiring subscrip- tion to anypredge, shall have for its special de- sign .the mental and moral elevation of the mem- bers of such Societies."</p>
        <p>On the motion of the Rev. A. J. Morris, sec- onded loSo' Mr. Joseph Smith, surgeon, of Pen- keth, and carried unanimously, it was resolved: That the following he the general rules of the Association:—</p>
        <p>i. That a society, not requiring a subscription to any pledge, be now formed, to extend its operations as far as may be deemed practicable for the purpose of promoting the diffusion of useful ,knowledge in connection with the princi- ples of Temperance. ii. That the society shall be denominated: The Association for the Promotion of Useful Knowledge of a Religious and Moral Character in Connection with Temperance, and shall con- sist of patrons, vice-patrons, a president, vice- presidents, a committee, and such officers as may be deemed necessary, of any denomination of Professing Christians. That no Party Politics or Sectarian Peculiari- ties be advanced or discussed in any meeting or Publication of the Association, or in any Lec- ture or Lectures which the Association may establish or support. That the society shall have a Depot for such Tracts, Periodicals and other Publications as the Association may deem necessary to circu- late, and that the " Journal," if made to contain other useful matter in addition to subjects on Temperance,. be the constant vehicle for such communicahons, as accord with the Principles of the Association, and all Papers, which the society may deem proper to have inserted. That the Association establish a Library (and recommend the same at each Branch, where practicable, to consist of works on general Literature, Morality, and the Arts and Sciences; the Works to be lent to any Persian, who shall pay the sum of One Shilling per Quarter. In addition to his or her Annual Subscription. On the motion of the Rev. H. S. Joseph, of Liverpool, seconded by Mr. Peter Phillips, and carried unanimously, it was resolved. That the following Gentlemen be elected Officers of the Association : VICE-PRESIDENTS.</p>
        <p>Win. Stubs, Esq., Jas. Cropper, Esq., J. Pylands. Esq., T. Escrigge, Esq., Joseph Stubs, Esq. TREASMER. Mr. Jas. Crosfield. SECRETARY. Mr. Peter Rylands. COMMITTIT• W. Beamont, Esq. Mr. Win. Kirkpatrick Mr. S. H. Broadhurst Mr. John P. Lane Mr. W. BFetherton Mr: R.Moutgomery</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph bvsfield Lodge Moore, Esq. Mr. James Carter Mr. IT. P. Mather</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Carter Mr. Peter Longshaw Mr. Geo. H. Crowther Mr. John Monks Mr. Wm. Clarke Mr. Peter Phillips Mr. Thomas Eaton Mr. William Phillips Mr. J. Forrest Mr. J. Rylands. jun. Mr. Benj. Greening Mr. T. G. R. Rylands Mr. Robert Garnett Wm. Wilson, bsq. A vote of thanks having been passed unani- mously to John Fitchett, Esq., for the use of the theatre, who kindly lent it on the occasion, and to Mr. Rylands for his services in the chair, •the meeting separated. Psi RYLANDS, Secretary. That Peter lands should have been hon- oured by the promoters of the society, and that he should successfully fill the post of secretary before reaching his 17th birthday, says much for the confidence he had inspired. Akerly he must have made himself felt in the Temperance movement, and the picture of a youth so en- grossed is an altogether pleasing one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
