      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of interesting</p>
        <p>Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>Experiments in electroculture carried on by the United States Bureau a Plant Industry dur- ing the past year have not had encouraging re- sults' Electrically treated plats of wheat gave an increased yield of only one-half bushel per acre above that of the check plats. in 1912. an increase of two bushels per acre was obtained. None of the experiments conducted by the Bureau has so far indicated that clectroculture is 'Practicable from a commercial standpoint.</p>
        <p>Wood That Won't Rot. According to the " Chemical News," a wood which will never .rot has been found in that of the mangrove. For four years pieces of man- grove wood have been lying in soaking pits at Collonges, Fiance, surrounded with all the ele- ments susceptible of producing the decOmposi- tion and rotting of wood in 'a minimum of time; but they have not yet shown signs of alteration. The quality is duo to the closeness of its grain and the large quantity of tannin it contains. mangrove wood resists twice as much bending strain as oak; it is easily worked and is not brittle.</p>
        <p>How Indians Make Flint Arrow Heads. • The old Indian art of making flint arrow heads was not so difficult as is usually imagined— white LUCII are making old and genuine" In- dian arrow heads now for commercial purposes and by the old Indian methods. Flint is not chipped with stone or with metal, but With water. When an Indian Wished to make an arrow head, he held a piece of flint in a lire until it was very hot and then allowed a drop of water to drip from the end of a stick upon the spot to be. chipped away. The sudden cooling made the flint chip off immediately.. Some cun- ning is, of course, necessary in the shaping of the arrow head, but the Old Indian method is the best that has been found.</p>
        <p>- Meat and Fighting Power. The wheat-eating Carthaginians were no match for the pork-eating Romans; the goat- .eating Greeks easily- overcame the herbivorous Persians; while the beef-eaters of England and the sausage-fed German battalions have proved their bravery and endurance on a hundred stub- bornly contested fields. • Yet even this rule has its exceptions, says the "Family Doctor," for</p>
        <p>the British Army contains no better soldiers than those of the Scottish and Irish regiments, to whom meat was a luxury until they found it in their daily ration. So, after all, whatever the PllYsiologists may say, a flesh diet limy not be Particularly conducive to ferocity of character.</p>
        <p>Modern Magic Wand. \ A curious magic wand has been developed in France. It is worked by the action of a con- cealed magnet, so that a mouse or other small animal appears to run up and down the wand in a mysterious way. The wand is a square tube of light wood covered with silk, and the animal is of celluloid and has a small piece of iron on the bottom Inside the rod is a small lead weight on an endless cord, and when the wand is turned up; the weight falls gradually and draws along a Binall magnet, also fired on the cord and just below the surface. When the rod is inclined, the mouse thus climbs up,to the top, and this can be repeated as often as one may wish. •.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>Boy Sui,eides Numerous..</head>
        <p>Boy Sui,eides Numerous..</p>
        <p>„... . The•rSuiCide Of shtotilboys in Gbrmany and Austria has become a problem of national im- portance latterly, the average worki»g out at three tragedies a week. A volume from the pen of Trans Ostwald indicts German home life as representing the ” all work and no play " stan- dard of upbringing. Statistics show that the suicides invariablconiev at the end or begin- ning of a term, the 'Victim in most eialastesb having failed failed to achieve Promotion. How serious a matter this is for the German boy few foreigners understand. It means ultimately t fail in his "going away" examination, which in turn entails two years' service as a common sol- 'trier, whereas should- be pass all that the State asks of him is one year as a " gentleman cadet," hicihg out of barracks. Without a doubt the • fear of these two terrible years in barracks— life in a German barracks has been exposed frequently—drives hundreds of boys to suicide.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>Darwin's Experiment.</head>
        <p>Darwin's Experiment.</p>
        <p>.1s of mud from Y of a certain t:hfleFaie)eberaufat u Darwin tells us in his "Origin of Species'" yearthat liine tteheek tmonthhree three different places beneath the surface of the water of a small pond, which mud weighed only GI ounces, and was all contained in a breakfast Cu!). lie kept it covered up in his study for six months, and during this period obtained from it the astonishing number of 537 plants. On November 15, 1911, a "Scotsman" contributor Planted a dozen acorns in a patch of soil taken from a hedge-root, of about 28ia. ia length, .by about llin. in width, and about 28in. deep, and he resolved to take a note of the number .of plants which should grow from seed naturally contained in it. By November 15, 1912, lie had removed front time to, time, as they showed' themselves, 155 plants: and by November 15, 1913, 58 more, or' for the two years 211 plants in all.</p>
      </div>
