      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>NAVVY'S ADVENTURE AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>NAVVY'S ADVENTURE AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>John F. Goodwin, a navvy, was fined 10s. 6d., or fourteen days, at St. Helens, for stealing a cap from Stanley's lodging-house, Salisbury- street. lie was also fined 258., or 28 days, foi smashing a plate-glass window worth £3 15s. at the lodging-house, when he was turned out because he was drunk and had no money.</p>
        <p>The Rural District Council of Sedbergh, re- sponding to pressure from the Local Govern- ment Board after an inspection of housing con- ditions, has adopted a scheme for building two blocks of five houses to take the place of con- demned houses in liiillthorp and Sedbergh..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <p>.</p>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS.</p>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>STORIES FROM THE EAST.</p>
        <p>_ A large number. of anecdotes and yarns are to be found in Mr. F. E. Powell's book, "With Eastern Merchandise" (6s.W. .London: Thomas Murby and „CompaK), of whichi the following arc a selection At the St, kudrew's ball, whhlwas attended by 4 eight itinidred at 10.14£15k4.,. -and where they had a procession of bagpipes; haggis,. and whisky, a rather good story was told. " What a pity," said a Scot to. his partner, a newly- married bride, " that a. nioe,• pretty young girl like you should -have married a Chinaman." " Whatever will.you think of my sister, then?" was her reply. " She's married a Scotsrpan!" In the levant I was reminded of a shock I once received there; I had said good-bye to one of my little boys while lie was cutting- up a sheet of illustrated texts. :,Without tn 34 looking at it, In) slipped one into my pocket-book, where a fortnight afterwards:l, discovered it. " Ye- serpents, how can ye -escape the damnation .of hell?" were the consoling words of the young hopeful's farewell message of -solicitude for his father's welfare. It was his youngest brother who propounded to -me the problem—" Is Satan Married?" "Never mind, daddy,".he said, as I was about to hedge, "I only Wanted to know whether his wife would be Mrs. Satan or, Mrs. Devil!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>AMUSING FORGERIES.</head>
        <p>AMUSING FORGERIES.</p>
        <p>The adventurous spirit which makes the young Englishman the most successful coloniser in the world is disclosed every day in the huge correspondence dealt with by the Immigration Department of New. South Wales and Victoria at Melbourne Place, Strand. A special offer is being made to boys of all classes, including town workers, who are willing to engage in work on Australian farms. Many boys who are other- wise eligible are prevented from becoming Aus- tralians because parents refuse their consent. In consequence the New South Wales and Victor- ian officials are, every day discovering, flagrant and amusing cases of forgery. During the past year hundreds of bright boys, • eager for the wider life of the new Common-wealth, and dis- appointed in not getting their parents to see eye to eye with them, have boldly signed their father's or mother's name with the intention of secretly stealing away from their homes. 'l'll e interesting thing about these forgeries is that they are sti!very obvious. The Same schoolboy's hand that writes " John Smith" to the appli- cation form will immediately afterwards write " James Smith" on the parent's consent form. Needless to say,- the Australian Government officials, while .very sympathetic with the brave young forgers, firmly reject them in every case.</p>
        <p>LABOURERS BECOME PRIME MINISTERS. Australia is to an extraordinary extent the home of sell-made men. The • present Prime Minister of the Commonwealth, Mr. Joseph Cook, began life as a miner. The ex-Prime Minister, 311. Fisher, worked as a boy in the coal mines of Scotland. Mr. Holman, the Premier of Nev South Wales, who is now only in early middle life, served his apprenticeship in a furniture factory in Tottenham Court-road. 31r. Scaddon, the Premier of Western Australia, was a few years ago a locomotive engine driver. The late Sir Henry Parkes, who had so much to do with the bringing about of Federation, began in England as a farm labourer. And it is not only in_politics that capable young men find opportunity. Australia is singularly free of class and class feeling. You never see there any reference to the lower, 'middle, and upper classes. There is, of course, the wealthy class and the wage-earning class, but all temperate, industrious wage-earnexs feel that the best posi- tions and highest honoters in the State are open to them. Many thousands of English lads of all classes between the ages of sixteen and twenty years will go to Victoria and New South Wales this year and engage in farm work. In • establishing this boy immigration and making it possible for parents to send their lads to</p>
        <p>• ;••,...„4,-1,.. •</p>
        <p>assured work on the Australian countryside for as little as £3, the Governments of New 'South Wales and Victoria are doing an excellent service.</p>
        <p>"MEMORIES OF CHARLES DICKENS."• Mr. Fitzgerald's position as only survivor of the Dickens' group has given a special tone to these "Memories:" The cordial relations be- tween Dickens and his followers, and the atmos- phere of comradeship under connuand',do actu- ally combine to form a-Pit,tuiv• of literary life which is singularly, attractive. Mr. Fitzgerald may be called the Boswell of the group; he has been storing up records for over "forty years"; and his "glimpses" of ,G. A. Sala, Wilkie Collins, Chaiies. Head, Harrison Ainsworth, Payn, the gentle Howitt„ the Trollopese, and many another of that ilk, abound in those personal touches which enliven the best bio- graphy. Even more valuable, perhaps, are the slight, but vivid, impressions of those who met Dickens on in equality; or whom he—naturally. —himself revered—Carlyle, Browning, Lytton,. and Forster. Mr.. Fitzgerald shows us, indeed, how eminently chsxacteriatic of the man, and how thoroughly justified, were the " Prelim- inary Words" with which Dickens announced his great venture. He was always fresh, light- hearted. and hopeful to the end. There were difficulties, of 'course—discouragement without, disagreement within—but the untiring energy and the buoyant vitality of the man at the wheel was proved universally infectious, and the spirit of personal friendship towards the public may be seen on every page. The picture presented of Dickens in his office, at the manuscript-laden chief's desk, reveals an aspect of his many-sided nature which is not so familiar as others. The infinite trouble he was willing to take about nearly all manuscripts, the detailed advice freely given to contributors, the "sweet 'reasonableness" of his rejections, are unique. It is an achievement which bears witness to remarkable personality, that he should have carried on such a work for twenty years, practically without a break, and with no perceptible falling off in the steady output of original fiction. That he should have done so much—largely to help others—and should have so thoroughly enjoyed doing. it, .will surely enable us to forgive the somewhat irritable vanity of which' he has been accused in less intimate relations. And the private charity, which Mr. Fitzgerald rightly ascribes to an active sense of "duty towards the. poor," throws an interesting light on the ."'exposures" and the graphic delineations of suffering and want, which crowd many of his novels.</p>
        <p>n),-eO,-,a(Av-t0v(),,,v.,,at0.w0-vo=k-vpoiez] CHILDREN'S CORNER. Concluded by UNCLE JACK. k'l-c2,:0AAE.,;.0,-,t(voyezetA:ovotw,tow 0 ° C 3</p>
