      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>NIECE'S NICE LETTER.</head>
        <p>A NIECE'S NICE LETTER.</p>
        <p>My dear Children,— As I have said more than once, I am always pleased to hear from any member, and therefore I was delighted to receive a nice letter from Niece Beatrice .Marshall. She writes:</p>
        <p>" Dear Uncle Jaek,—l have just finished my practice- on the piano, so I thought I would sit down and write to you. I was pleased to see the prize-winners' names in the paper. I am sure they well deserve the prizes. We will all have to try very bard next time there is a competi- tion. My little sister said she would like to be a member of the Children's Corner, but she is not quite seven years of .age.—Your loving niece,</p>
        <p>" BEATRICE MARSIIALL." It is a very interesting letter, Beatrice, and I would like your sister to join. 1 hope you will Icy in our 'competition, announced last week. The subject is, "What you would like to do dur- ing the Easter holidays, and give the reason why you would like'to do it." The competition closes on 28th March.</p>
        <p>I hope many will enter for the above compe- tition. Nephew Fred Shelley sends in some i more conundrums, but these I shall have to hold over until next week, for the printer man has just come in and said, "Space is full, Uncle ' Jack."</p>
        <p>* Your loving</p>
        <p>UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>THE BRAVE BARNACLE. There was a little Barnacle Stuck closely to a ship, As through the raging, roaring waves The vessel went " ker-zip!" "Oh, my!" exclaimed the Barnacle,</p>
        <p>"This trip will be our last, Unless through all this tempest Fmanage to stick fast." The waves dashed o'er them., mountain high And icy was the air, • But plucky little Barnacle - Kept sticking closely there. And when at last, all danger past, • The vessel came to land, The happy little Barnacle Felt very, very grand. Ile swelled with pride. "Hooray !" he cried. " I've conquered wind and wave! I bravely stuck fast to my post, Our noble ship to save! The sailors, too, it's very true, Showed splendid nerve and pluck; But I could see they needed use, So to the ship I stuck !"</p>
        <p>COMING OF SPRING. Spring, the youthful season, the season of jollity and mirth, has always been felt' to pos- sess a special charm. Nature, which has lain dormant, and to all appearances lifeless, through the long winter months, suddenly awakes. Tao trees begin to .show buds, and banks and hedge- rows are. alive with coming flowers. Birds fill 1 the woods with their cheery notes and insects buzz about in a freiNy of excitement. , The lark soars'. high up, pouring forth his 1 sweet song in the cloudless sky. The cuckoo signals the approach of the season, While the 1 robin no longer haunts the doorstep. From the earth spring up new flowers. Primrose and 1 daisy mingle their contrasting colours in a sweet harmony. The ground is tilled and prepared for the seed, from which the farmer expects to gather his fruits. The rooks and crows in their well-built nests set the whole country ringing with their caw—caw—caw.</p>
        <p>The frost of the air disappears, and cool fresh breezes bring with them fresh hopes, life and animation. And spring: when it does come, ex- ; hilarates, almost intoxicates, one with its new sense of beauty and freedom. _ _</p>
        <p>" Ah ! how wonderful is the advent of spring— the great annual miracle of the blossoming of Aaron's rod,' repeated on myriads and myriads of litancheW;:.the gentle progression and growth of herbs; flowers, trees, gentle and yet irrepres- sible, which no force can stay, no violence re- strain, like love, that wins its way and cannot be withstood by any human power, because itself is divine power."</p>
        <p>THE OWL AND THE PUSSY CAT. The Owl and the Pussy-Cat went to sea in a beautiful pea-green boat; They took some honey and plenty of money, ' Wrapped up in a five-pound note. The Owl looked up to the moon above, And sang to a small guitar, " 0 lovely Pussy, 0 Pussy, my love, What a beautiful Pussy you are— You are, What a beautiful Pussy you are !" Pussy said to the Owl, "You elegant fowl How wonderful sweet you sing, Oh, let us be married—too long we have tarried— But what shall we do for a ring?" They sailed away for a year and a day To the land where the Bong-tree grows, And there in a wood a piggy-wig stood With a ring in the end of his nose, His nose, With a ring in the end of his nose. " Dear Pig, are you willing to sell for One shilling, Your ring?" Said the Piggy, "I will." So they took it away, and were married next day By the turkey who lives on the hill: They dined upon mince and slices of quince, Which they ate with a runcible spoon, And hand in hand on the edge of the sand They danced by the light of the moon, The moon, They danced by the light of the moon.</p>
        <p>• NIAGARA FALLS SILENCED. Most of you will have heard of the Niagara Falls, but few will know that they once ran dry." Their mighty roar ceased only on March 31, 1848. Early on that morning people living near the Falls were surprised by a strange silence Many thought they had been afflicted with deafness, and all were oppressed by a sen- sation of dread. With the coming of the light the amazed People discovered the reason for the disquieting silence. Where they were used to see the great Falls was a bare precipice, down the face of which a few small and constantly diminishing streams trickled. i'fbore the Falls, instead of a rushing river, was only a naked Channel, with insignificant brooks splashing among the rocks. All day long this astonishing condition continued, and persons walked dry shod from the Canadian side along the edge of the precipice as far as Goat Island, on the American side.</p>
        <p>Early in--tl; morning of April 1 the familiar thunder of the great cataract was beard again, and never has been silent since, though similar conditions with like results might prevail any Spring.</p>
        <p>The- winter of 1847-8 was one of extreme sever- ity, and ice of unprecedented thickness formed on Lake Erie. When the break-up came, towards the end of . March, a strong south-east wind was blowing, and ice was piled into banks as large •as icebergs.- On the night of March 30 the wind suddenly changed into the opposite direc- tion. increased to a terrific gale; and drove the ice into the Niagara River with such force that a huge dam. was formed of such thickness and solidity as to be practically impenetrable, and Strong enough to hold back the great mass of water pressing against it. At last, in the early Morning of April 1, the ice dam gave way under the tremendous pressure of restrained water, and the Falls were one of the scenic wonders of the world mice again. \</p>
      </div>
