      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>EARLESTOWN EISTEDDFOD.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN EISTEDDFOD.</p>
        <p>There was a record number of entries at the Earlestown Chair. Eisteddfod on Saturday, in the Town Hall. The Rev. D. Owynfryn Jones was the conductor, and Mr. Harry Evans adju- dicated the music. The prize in the juvenile choir competition was secured by the Dotal- road Council School, Salford, conducted by Mr. J. E. Davies. A Prescot girl, Amelia Ilarren, was highly praised by the adjudicator for securing the first prize in the lady's solo com- petition with an excellent rendering of "The Nazarene." The first prize for the girls' solo competition was won by lions Booth, of Man- chester, and the second by Edith Roberts, of Ashton.</p>
        <p>The chief feature was a choral competition for Male voices, thirty to forty in number, the prize being a stun of with a carved oak chair valued at .25 for the conductor. ThiS WaS secured by the llighfield Male Voice Choir, Run- corn, conducted by Mr. Weedall. Followed by a huge crowd, the choir carried the con- ductor to the station shoulder high iu the chair.</p>
