      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 14, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 14, 1914.</p>
        <p>August had come, with its golden sultry heat, and Iresh dewy evenings of delightful coolness. Erica loved these evenings. They were linked up in her mind with thoughts of Jack. August had always brought Jack to Dale End. Sometimes she would wonder whe- ther when he came home it would not be in August that he would appear! August thun- derstorms brought splendid rainbows: and always the rainbow spoke to her of Jack— Jack and the golden cup which he was seek- ing for her sake—so far away ~ . " Erica, my child!" " Yes, father, dear."</p>
        <p>" I have something to say to you to-night, my love. Will you sit down beside me whilst I say it ?" She seated herself on a, low seat at his feet. They were out on the sheltered terrace, where so much of their life was spent, and where chairs and rugs and small tables made semblance of a room, though they looked straight out from between rose draped pillars across the moonlit valley. "Erica, my child, you know that things have been going badly with respect to Dale End for many years now." "I have been afraid of it, father dear." " And I have had to mortgage field after field, farm after farm: yet there is nothing else for it: though I knew I was robbing you in measure, of nur heritage, my child !' Dear father, do not thine of it so. Ton know that I shall never think of it like that—"</p>
        <p>' But it was my great trouble, Erica: and somehow Cuthbert found it Out. Do you know what he has done ?" „</p>
        <p>" Mr. Fanshaw, father ? 'What ?" " He has bought up all the mortgages. He is now my only creditor. And Erica—upon the day of his marriage with my daughter, those mortgage bonds will be destroyed by your dear hands: they will be his wedding gift to you. You know he has gone away to London for a little while—to make certain arrangements. When he comes back—he will come for your answer. He , has asked me meantime, to stand ambassador for him. Eri- ca, my child, Cuthbert Fanshaw loves you. He is coming to ask you to be his wife ! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>COMPLETE EXAMINER STORY.</head>
        <p>COMPLETE EXAMINER STORY.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESIIIIVED.I</p>
        <p>THE GOLDEN CUP.</p>
        <p>EVELYN EVERETT—GREEN (Author of " Our Great Undertaking," "me Magic Island," " Married in Haste," etc.).</p>
        <p>There were tears in Erica's brave eyes. Jack's young, resolute face wore the look as of a warrior going forth to do battle with the world, from which he meant to snatch the victory. Friends from childhood lovers uncon- fessed—save to themselves were they. And this hour of inevitable parting for an indefi- nite period of time was trying them both to the uttermost.</p>
        <p>About them were the dear familiar rocks and fells of her home; that place where his holidays had been spent, and which was to him the only home he remembered. How they both loved it! Every nook and cranny in the grim sides of the great tors was dear to them, the home of a hundred childish mem- ories. They loved the sweeping rain clouds and singing tempests; they loved the glow it sunlight, the flush of dawn, the golden glory of noontide, the magic transparencies of evening lights melting into twilight mystery. Best of all perhaps they loved those shining arcs of coloured light, which after rain would overarch their valley, as the sun sank to- wards his setting. It this very moment such a glorious rainbow was flung athwart the liquid rain-washed sky, that they seemed to i see where one end came to earth n a clump of golden gorse not a hundred yards from where they stood. Erica pointed with a smile to the spot. "Jack, do you remember how we used to hunt and hunt for the golden cup, where the end of the rainbow had been ?"</p>
        <p>1 How the words brought back those golden days, when they had woven dreams of what the future was to hold for them! The legends of the fell side were dear to them. He who found the golden cup where the rainbow touched earth would be prosperous, happy, fortunate—would win for himself his heart's desires. Orif a maiden found it, then to her would come the husband of her choice, the man whom she could love: who. would cher- ish her to her life's end— Suddenly they looked into each other's eyes—their hands met: their fingers clung together. "Erica, I am going to win the golden cup !" She knew what he meant. Jack's fortune was small: her father, the boy's guardian, had expended the bulk of .it upon his educa- tion. A very small capital was his now, with which to confront the world: and he was go- ihg far, far away to see what he could make of the opening of which he had heard. For Squire Dale was a man of dwindling re- sources and impaired energies. He wanted his daughter to marry a man of means. He made no secret of this fact. " Erica must save the old family property in the one way a woman could." She had been told this in varying, forms of words a thou3and times. And she loved Jack! "I am going to win the golden cup! Eri- ca., will you wait for me ?" Yes, Jack." He 'seized her hands and held them close. His boyish face had taken some of the force and earnestness of manhood. .</p>
        <p>" And I may be gone a long while, Eric: and your father does not wish me to -write to you. lie tells me to write sometimes to him: and if I go up county, as I expect, I may be out of the way of posts for months together —longer perhaps. Erica, may you write to me ?, Will you, my darling ?" ' Jack, dear, I think not. Father does not wish it. For us it is hard: but he is my fa- ther: ,and he gave you a home hem with me. Ile wants us now to forget one another. That we never shall d0...,We know that; and we must be patient. The time will come when we shall have our turn—our way. Till then—" Ile drew her towards him with a gesture vf infinite tenderness. Ile saw the tears in her eyes: all the strength of' his young manhood rose within him at the sight. For her dear sake he must play the man. " That's all right, Erica: till ther4 we can just belong to each other: and wait for the time when' come back with the golden cup. Then your father will be glad. And. I shall lind you here—waiting for me ?” "Yes, Jack: waiting here for you--always for you." Under the care of the rainbow—as it were —they exchanged a lover's kiss. That was their true farewell.</p>
        <p>" Well, my dear, so we have lost Jack! Of course we shall miss him: but it was high time the boy went out into the world. There is no opening in these days for a young man in the old country. Over yonder, with plenty of energy and a small capital a man may do well. And it was time for other things that he should go. It was all very well when you were brother and sister together, his coming to and fro here so much: but the time .has come when that kind of thing. has gone on long enough. I know, my dear child, that ycli cherish a little tenderness—shall we call it— for Jacic: It could scarcely be otherwise. But you will soon get over any transitory feeling of that kind: and presently you will be glad that he is out of the way.'" Erica smiled in a fashion which her father did not entirely understand: but she did smile, and that was satisfactory as far as it went. Squire Dale had every desire to be a kind and judicious father, Just as he strove. to be a kind and judicious landlord and farmer. He had inlany of the qualities which go to make up a fine personality: but some- thing was lacking in his nature---some weld- ing power which should knit these qualities together and give value to his efforts. And -just this quality was lacking. Ile ,was ineffec- tive as a man, and his estates were suffering from the effects of a certain lack of grip. Yet he would not suffer advice. Often Jack and Erica had gently sought to influence him to modify some of iiis views, or to change some of his methods: but never with any Success. He thoroughly trusted his own judgment whether in his personal relations with human beings, or with respect to his theories of land culture or estate management. When things went wrong, it was the bad season, the baa government, the growing idleness or incom- petence of the labouring classes--never his own methods or ideas.</p>
        <p>The summer following Jack's departure was a bad Ine: and it was followed by others which if not exactly disastrous, were unfav- ourable to agriculture, and brought trouble to farmers and landowners. In winter floods' swept the valleys, and sheep and cattle were carried away. In vain Erica strove to point out to her father that by the expenditure of a small slice of their capital they could do much towards reclaiming certain valuable tracts of land suited to corn culture, and fence their stock from peril in winter seas- ons. Jack had pointed this thing out to her many a time, and many a time urged it upon the 'Squire: but all in vain. Mr. Dale lia4 grown touchy upon the subject: and he was more irritable than ever after these recur- rent loeises, and clung with greater tenacity than ever to the funded money he had. If ruin came it would be all he and his daugh- ter would have to live upon: and now and again he experienced ugly moments of appre- hension, when the thought of ruin seemed to stare him hideously in the face. As for Jack, his letters which had been fairly regular at the first, had now entirely ceased. This was not wonderful, for he had gone up country, and was likely to be long there away from the radius of any -post ser- vice. Ile haa never said very much about himself or. his prospects of succesA. Letter writing is seldom the forte of the youthful man of action. His portrait stood upon Er i i- ca's table. Before it lay her diary n which each day she put down a few lines. " Jack will want to know all about my life when he comes back. lam keeping this diary for him." - - . _</p>
        <p>But the father, satisfied that Erica had quite got over her girlish partiality for her old-tirue compaulou,l and plumitig himself</p>
        <p>upon his perspicacity lh 'laving foreseen tile inevitable end to a mere boy and girl rom- ance, was now seriously casting about in his mind for a suitable husband for his hand- some daughter, who with her good old name, her personal beauty, and a fine (though cad- ly impoverished estate) for her ultimate in- heritance might aspire to a good match, and would grace any position to which her marriage might raise her. At a time when old families are often go- ing under, and new men coming to dwell upon old acres, changes in country neigh- bourhoods are of frequent occurrence. Squire Dale returned one day from the county town, where he was wont to attend the sittings of magistrates as often as his other duties permitted, with a subdued air of elation about him which Erica was quick to note. As she poured him out his tea, he spoke, and instantly the clue was in her hands. . _</p>
        <p>" I met for the first time to-day, the new owner of Roebuck Hall—that Mr. Fanshaw of whom I suppose you have heard." " Yes, father: I knew that a. Mr. Fanshaw had bought Lord Manson.'s place. So 5" have seen him ?"</p>
        <p>"Yes--and I am greatly pleased with him: nothing of the parvenu about him and indeed his mother was one of the CealYiss of Worcestershire--quite one of our Old fa- milies. He seems to be rather solitary: has no brothers, and both his sisters are married and live abroad or in India, I forget which. I told him that as the crow flies 1 was one of his nearest neighbours, and that quite inter- ested him. He is going to waive ceremony, and dine with us to-morrow night. His own place is still rather in confusion, found so much to do there., So he does not care for callers yet: but ,will be very glad to come over and see us. Possibly I may ask him to stop here for a short time. A house full of workmen is a dismal kind of Place." Erica, felt her father's glance resting upon her with certain meaning appraisement. A faint flush stained her cheek. he says. He "You must make things pleasant for Mr. Panshaw, my dear," said the Squire: and she dutifully replied, " I will do what I can, dear father."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>[IL</head>
        <p>[IL</p>
        <p>Mr. Fanshaw dined two evenings later, at Dale End. Erica found him talking with 'ner father when she descended to the drawing room shortly before the appointed hour. lie was standing with his• back to the light, looking down the valley at the view, which was one of singular beauty. Erica was aware of a slimly built man with no very salient point in his aspect; for he was neither tall nor short, handsome nor plain, neither fair nor dark; just a well-groomed young man of amiable expression, who talked in a pleasant, gentlemanly fashion, and seemed both appreciative and well-read when he en- tered into conversation. The dining-room at Dale End was a fine panelled room—the only bit remaining of the original ancient house, which at one time must have been almost a castle. In those days this fragment formed the banqueting hall, and its fine proportions, its groined and vaulted roof, and the beauty and anti- quity of its carved woodwork alike awoke ad- miration and interest. Mr. Fanshaw was loud in his expressions of praise and interest. Yet his glances were not all for the fine surround- ings in which he found himself. His eyes turned again and again to the bright, ani- mated face of the lady at the head of the table, and as the meal progressed he spoke less and less with his host, and more and more with the beautiful daughter, who graced her father's table, and played the part of hostess with such charm and anima- tion.</p>
        <p>For Erica's nittureWas 'bright: and happy, and the traditions of the place made for hos- pitality and friendliness to strangers. Also lihe enjoyed seeing fresh faces: it interested her to hear stories from the outside world, Which seemed somewhat remote from her own daily life. She drew out their guest by her eager and discriminating questions. He could talx well on. several subjects, and had a fair- ly wide range of interests. He' had been edu- cated at Eton and Oxford: he had travelled, and had been round .the ,world in his own Yacht. tie could answer questions and tell amusing anecdotes about the thing&amp; he had seen and the persons he had met. Travel tales had an attraction for Erica—for was not Jack a traveller questions concern- ing that far-off land where he was toiling in search of the "golden cup" were many and eager. After dinner he joined her in the gar- den in the soft summer dusk, and they talked together almost like friends of long• standing, while the Squire walked up and down with his dog at his heels, and paused at intervals to join in the talk himself, well pleased at the sight of the two young people SO much engrossed one with the other</p>
        <p>That first visit of Mr. Fanshaw's was quickly repeated. Before very long the Squire had dropped any prefix to his name; and at the end of three months he was " Cuthbert " to 'the master of Dale End. He was eager to consult with his more experienced neighour over all matters connected with his newly acquired land, and to ask Erica's counsel, and consult her taste with respect to the plenishing and interior arrangements of his house. Often the Squire would bid his daughter to come dressed for the saddle next morning. Cuthbert wants us over yonder, to advise him about "—this or that, would be his re- mark. Ad at the first Erica went willing. ly. It was a charming ride across the valley, and through the beech woods to Roebuck Hall: and it was interesting to see what wealth and taste combined could do for a fine old house which had run into disrepair and neglect. The gardens were beautifully laid out, but had been suffered to 'fall into de- cay. Now all this was to be changed, and Cuthbert Fanshaw appealed to Erica at every turn for instructions • and advice, which it was fasciireating to bestow, where monty. worked such wonders in so short a space. Yet as time passed by, Erica grew uneasy She had not been blind from the very first to her father's hopes and wishes: but her own calm common-sense had prevented uneasiness in the initial stages of friendship with their new neighbour. Reviewing the situation in the privacy of her own maiden's bower, she had told herself that here was a man of great wealth, who had travelled the world over: who had spent long intervals of time in -some of the fore- most cities of the world, who had his London club, his fashionable friends, and the entrée into the smart society of the metropolis. Was ? it likely that he would incontinently fall in love with the first Squire's daughter he hap- pened to .come across in the wilds of the country Was it not almest a foregone con- elusion that he was •already engaged to be married, and had bought this property with s view to his married life ? All wealthy per- sons had their town and country residences, and every week as they advanced' in intimacy she expected to hear him 'ell of the lady he was to marry. Hence she gave him her frank friendship and goodwill, and •in offering counsel and advice was perpetually wonder- ing about the other lady who was to Ire mis- . tress here: for now and again in some unde- finable way, he made her to understand that he was not thinking entirely of himself in t whom he alluded from time. to these preparations he was making: there was another 0 time in that half shy and eiusive fashion, which Erica believed to indicate the ardent lover, to whom his happiness was something beautiful and new. And this had touched her somewhat—for was she not herself a lover unconfessed ? so they had been drawn to- gether, and friendship ripened apace; till all of a enddcn Erica understood her danger. Her father's manner had made her faintly uneasy for h space of time before full en- lightenment came. But then, as she reasoned to herself, her father was in the case of the proverbial he with one chick,. He thought too highly of this particular chick! He could not rri himself of the notion that his posses- sion was coveted by others! He did not un- derstand the inner meaning of some of Cuth- bert Fanshaw's words nor share her con- victions as to what they implied. So he went on wandering in the land of his own dreams and ambitions: and Erica hoped that the truth would come to him gently: and that the advent of Fanshaw's wife would not strike him with dismay. Theo ouckleuly tell the- bolt frQm the blue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>IV.</head>
        <p>IV.</p>
        <p>" What shall Ido ? What can Ido ? 0, Jack, my Jack, where are you ? Why do you lcave me like this ?"</p>
        <p>Such was the voiceless cry of Erica's heart during that week of golden sunshine and silver rain storms which followed upon her father's tale of Cuthbert Fanshaw's generous dealings with him, and the hope which both men • cherished so strenuously in their hearts. Gently had Erica sought to break to her father the fact that her heart was not hers to give. He would not understand. She liked Cuthbert as a friend—well that would do for a beginning. When she knew him more inti- mately, she would learn his worth: his love would kindle hers. Squire Dale was not wont to be patient with those who opposed him: truly as he loved his only child, he regarded her in some sort after the fashion of bygone days, as somewhat .of his own chattel--a sub- , servient and dutiful female creature, of whom he could dispose as he desired. What he did desire was so manifestly for her own welfare in every respect that any objection was peculiar7y irritating. Erica did not dare to mention .jack's name. Where indeed was Jack? Had he perhaps himself forgotten ? That is what her father would tell her if ever she dared to speak his name. For three .years he had kept silence, so far as Erica knew. For two years Cuthbert Fanshaw had been their neighbour at Roebuck Hall, bringing the old place back from a condition of ruin- otts neglect to a state of remarkable per- fection. During that time he had become her father's close friend and a pleasant compani- on to herself. She liked him thoroughly— that was the hard part of it; and her con- viction that he would still deal generously by her father, even though she refused him the desire of his heart, did not tend to make her position any easier, or to aid her in reaching an adverse decision. The house stifled her. It had been a day of thunderstorm interspersed with brilliant sunny intervals. Now the• heavy clouds were rolling all away, and rainbow lights were shining in the sky. As though guided by some spirit " 't in her feet " Erica walked along the narrow track to that spot where she and Jack had • exchanged their vows of constancy before he went forth into the world four years ago almost to the day. She stood in the self same „spot,: ,her. heart cried out for her lost lover. And as -she stood alone where once they both had been, the rainbow bridged the valley again, and its ,foot seemed to shimmer in the same gorse bush not a hundred yards away. Scarcely knowing Why, Erica traversed this distance: there seemed to be something glittering with golden fire in that bush. As shc drew nearer her heart began beating tu- multuously. There was something. It did glit- ter! Next minute she had plunged hand and arm fearlessly through the pri9kles. She drew forth—a beautiful golden cup ! With the cup in her bands, she stood up: she held it high above her head. She called a name aloud.</p>
        <p>"Jack, Jack—my own Jack! Come to me. I want you!" Was she surprised at what followed ? She never could tell. From some unseen vantage spot a tall figure sprang up. She was locked in the strong arms of the man she loved. Jack had come ,to her in the hour of her suprem- est need.</p>
        <p>"Jack. Jack!—where have you been all this long weary while ? Why did you not write ?"</p>
        <p>"I have written always--to your father. But not 60 often after he told me—that you were to be—married."</p>
        <p>--- " Jack! Did. he tell you that ?" " About a y.ear ago: to that Fanshaw, of Roebuck Hall. I knew how it was. I saw how his heart was set on it. I understood— remembering what he was like—" " Jack—did you think—l had—forgotten?" "I thought he had overborne you. %know ywhat that sort of steady pressure can. do. But it made me a bit desperate. That was how I came to strike ile. But mine was gold. A lot of gold, Erica. lam a rich man now. I can buy up Fanshaw—at least I can buy up his place if he will sell it. I can clear Dale End of all encumbrances. I can make every- thing right with your father. 0, Erica, my Erica—it seems almost too good to be true. I schooled myself to think of you as the bride of another man: and yet I !fever could believe it. I had had my first nugget—a splendid fellow fashioned into the golden CUP you hold in your hands, any darling. When I looked at it, it gave me hope. Even when I told myself I was bringing it home for your wedding gift, I . still found myself hoping. When I got here and heard that you were free—though being courted by the gen- tleman fron over yonder—my hope changed to triumnh, When I saw voit coming to the tr.)sting place of olct days-1 knew: I threw the cup into yonder bush, and hid myself be- hind another. You found the cup—and called any name. My darling, my darling—look at the rainbows all amongst the hills—and look at the sunlight shining on our Golden Cup!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>INDIGESTION WIND OR A SICK, SOUR STOMACH. •</head>
