        <p>A musical -friend, who travels about the Cr try a little, recently remarked on how each' hind a favourite song; at least &amp;to some for only long residence in each can really' , this. Of course, this is exclusive of the "14 lar" songs, here to-day and forgot to.tooll Ile mentioned that when going through streets the itinerant musician would strike and thereafter his strain would be huult° whistled or the like by the passers-by. way Birmingham readily takes up " Genevle though the hymn "Lead, Kindly Lights''ld haps, has a wider appeal. • Go up to Manch) and the strains of " Mary of Argyle" ore 5 readily taken up, with another Scots min " Annie Laurie" a close second. The latt favoured in Hull—of all places. In the district one often hears "Will ye no vorae,.s again?" while, Carlisle seems to favour T 1 ; Bowling." Durham tells all that "'la conmeAgaiti.• Mere." When the Bad!! crossed the most lugubrious ditties assali- • ear, so we turft to tlie^' South. In Briatoi sprightly "Well of St. Keyne" well lien/ to. Whether it is because Carlisle has Po°. it of its own, Portsmouth has fastened e° " Won't you huY- My pretty flowers." L°l° rd knows only, the latest music hall ditty. , professed himself quite unable to reason of the peculiar attachments. The in Manchester might account for its Chu°, while Leeds may have taken up the niare,t° tune of a Yorkshire regiment, but on tbe re"' could suggest nothing:</p>
