      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>Vogue for Transparencies.</head>
        <p>The Vogue for Transparencies.</p>
        <p>• The daintiest of laces are ennuiugly combined Transparencies are the feature of the hour. with the most fragile of gauzes and other filmy materials. Many of the newest evening gowns consist merely of several layers of\ transparent Materials, and the most shadowy of laces. Fichus are seen on the majority of evening and afternoon corsages, i and these when not corn- iosed of a delicate lace m „are trimmed with it. A couple of lace tunics are'often seen one over the other, and flounced skirts of lace will be much worn in the summer. The lace e 1 13e Isi iinderslip will or wearing ri-ng under transparent lolie mad d shoulde at home an .ebeeeTsfsialietssYhei fashioned e of an fin- ished with a beading and tine lace edging. Quite a• new design for this little garment reached Just above the bust, was perfectly straight all round, and was kept up with ribbon straps over the shoulder, and fastened into the Waist in front and at the back. Narrow pieces of ribbon, after the manner of bracelets, did duty for sleeves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>Concerning Fans.</head>
        <p>Concerning Fans.</p>
        <p>There are several novelties in fans this spring, among which those Made from the rich plumage of tropical birds, or from the peticock, are noticeable. The superstition regarding the latter is evidently dying out. -Not only are pea- cocks' feathers used to make these fans, but Perfect models of the birds in miniature, with outstretched wings and spreading tails, Orna- ment them. Very dainty are the lace fans, richly jewelled. Chicken skin, dressed for the purpose, makes a beautiful ground on which to paint the loveliest of tiny pictures, or groups of charming figures, or again exquisite flowers. When these are the work of a well-known artist they are very costly. The sticks of all the new fans are very ornate, and composed of delicate fretwork, ivory, tortoiseshell, dyed horn, &amp;c. Frequently they are further embellished with precious stones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>Josephine Museum.</head>
        <p>The Josephine Museum.</p>
        <p>The Empress Eugenie, who has been aptly called the Mater Dolorosa of modern times, spent a fortnight at Paris on her way to the South. • One of the most interesting museums in in vile huniere is the Josephine, to which the Empress has contributed tastily valuable relics. Very pathetic was the label attached to last consignment, viz., '`.` From the last ex- Empress to the first.',' Her Majesty has also presented Napoleon's'Walk at MalmaisOn to the nation,' thus preserving it from being covered w ith houses.</p>
        <p>The First Woman Agregie. Among living members of the Legion of Honour is an Englishwoman, Miss Edith Wil- liams, who studied in Paris for her degree, iind, although a foreigner, was the. first WOrnall agregie that is to awarded the highest teach- ing degree in Fl'allef. This lady is a doctor or literature, and was professor of English in two eeoles normales. She lists •earned• international fame, for she founded an international guild, to assist women studying for the agregie, under the shadow of the Sorbonne. A branch of this guild has .lately been opened in London. Miss Wit-</p>
        <p>Continued in re xt cciumn</p>
      </div>
