        <p>.111 "A" MEN BEATEN.</p>
        <p>Rees.' Fine Win.</p>
        <p>. -----...- 4il,liliNerilsgtan gavea alßecreation, at against the St. Helens 3Pt to kljants f.si. and were defeated by point- -19 Min, d by 1111 t ,vitt'oi:-.e last eight of which were seine .. litlh,fles. ;in the second halt. At the _iinet_ervaar! 'tk"llit e.d by 11 points to 2. From tt w eql, ti 4 hoint of vi -it was a disappointing ,'„bllt thF, R ell the .1'- - ees• deserve credit forring 4 1 teao7 win. Through the changes 41%•ted --' tile Warrington "A" 8j(le was Tat th and this proliably helped to bring ,ch th e defeat. tiZtvel t.rst half Glower, Griffiths and Gaskell dropped a goal. All ee 't IV .les and fir nail arr. \l._ 'ngton could reply witii was a. Penalty 1a....-</p>
        <p>goal, which was kicked by Critchley, and the teams turned round at the interval with War- rington 9 points to the bad. It was hoped the "A's" would be able to wipe,out the arrears in the second half, but the hope was not realised. In the second portion Corns and Glover scored tries and McNulty kicked a goal. This ended the list of scoring. Warrington did not play up to their usual standard. They seemed to be lacking in all departments and were not nearly as smart as their opponents, who ran out easy winners by 2 goals b tries (19 points) to I goal (2 points). .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>TEAM FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAM FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Warrington "A" have a visit from Pemberton at Wilderspool to-day. The home side will be: Bramhall; Baker, IL Davis, Steele, Shako- shaft; J. Brown, C. Brown; Naylor, Ila.rmer, Critehley, Green, Bevan, Boardman.</p>
        <p>THE RUNCORN MATCH. The match with Runcorn, which was post- poned on account of frost on New Year's Day, will be played at Runcorn on Wednesday. The kick-off is timed for 5 p.m.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Rylands' Defeat Pendleton. On the Gorsey-lane enclosure before a fair crowd. Stretford, for Rylands', kicked off. Bar- racks and Laws quickly 'made -tracks for the line, but good work by F. Leah and Flanagan took the ball to the other end, Flanagan being pushed into touch. Rylands' attacked again through Warren. Iforrocks and Laws, the last- named scoring, but was brought back for step- ping into touch. At this juncture Laws had to leave the field through injury. Brandwood going on the wing. For obstruction Warren kicked a goal for Rylands'. Half-time arrived with the (two Points); Pendle --- ton, nil.re:ylands% one goal Pendleton commenced the second half, and although playing a man short in the pack, Rylands' had their fair share of the ball, and kept Pendleton on the defensive. Warren, pick- ing up in the loose ran through the whole team, scoring a brilliant try under the posts. The same player goaled. Warren again getting pos- session, passed to 6ttetford, who scored under the posts, Warren goaling. Stretford and nor. were ordered off. Stretford being in. scored for Ryland?. Flanagan and GregorY jured, made the sides level again. The game ended with: Ryland?, three goals four tries (18 ,oints); Pendleton, nil.. Warren, Stretford and „reeks were the pick of the home lot.</p>
        <p>Teams for To-day. Rylands' Recs. play Wardley, at Wardley, with: F. Horrocks; Griffiths, Morris, A. nor- rocks, Searisbriek; Warren, Floyd; Welsby, Whittaker, Brandwood, Morris, Mason, Slater. Ryland' Rees.' "A" meet Warrington Ran- gers, at Gorsey-lane, with: Potter; Hewitt, Riley, Hardman, Nunnerley; Warren, Britton; Peers, Dumbell, Hackett, Carter, Jackson, 8ibby......</p>
        <p>I ger.s,</p>
        <p>SCHOOLBOY RUGBYITES. Cup Tie at Wigan To-day. ' The Warrington boys were drawn away to Wigan in the South-West Lancashire School- boys' Cup Competition, and in order that the best possible team might be selected a practice game was played last Saturday between two teams made up of local boys. Judging from the form displayed the committee have at their dis- posal a wealth of talent, and a well-balanced side should take the field at Wigan. The follow- ing were selected to visit Wigan for to-day's cup-tie : Newall (St. James'); Brady (St. Mary's), Meller (Silver-street), Smith (St. James'), Wat- son (Arpley-street); Skelhorn (St. James'), Ox- ley (Arpley-street); Armitage, Bibby, Lockhart (St. James'), Oraham Nolan (St. Mary's), and Spittle (Arpley-street). Topping and Armstrong (Silver-street) accom- pany 2.m .m nythe rteam a t Central as reserves. The kick-off Tin kick-oraffis timed f leaves Bank Quay at 1.17.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>SCHOOLBOYS' NORTHERN UNION RUBGY</head>
        <p>SCHOOLBOYS' NORTHERN UNION RUBGY</p>
        <p>LEAGUE. Results to date. Points P. W. D. L. For Agt. Pte. Arpley Street ...... 4 3 1 0 47 8 7 St. Mary's 4 5 3 1 1 41 21 7 St. James' Silver Street Heathside 4 1 0 3 8 68 2 Deamont 3 0 0 3 8 48 0 Dolton</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>PICKS FROM THE SCRUM.</head>
        <p>PICKS FROM THE SCRUM.</p>
        <p>Pithy, Pars About the N.U. Gaines.</p>
        <p>Warrington experienced hard luck. Even Hull supporters admitted it. Of course, they were pleased to win, Only they saw the difficulties which Confronted the Wilderspool side. The absence of Jolley undoubtedly Made a considerable difference in The game, and good though Dainteth was, He was not so resourceful as Jolley. Still Dainteth made a bold fight , In an unaccustomed position, and He deserves credit for what he did.</p>
        <p>IlPe 0 0 0 The bright patch was the left wing. The placing of Young as partner To Waywell greatly strengthened that Position and more should be heard of them. They are about matched in speed, And both play the same style of game. Ruddick was not so successful as He usually is, and one bad slip He made when he missed an easy pass. In the tackling work he was a long Way more Sticcessful than in attacking. His partner did not play to him as he might. C)0000</p>
        <p>The "A" men got a trouncing, didn't they? Who would have thought St. Helens Recs. Capable of administering such a defeat? The first three tries were scored For the Recs. by men whose names started with ." G." There seems to he luck in these In the first portion Warrington could only Score two points against the Recs. 11 points. Eight more were added by the Recs. And Warrington retired hopelessly beaten. This will interfere with the chance Of gaining the lead in the Combination table. •</p>
