      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <p>TLIE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 14, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD DISAPPOINTED.</p>
        <p>The first eleven match with Southport was cancelled, owing to the latter being unable to raise a full team.</p>
        <p>The second eleven beat Hyde let at Flowery Field Cricket Ground by 6-1. Owing to th recent rains the ground was very heavy, and the reverse of "flowery." Latchford, however, t. played the correct game from the start by sling- ing the ball about, and, inspired by the strains of a brass band in the vicinity, were leading by 5-0 at the interval. Wilkinson played a dash- ing game at centre-forward and shot four goals in succession, being plied with some good passes from Knight and McCawley on the wings. The fifth goal (scored by W. Starkey with onc of the best shots of the match) made the second eleven's total into 100 for the present season. In the second half Hyde pressed very strongly and the visitors' defence was severely taxed Wild scored for Hyde, and Furness replied with another -for Latchford. Mynekyrue, in the visitors' goal, was safe and made one fine save by arresting a hot shot with his hand as the ball was about to pass under the cross-bar. TEAMS FOR -110-DAY. The first eleven v. Sale "A" at home this afternoon is: J. Bruhl, goal; W. Fletcher and C. Billington, backs; J. Albinson, J. B. Wilkin- son and A. Knight, half-backs; R. A. Dale, F. M. Stoop, J. E. Pearson, A. B. Starkey and H. R. Colton, forwards. Umpire, Mr. F. W. Grounds. Bully-off 3.15 prompt. The second eleven at Bowdon is: 0. Myne- kyme, goal; W. T. Eccles and V. H. Fletcher, backs; P. Allanson, F. C. Newbold and T. Banks, half-backs; J. J. McCawley, W. S. Furness, W. 11. Minards, W. Starkey and C. Vernon, for- wards. Umpire, Mr. 11. Eccles. Train from Arpley Station 1.16 p.m.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH v. WEST DERBY. Stockton Heath were the visitors at West, Derby on Saturday, and playing with only ten men the whole game, ran out winners by four goals to two. Last season the game resulted in a draw of two goals each. From the bully the visitors got into the home circle, the goalkeeper being forced to put be- hind., From the corner, which was only par- tially ' cleared, Bainbridge opened the scoring in the first minute. West Derby now got going, but could not score, the forwards being very poor in the circle, open goals being missed. After 30 minutes Bainbridge scored a second goal for Stockton Heath. Just on time Prince put in a fine run on the left wing and made no mistake with his shot, which gave the goal- keeper no chance. Half-time: Stockton Heath, three goals; West Derby, nil. From a breakaway the home team got into the visitors' circle, Grindley opening the scoring for West Derby. The home team were now having much more of the game and pressed strongly, Robinson adding a second goal. West Derby continued to play up strongly, and only the steady play by the backs prevented them adding te their score. With only a few minutes to play Baxter got possession near his own 25, and put- ting in a fine run shot hard, the goalkeeper, however, clearing. Bainbridge, with a sharp follow up netted before he had time to recover. Result: Stockton Heath, four goals; West Derby, two goals. Stockton Heath complete their list of home 'fixtures to-day, when Western are-the visitors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Associa-</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>NORTON IMPROVES.</p>
        <p>Last week-end did not see the number out that was expected. This was owing to the heavy downpour in the morning of Sunday. Those who took advantage of going out on Saturday after- noon had all the best of it, both in the canal and Gowy. Messrs. Hill and Moore were fairly amongst the fish at the canal, Runcorn way. I saw one bag that contained about Gib. of nice roach, ranging from 40z. to Boz. The same spot on Sunday was a blank.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>GOWY.</head>
        <p>THE GOWY.</p>
        <p>- - - Members who visited the Govry on Sunday were very disappointed, not only with the fish not on the feed, but the stream rose to such an extent that all angling was done with at 2 p.m. It was then impossible to stay any longer, the surrounding meadows being under water. One or two anglers had to wade knee-deep to get through to land again. Saturday was more favourable. All anglers took fish. Some had six, eight, ten, and up to a dozen, and conditions were looking very favourably up to the rain coming.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hassell has kindly consented to allow anglers to fish from his meadows; it is for anglers to shut all gates and leave no paper or bottles about, to keep this privilege.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>N.A.A. EXECUTIVE MEETING. Delegates' Report.</head>
        <p>N.A.A. EXECUTIVE MEETING. Delegates' Report.</p>
        <p>Considerable time was taken up with the minutes of the last meeting, owing to the Leeds and Liverpool Canal agreements, regarding the F.F.P.L. and N.A.A. coming in. In the Secre- tary's correspondence it was mentioned that Warrington desired a later train from Hartford on Sunday evenings. This matter will now go before the L. and N.-W. for consideration. The Gowy was also mentioned, regarding planks being put across at different parts. It is for Chester to make a move..., . .</p>
        <p>The Emergency Committee was appointed, there being five members besides officials elected. The report of the Emergency Committee's correspondence with the F.F.P.L. was very fav- ourable and was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>NATIONAL FEDERATION ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL FEDERATION ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The representatives appointed were Mr. Moor (Bolton) and Mr. Melling (Wigan), it being decided to appoint two fresh representatives each year, thus giving all E.C. members a chance to represent the N.A.A. The Warrington delegate moved that the NA.A_ adopts a badge or button, the motion, finding much favour, and the matter will be dealt with at the first Emergency Committee meeting.</p>
        <p>. NOTICE. A special general meeting will be held at the Bay Horse on Saturday, March 21st, at 8 p.m. All members must make an effort to attend, as the business is most important. A. S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>ELEMENTARY SCHOOLS' LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>ELEMENTARY SCHOOLS' LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Results up to date.</p>
        <p>Oasis.</p>
        <p>P. W. D. L. For Agt. Pte. Wycliffe l4 12 2 0 78 15 26 S. James' Evelyn Street l5 11 0 4 52 19 21 Arpley Street.— 10 6 0 4 27 19 12 Heathslde l3 5 1 7 21 37 11 Parochial ll 4 1 6 28 22 S Penketh 9 2 0 T 9 52 4 St. Barilabas' Bearnont St. Ann's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>By "Linesman."</head>
        <p>By "Linesman."</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District League Great Sankey proved far too goad for Crosfield.s. Owing to the latter having to cancel a North Cheshire League match they were able to greatly strengthen the reserve team, but they failed to do themselves Justice, and had to admit defeat to the extent of six goals to none. The only match in the Second Division was that between St. Barnabas' and Fairfield, at the Lovely-lane ground, where the home team ran out easy winners, scoring five times without any response from the visitors. Minor Medal Competition two goals to one. yfthaßerreiFfinnagteiri met with a surprise at the h neld United Club, suffering tdlealAlefnnetdWas.tt,thecboav e :ton village the home lads met the Bank Quay Recs and gave them a surprising game, leading at half-time by four clear goals. After change of ends the lads continued to have the best of the game, and added a fifth goal, after which they appeared to slacken the efforts, and the Recs. registered their first point by a mistake on the part of the home goalkeeper, and before the final whistle blew they secured two further goals, but in the end they had to retire defeated by five goals to three. The game between Vulcan Mission and the Army Service Corps was keenly contested from start to finish, no g'oals being.scored in the first portion of the game. Early in the second half the Mission drew first blood and very quickly emphasised their superiority by scoring again, and the Army lads responded once a few min- utes before the finish.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM AMATEUR CUP. Second Round. Latchtcurd V. Lostock Gralam. At Thelwall-lane. The Gralamites soon be- came aggressive, but Robinson sent them back and play was transferred to the opposite end, where Fogg hit the upright with Garnett well beaten. Gaining relief from this escape the visitors endeavoured to break down the home defence, but they were stubbornly resisted, and Smith raced away on the right and made a grand centre from near the corner flag. Siddall scored for the home team, Garnett, the visit- ing goalkeeper, being injured in attempting to save. The Gralamites now improved in their tactics, and Weedall tested Rite with a good shot, which was well saved. Following this the home half-back line fed their forwards nicely, and after several shots had been saved by the visitors' custodian, Haspell increased the score for the home team. Latchford led by two goals at the interval, and there was no scoring in the second half, Latchford winning by two goals to nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. ' Great Sankey v. Crosfield's Reserve. On the Penketh Recreation Ground. Both teams were fully represented and from the start the homesters made their presence known to thel visitors' defenders, Ashcroft being quickly called upon to clear. Chesters was also prominent with a good clearance. The visitors now made! a bold effort on their left, but 11. Ditchfield proved too good for them and transferred to the visitors' end. From a goal kick, taken by Ash- croft, the ball struck Poulter on the back. He promptly turned round, gained possession, and sent in a shot which grazed the upright, and strong o thell g opened the score. After a spell of even play, Gardiner was responsible for good work which scoringtlime adseceonedevgeoraoll f lhomeedup tteoo m Banner Tl e visitors attempts to break down the home defence, but were always driven back to their own quarters, and the visitors' citadel was in great danger of being captured when Illidge handled inside the goal area. The free kick brought relief to the visitors fora short time, but the decisive work of the Sankey forwards was too good for the Recs.' defence, and J. Ditchfield was fouled in- side the area when a score seemed certain. 11. Ditchfield took the kick and sent the ball the wrong side of the upright, and shortly after Poulter scored a grand goal from twenty yards' range, the score at the interval being: Great Sankey, three goals; Crosfield's Reserve, nil. Poulter, receiving the ball, beat a couple of opponents and placed to Houghton, who sent in a splendid long shot, which the goalkeeper failed to hold and the home team were four goals ahead. From a free kick, which was well placed by Payne, Gardiner headed towards the goal, and from a clearance Gardiner secured and scored a fifth goal. Glover scored a sixth. Re- sult: Great Sankey, six goals; Crosfield's, none.</p>
        <p>own, sent in a stirviiag allot, which Collier mulled and allowed the ball to roll over the line, thus opening the aware for the Recs. This success gave encouragement be the visitors, sail Deakin, Daviea and Braolshew, by good com- bination, made another attaek' on the home goal. Worrell added a seconsl: point from a free kick. The game was continued in a very tame fashion for a time, the Recs. being hopelessly in arrears. Berry managed to, score again for the Recs., and when time arriva4 the result was: Acton Village, five goals;. Bank Quay Recs., three goals.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLIES.. Emmanuel Institute v. Old Botelerians.</p>
        <p>... _ Played on the King; and Queen ground on Sat- urday. Result: Emmanuel Institute, eight goals; Old Boteleriansi three goals.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL rrrms.</p>
        <p>The tea party and social in aid of Emmanuel Institute Football Club was, very successful, and the club tender. their thanks to all those who have helped to make it so. S. Roberts and A. Hughes, of Emmanuel In- stitute, did well to score thretegoals each against the Old Botelerians.</p>
        <p>W. Newton, Emmanuel Institute's left half- back, played a sterling an last Saturday. Of the Medal Competition, a local poet writes: " Weston" have bidden avlieu to success, For " Fairfield " deserve their great win, I guess; "St. John's" dismissed " Excelsior " this</p>
        <p>-time, I fear, And "St. George's" at last have conquered the "Beer"; "Bank Quay Recs." bowed down to " Acton " last week, " A.S.C." did not get the success they did seek. "Lo-ngford" broke down "Orford Athletic's " pride, And "Emmanuel Old Boys" their skill have not tried. " Weaver Refiners" have made their farewell, For "Fairfield United" have done very well; And I think more surprises are still hold in store, As to who will compete in the Final once more. A.S.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT MEDAL COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>_ A meeting of the above was held on Wednes- day night at the Lion Hotel, Mr. J. Wright presiding. Arrangements for the two remain- ing matches in the second round were as follows!</p>
        <p>To-day.—Lakhford Baptist V. St. George's Mission; Mr. Rule. March 21st.—Fairfield v. St. Barnabas'; Mr. Bennett. The draw for the third round resulted as follows</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist or St. George's Mission v. Longford United. Fairfield or St. Barnabas' v. Fairfield United. Acton Village v. _Mirmanuel Old Boys.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Altrincha-m— -An-late-Jr .C-u-p--Semi-Final Tie at Heatley. Latchford v. Altrincham Lads.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>League. Orford Barracks v. Weaver Refiners. Crosfield's v. Stockton Heath. Fairfield v. Wilderspool Brewery.</head>
        <p>League. Orford Barracks v. Weaver Refiners. Crosfield's v. Stockton Heath. Fairfield v. Wilderspool Brewery.</p>
        <p>WIDNES MINOR MEDAL FINAL.</p>
        <p>_ Owing to the fact that two Warrington clubs are in the final of the above competition, namely, Orford St. John's and St. Barnabas% arrangements have been made with Latchford to play the final tie to-day on the Thelwall-lane ground. A keen struggle is anticipated, and given fine weather, there should be a good crowd to witness the encounter. In a short interview with both captains, Shuttleworth and Meachin both have expressed themselves as confident of winning. The teams will be as follows: Orford St. John's—Forber ; Millward and Smith; Denny, Stretford and Atherton; J. Smith, Tickle, Meachin, Irankinson and Smal- ley. St. Barnabas'—Shaw; Spruce and , Long; Glover, Appleton and Forster; Maddock, Woods, Shuttleworth, Bevan and Vernon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>TEAMS FOR. TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR. TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's meet Bewsey-street Wesleyans, at Orford, with': Woodward; Fair- hurst and Peters; Ravenscroft, Evans and Parr; Hankey, Ford, Starkey, Richards and Yarwood. Padgate United play Emmanuel Old Boys, at Vitoria Park, with: Nork; John Houghton and Sankey; Hodkinson, Critchley and Bennett; James Houghton, Nunnerley, Gilbert, Kenyon and Hardman. Orford Athletic team, to play against Latch- ford United, at Westy-lane, will be chosen from: O'Neill, Butler, Timmins, Fitzgerald, Stanton, Mannion, Rigby, Newton, Oxley, 3lellitt, Nelnms, Fallon, Dooly, Jennings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB. Lieutenant-Colonel Lyon Elected President</head>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB. Lieutenant-Colonel Lyon Elected President</p>
        <p>Many of the "crack shots" of the Appleton Rifle Club assembled at Atkinson's Cafe on Tuesday evening, but they proved themselves to be far from a dangerous community—when they are in general meeting assembled! In the absence of the President-elect (Lieutenant-Col. Lyon),llr. F. J. Grocott presided. Mr. F. J. GROCOTT (the honorary treasurer) submitted his report and stated that the yew had been a very satisfactory one. The balance due to the bank (.24 10s. 2(1.) in 1912 had been converted into a balance standing to the club's credit of ...e7 2s. 2d., whilst the year's net profit amounted to £8 13s. 4d., which was mortgaged by the necessary repairs. The report was adopted. Mr. A. SgEpaRRD (the honorary secretary) pre- sented his annual report, which detailed the year's work. Mr. C. P. Day (match secretary) reported that (luring the year the club had played 21 matches, which resulted in 8 being won, 12 lost, and one drawn. The total number of points for was 26,224, against 26,215, which left the small mar- gin of nine in favour of their club. That did not compare very favourably with the previous: year, when the club took part in 19 matches, 14: of which were won. The difference was ac- counted for to some extent owing to several of the old members not taking part in the matches and the club having to resort to untrained shots. They had had to call upon 24 members to take part in the matches, which was enough for prac- tically three teams. Both secretaries' reports were adopted. A letter was read from Lieutenant-Colonel C. Lyon, in which he said he was glad to do any- thing he could for the cause of good shooting, and agreeing to accept the presidency. He was unanimously elected. The other officers elected were: Vice-presidents, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Messrs. Vere B. Davies, J. J. Whitley, Segai Owen, P. Warburton. and Lieutenant-Colonel Fairclough; honorary treasurer, Mr. F. J Grocott; honorary secretary, Mr. A. Shepherd; armourer, Mr. J. Allardice; auditors, Messrs. F. Gandy and J. Sankey; match secretary, Mr. C. P. Day. The new committee will consist of Messrs. A. Down, R. Thompson, C. P. Day, J. Towers, S. •James. G. H. Ingram, I'. Harrison, J. W. Elliot, J. Hartley, H. Baines, F. Tole, and F. Black.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>Second Division. St. Barnabas' v... Fairfield.</head>
        <p>Second Division. St. Barnabas' v... Fairfield.</p>
        <p>a strong At Lovely-lane. The defence on both sides 'being equal to the attack, the forwards got few chances to score. Howard, in the visitors' goal, to their made several smart clearances. Half-time ar- rived with no score. The second half was opened with Re attack by the home forwards. and Shnttleworth scored the first goal within a couple of minutes,__, repeating the performance directly after. The 1" gFaarai refieladn alatdhse cßoualrdnanboats, sfrweottlearddos7ame down again, when Woods sent in a long shat which deceived the goalkeeper and added the third point. Vernon notched the fourth tleworth scoring the fifth goal. the shut_ sult : St. Barnabas', five goals; Fairfield, nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. Replayed Tie. St. George's Mission v. Wilderspool Brewery. At Longford. At the end of 2.5 minutes' play Marsden opened the score. This served tonic to the home players and they were as a soon swarming round the Brewery goal again. s Hands increased the lead. At the interval the. Mission were leading by two goals to none, and finally won by that score.</p>
        <p>escapes Hard- ing was tackling and clearing in fine style. At yver i7kwelle, pes the Vulcan Mission v. Army Service Corps. This tie was played on the Vulcan ground. hTohme eAfromr yr.% rgdosa lc ohmabdi nminagn half-time there was no score. The second por. tion was started very vigorously. The home tew continued to attack and gained a corner, which was well placed, and Robinson scored with a shot that gave the custodian no chance. A few minutes later °leave added a second goal by li splendid individual effort. Result: Vulcan Mission, two goals; Army Service Corps, nil.</p>
        <p>BankrmerQ. ual,heßegcasm. e had On tAhectgornouVnidliaogfe vth.e fo only been in progress a couple of minutes when Leviis opened the scorefoprast,:es haonmdertueskhimin.gEnd- to-end play followed until Yoxall found himself well placed and he sent the ball past Dainteth and registered the second goal. UP to this point the long swinging tac- tics of the home team had completely Put the Recs. off their game, and now by adopting similar methods they got near their opponents' goal, and were awarded a penalty. Lagar was entrusted with the kick,' which he sent high over the bar. Betley and Hough were playing a fine game in the home rear division, and from a grand pass by Cox. Nixon, the home centre-for- ward, notched a third goal. Directly after Bet- ley scored a fourth from a free kick just outside the penalty area. At half-time the score was: Acton Village, four goals; Bank Quay Recs., nil. The homesters were the, first to become dan- gerous, and Lewis sent in a shot, which Pain- teth failed to hold. The'Rees. appeared to be completely outclassed;' and 4liadshaW and his colleagues received very little support from their half-backs, until Eccleston, going on his.</p>
        <p>-'A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>LEAP IN THE DARK.</head>
        <p>LEAP IN THE DARK.</p>
        <p>Bowlers and the New Green Scheme.</p>
        <p>OPTIMISTIC SPEECHES.</p>
        <p>If the working men of Warrington want bowling greens, Sic., of their own, they must all ombirie and do their level best to attain them. Indeed, this is what one gathered at the annual meeting a the Warrington Works Bowling Association,. held on Monday evening in the ;Torso and Jockey Motel, Winwick-road. There was a good attendance of delegates from the different works; and it was decided to set up a shelcing fund fps the association, partly out of thel pennies which are to be paid by the players for admission to the gieen.</p>
        <p>CHArRMAN RE-ELECTED. Mr. HALFORD loroposed that Mr. J. Willett be the chairman. Ifki thouglit Mr. Willett had held that position since-the initiation of the associa- tion, and he was wall. ac,qariatintecl with the work- ings of -that orgarriiation,.. Sad he was sure they could not find anycerm in. the town more qualified to fill that office. (flbar, hear.) Mr. BP-3WICK RetOritikr.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLErr, in -reply, spolce of the better idea of letting those who attbndecf the annual meet- ing elect the officers. Ile fere more proud be- cause they had appointbd him, for it proved he had the sympathy and' the support of the dele- gates from the firms. My would do all he could to make the association a success, and if he had their hearty support fus.was sure the coming season would not go behind, dime of years past. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>SECRETARY'S; REPORT. Mr. PATRICK submitted 11is annual report, in which he said: "The year lies been a successful one. The entry for the "Finfayson" Cup Com- petition was somewhat less tfisn the season 1912, and several reasons have been put forward to account for this. but I thinli the chief one is the change in the way of handicapping teams that was adopted, in preference to the way of handicapping previously. The public seemed to think we were treading upon treacherous ground, and preferred to wait to see how it worked before entering a team, but, personally —and I believe it is the general opinion of the public—l think it will be a long time before the committee need to look for a fiesh way of han- dicapping teams, as the restirt of last season's competition was such as to give satisfaction all round. Fifty matches were prayed altogether, and out of them 30 matches were won by 20 points or less, 17 matches were won by 10 points or less, and on two occasions the last bowl of the game decided the result of the match, which is cutting the handicap very fine. (Hear, bear.) Rylands' No. 1 team won the final, after an ex- citing game, by six points. St. Helens Cable No. 1 team, their opponents, played manfully. but had to give way to the better team. Rylands' won six matches, with an average of 22 points per match, or less than two a man. St. Helens Cable won five matches, with an average of 11 per match, or less than one a man. The annual handicap, in which we had a record entry of 661 players, was won by J. Spencer, and the second was W. Farrington, both of Pearson and Knowles; 96 prizes being given altogether. The competition and the handicap required twenty Saturdays to cemplete, which, along with the committee meetings, show what a vast amount of work has to be put in by the committee anal officials of the association. _</p>
        <p>"The thanks of the association are again due to Lady Greenall, and our President (Captain George Crosfield); also the vice-presidents and the various works in the town and district, for the unselfish way they respond to our appeal for subscriptions year after year. "Our thanks are also due to Mr. and Mrs. Rushworth for the able manner in which they have catered for us, both on and a the green, the latter being in splendid trim the season through; to the Press for the splendid mannez in which they have reported the progress of the competitions week after week; and last, but not least, to the committee and officials for the un- selfish manner in which they have carried on the work of the association. "During the season we played our annual charity match with the Caledonian Association, and, thanks to the officials of that association. along with our own, we were able to hand over to the Poor Children's Camp ..£6 4.8., and to the Nursing Association £6, which was gratefully received. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>"It is with regret that I have to report the loss during the season of one of our committee men, namely, the late Mr. E. Lunt, of Howley Tannery. In Mr. Lunt the association had a man who carried out his work fearlessly and well, and his loss was very much felt during the remainder of the season.</p>
        <p>"In conclusion, just a word for the effort your committee made during the year to provide recreation grounds of our own. Those efforts did not meet with the success they expected. We have not, however, let the matter drop yet, and we are hoping to be able to make a more favourable report in the near future."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>£6O IN' PRIZES.</head>
        <p>£6O IN' PRIZES.</p>
        <p>-•-- •••---- A long list was submitted by the Secretary, showing the total prizes going to the works and the value of the same. Ninety-six prizes, value in all £6O 35., went to the 30 works, and Pear- son and Knowles headed the list with 15 prizes of the total value-of £l5 Gs. 6d.; Rylands' Bros. won 12 prizes of the value of £8 15s. Mr. Ilumernixze proposed the adoption of the report, and Mr. HOWARTH seconded. . . .</p>
        <p>The balance sheet showed that the year was commenced with a balance in hand of £4 188. 3d. and finished -with .£1 Os. 3d. in the bank. 'The total receipts were as follows: Team competi- tion, £44 ss. 3d.; handicap competition, £72 3s. Gd.; charity match, £l4 11s.; sundry sceipts, 95.; total, £136 is. 6d. The expenditure was: Team competition, £43 3s. 3d.; handicap com- petition, £62 2s. 11d.; charity match, £l4 Bs. 6d.; new scheme, £1 14s. 9c1.•. sundry expenses, £l3 17s. 10d.; balance, £1 08.3 d. Mr. A. E. Brnnrrr, the treasurer, said he re- gretted the balance shown was not larger, but various reasons might be assigned for the diminution. The auditors had written suggest- ing it should he carried forward from year to year to provide against contingencies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>GREEN FOR THEMSELVES.</head>
        <p>A GREEN FOR THEMSELVES.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN explained that the scheme they started in trying to get a home of their own cost a bit of money, and they could not get such things carried through without launching out a bit. (Hear, hear.) After illustrating some of the nasty knocks the committee got, he said that the committee were as economical as possible, and tried to keep expenses down. However, if they were going to make that association a suc- cess, they must give decent prim, and they wanted to give as many as they could. (Hear, hear.) He proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Burdett and the auditors. This Was seconded, and in reply Mr. BrRDETT said the increased price of admis- sion had not been altogether a success. They had given greater value in prizes-1;19 10s. for the first team prize was a large sum—(hear, hear)—but they thought that if they were going to run that association for the workmen of War- rington they must give something in conformity with the entrants. The new scheme was a leap in the dark. They thought they would have got more support from the bowlers, and if they had, then the £1 As. 9d. would have been well in- vested. (Applause.) _ _ - - -</p>
        <p>• Captain G. R Crosfield was re-elected presi- dent of the association; Mr. Patrick was re- elected secretary, and, in reply, said lie was , upset at the results of their efforts to supply a green of their own. It was a pet scheme of his, and always would be. If only the work- ing men would combine together the time was not far distant when they would have a recrea- tion ground, club-room, &amp;c., of their own, and they would not be dependent on Dick, Tom, or Harry for a green. The necessity for subscrip- tions would not be so great if they hid- greens of their own. He believed they would be in such</p>
        <p>a position before very long, lie had not the least doubt but wkat they could get a sum of money to run the association from the Presi- dent, but he thought that if the working men financed the scheme themselves there would be a greater, guarantee of success. They were in communication with the Town Clerk to try and- get a piece of land adjoining Victoria Park, but until the land had been purchased the Corpora- tion could not do anything in the matter. The Town Clerk spoke favourably in regard to the matter, and they had several of the Council at their back. lle hoped the working men would make it a point in their arguments at municipal elections— (hear, hear)—and get the matter pushed forward in their own interests. In re- gard to the decrease in the balance he might say that he was of the opinion that the com- petitors should reap the benefits of the associa- tion in prizes. He felt, however, that they ought to carry a balance forward each year, and provide a sinking fund. If that had been adopted at the beginning they would have had about .e. 50 or .t6O to go towards the new scheme. (Appla.use.) Mr. Burdett was re-elected treasurer, on the proposition of" Mr. A. HARRISON. Masters. R. Straw and T. llorrabin were -re- elected auditors.</p>
        <p>A rww rule to the effect that if a a:lumber of the Esiztutive Committee were ill or died, the man with the next largest number a rotes be appointed was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>FORMS FOR MARKERS.</head>
        <p>FORMS FOR MARKERS.</p>
        <p>It was xLso decided that forms be provided for the markers to sit on, as this would save a great deal of tremble.</p>
        <p>After a }king discussion it: was decided tha; the price off admission be onw penny, and that the players( also pay one penny on entering the green. The' money received from the players will go to the sinking fund. It was also decided to join the Lanes Shire County Bowling Association, and on the argveral advantages being pointed out,, it was thought that it would be a guinea welr spent In conclusion the CnAmass intimated that it was likely they would have an individual merit competition in addition to the handicap. He had henvd a whisper that they would have one during the present season, and he could assure them that if that were so, the prizes would be worth playing far. (Applause.) The following were the committee appointed' with. the number of votes in parenthesis—F. Dean, Whitecross (29); H. Halford, Wire Itopery (31); J. Howarth; Naylorts (22); T. IlumpllreYa. Rylands' (21); I': Dale, Cheshire Lines (2I)); W. Robinson, Howley Tannery (191; A. Peckett, County Asylum (17); J. Smith, Richmond's (19): J. Broadhurst, sen.' Fletcher's (/9); W. Taylor, Porshaw's (22); Si Share, Warrington Tanning Company 00.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded, the meeting.</p>
