      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <head>WHITECROSS WOMEN LIBERALS,</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS WOMEN LIBERALS,</p>
        <p>A Successful _and Pleasant Tea</p>
        <p>and Concert.</p>
        <p>MISS CROSFIELD AND VOTES FOR WOMEN</p>
        <p>The Liberal women of the Whitecross Ward held a pleasant evening in the Thewlis-street Schoolroom on Thursday, on the occasion of a tea meeting and concert. There was a large number present for tea, but the attendance was considerably augmented for the concert.</p>
        <p>Miss TRUDA CROSFIELD, who presided, spoke in a pointed and clear manner upon the two great political questions of Home Rule and the Land Reform, and appealed to the assembly to take a greater interest in the enfranchisement of women. Speaking of the "tremendous and serious question" of Ulster, she said they had really arrived at a most critical point. The Government were doing all they could to bring about an amicable settlement of the trouble, as Mr. Asquith's proposals would show, but Mr. Boner Law and Sir Edward Carson had refused those concessions, so now, if there was any rioting or bloodshed, the blame could not be laid against the Government.</p>
        <p>The Land Question. Another question attracting attention was the land campaign. No end of trouble, and no end of abuses which existed in England to-day, could be traced to the landlords. It had been said that Mr. Lloyd George was attacking the landlords as a class, but that was not so, for he was attacking the system and not the dukes. They had no quarrel with landlords—probably there were just as good men amongst the land- lords as amongst any other class of men—but they were opposed to the present conditions.</p>
        <p>The Women's Question. There wa.s a third subject, which Liberal women were taking an interest in, and that was the proposal to enfranchise women as citizens of the country. She was much surprised the other day to hear that Mr. Walter Long—that typical Conservative—say they wanted intelli- gence to rule the country to-day, and on the score of intelligence, he said, he could not sa.Y that women were less intelligent than men; if anything, he considered they were more intelli- gent- (Cheers, and laughter.) That was further than we ourselves dare go, remarked Miss Crosfield, amid laughter. I am Pleased to say that my brother is more in favour than against the granting of the vote. He was afraid of the ignorance of some women, but he upholds the Liberal principle that women are citizens as well as the men, and if YOU will only feel the same you will encourage him in this view, and if you do not it will not encourage him. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>SOLID WHITECROSS WARD. Mr. THOMAS DALE, in submitting a vote of thanks, referred to the solidarity of Whitecrose Ward to the Liberal cause, and said that if the rest of the town had been of the same mind as their ward, Warrington would not be repre- sented as she was that day. However, they were not thinking about the past, but about the future, when they would be able to repair' that mistake. (Cheers.) The vote was carried Miss Cnosrmn replying. An excellent concert was given, in which the following well sustained their contributions:— Mies Crostield, Miss Lethicke, Mrs. Sanders, Mrs. Phillips, Mr. Sanders and Mr. R. Delaney. Miss Delaney and Mr. R. Delaney accom- panied. During an interval Miss Crosfield presented prizes, which had been kindly given by Mrs. H. Roberts and Mrs. Joseph Hawthorn, to the winners in the home-made bread competition. The successful competitors were Mrs. Holt, 73, Priestley-street, and Mrs. Meachin, 60, Selby- street.</p>
      </div>
