      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>TEMPERANCE WORK.</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE WORK.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Philip Snowden's Racy Speech Last Night.</p>
        <p>"COURAGE OF A COWARD."</p>
        <p>"That this meeting hereby pledges itself to do all in its power to support the Government in their efforts to pass the measure of temper- ance reform at the earliest possible date."</p>
        <p>Such is an extract from a resolution passed unanimously at a meeting of the United King- dom Alliance in the Co-operative Hall last night, when Mr. C. E. Parker presided. Mr. Stephen Walsh, M.P., wired regretting his in- ability to be present owing to a labour dispute. In proposing the reslution, Mr. R. B. Batty (Manchester) said the main principle of that society was to commit that great question to the settlement of the people. They wanted the people to vote in earnest, and not with their eyes shut. He was sanguine about the ,future, and had faith in the Prime Minister.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <head>MRS. SNOWDEN'S CONFESSION.</head>
        <p>MRS. SNOWDEN'S CONFESSION.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Philip Snowden seconded, and said she believed that the liquor traffic would receive its most serious blow if the women of the country were made competeat and equal citizens with men. (Hear, hear.) A moderate beginning nearly always ended in immoderate indulgence. Every drunkard had been a moderate drinker at one time. (Hear, hear.) With her nervous strain, with public meetings, she knew that if she began to take a little drink she did not know where she would stop. Sometimes the temper- ate person was called a coward, and it was not nice to be called that. She had been called a coward because she would not be a militant suffragette. A deputation waited on her more than once and asked her to go to gaol. One asked her to abuse a policeman—the poor inno- cent thing—(laughter)—and another one said if you cannot do it we will stand near you and push you into him. Anything in the world to get her to go to prison for the cause. One of them made a strong appeal to her to make sacri- fices for the cause. "Yes," said Mrs. Snowden, "sacrifice, but sacrifice ourselves, not other people, not even a policeman. For an in- stant the taunt of cowardice got hold of me, and I understood the moderate drinker's posi- tion if he were taunted with cowardice with regard to drink." (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>TWO MEN AND A WOMAN. A Thrilling Sketch at the Hippodrome. Human beings and animals combine to make a great show at the Hippodrome this week. There is not a dull moment in the programme, each turn proves first-class, and each has some characteristic point of merit. "Ace of hearts," a thrilling tale of two men, and, of course, the necessary woman, is a sketch much above the average, both as regards the plot and the act- ing. Duncan's Royal Scotch Collies are much above the average in point of doggie intelligence. The Japanese entertainers furnish a fascinating turn, and the Musical .McGregors more than justify their title as clever musicians. The other turns are also upon a high plane of ex- cellence.</p>
        <p>" Rag-time Outdone" Next Week. The management deserve, praise for the attractive bill due next week. "Rag-time Out- done" will be presented by the talented Castillian Quintet: " I can't shut my mouth" is an absurdity which will make the audience open their mouths—with laughter. Alveretta. Rego and Stoppit are the responsible parties for this bit of fun. The mysterious singing and talking Mr. "C" foots the bill. Other turns include: Jack Stocks, the "trouble shifter"; Lumars, comedy acrobats; Maud Courtney, comedienne; Keir and Kempton, Scottish vocal duo; and Azella Wilson, lady gymnast.</p>
        <p>"SLAVES OF VICE" AT ROYAL COURT. This is one of the many white slave dramas on the road, and tells of the downfall of girls brought about by the agents of vice. It is much on a par with similar plays seen before in War- rington. There is excitement, passion; and, in the end, a thrilling rescue by the girl's lover. The sordid side of the picture is judiciously relieved by a full share of humour, in which Miss Amy Manfru and Mr. Freddie Edwards figure prominently. Miss Carlotta Anson is the heroine, a part she plays well. " Bunty Pulls the Strings" is the attraction for next week, an account of which will be found on another page.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0289" n="art0289">
        <head>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>A good programme is promised at the New Picture House next week, when, from Monday to Wednesday, "From out of the flood" will be shown: For the remainder of the week "The War Makers" will be the star picture. The film depicting the adventures of the recent deputation to Ulster will be shown every even- ing.</p>
        <p>STAR KINEMA HOUSE. The pictures at the Star Kinema House next week will be up to first'-class standard. For the initial part, of the week "The Girl and the Tiger" will be shown, whilst "The Wreelc," a fine drama, will be presented for the latter part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0290" n="art0290">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RSSEILVISD,I A DAY IN THE LIFE OF</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RSSEILVISD,I A DAY IN THE LIFE OF</p>
        <p>A TRAMP.</p>
        <p>I had reached S the night before, footsore, weary, hungry. In the casual ward they gave me a bath, eight ounces of dry bread and a pint of warm water. Then I went to bed on a wide-meshed wire mattress, overlaid with a worn rug, which must have served for generations of tramps. Another rug formed my pillow, a third served for shiiets, blankets, counterpane, and served very badly. Several times during the night I awoke, shivering with cold and with my knees numb. I had gone to sleep lying on my back, at full length, which for a man who had been stretching his legs all day was the least restful attitude. But the narrowness of the bed did not permit of any other posi- tion, whilst I continued awake. But when I was asleep, or half asleep,. I turned on my side, made my legs into triangles, with my 'knees protruding a foot outside the bed, resting on nothing, and the edge bulging into my flesh and stopping the circulation of the blood. So at best my sleep was but an unquiet one, and I had but half rested when the bell rang me up at six next morning.</p>
        <p>IN THE STONE-YARD. "Drinking vessels outside, clothes inside," shouted the labour master, for such clothes as I had were left in a heap outside the locked door of my cell. Then came another eight ounces of bread and another pint of hot water. I ate the bread but washed my face with the water, I was still hungry when the order came to go into the stone-yard. Fif- teen cwts. of stone I had to break and put through a sieve before I could get out and on to the high road once more. I was hungry when I started, I was ravenous when at eleven o'clock I had finished. And now / ask the British public what was Ito do? I was hungry, but the charitable institutions of my country gave me nothing to look for- ward to but eight ounces of bread, and for that I had to wait many hours and walk twenty miles, and the laws of ray country made it a criminal offence for me to ask for anything to eat or the means of buying it. So what was I to do? Why not try work? the cynic will ask, and the indifferent pill agree with him. I was more than ready to try work: I had scores of times offered to give my services in exchange for plain food and any kind of bed. But the very people who are so ready to prescribe work for the tramp would be the last to give him employ- ment.</p>
        <p>CONTENDING EMOTIONS. But I must return to the moment of my exit of the S--- workhouse. A hasty exit it was, for I did not want to be seen in the neighbourhood. I went to the station in the faint hope of being able to get a bag or a par- cel to carry, but my clothes had got into such a condition that I did not look respect- able enough even for that kind of work, and there was a month's stubble on my face. I had a razor in my pocket when I started cut, but that had gone in exchange for a two- penny loaf, and now I saw no prospect of a shave until I fell into work of some kind. A mirror in the window of a furniture shop showed me what a fright I looked, and, with- out knowing that I was speaking aloud, I said: "I wish I could have a shave!" _ .</p>
        <p>_ Then I knew there was a listener by In,' side, a rather dandified young fellow who peared to be waiting for someone. But he had a heart in his body, even though he seemed to be doing his best to conceal the fact.</p>
        <p>"Here's the price of a shave, old man," said he. giving me twopence. "For you do want one badly." I thanked him and walked away, to look for a barber's shop where I could get a hate for a penny. But on the road I was beset with a great temptation: why get shaved at all ? why not spend the twopence on bread ? Hungry as I was, a penny would not give me half a meal, and I had a twenty miles walk before me. The reader who has never been in auoh a plight can but little realise the poignancy of tha'c struggle, be- tween contending emotions. I wanted badly to look as respectable as I could, and I felt I should feel almost happy if the gnawing pain at my stomach could be fully satisfied. Passing a barber's shop, however, I made an impulsive dash through the doer, in spite of the vehement protests of my stom- ach. I was rather pleased with myself when the operation was over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>ON THE ROAD.</head>
        <p>ON THE ROAD.</p>
        <p>I bought a ISennyworth of bread at the last shop on the S—ton road, and as a kind of dis- cipline, began to eat it in only quarter mouthfuls, and I chewed it and ohewed it and rolled it about with my tongue until it had become a liquid and stole down my' throat unknown tome, or in spite of me. In this way I made half my stock last me for three miles, reserving the remainder until later on. Then I pulled out my pipe and though I had three or four matches in my pocket, on the present occasion I preferred to ask a light of a pedestrian I saw aPPreaabillg• I thought that as well as the match he might give me a copper, now that my face was clean. But he did not even give me the match, and barely allowed himself time to tell me that he hadn't one. He had been smoking a few minutes before—but no mat- ter I was able to light my pipe without him, What was of more immediate concern was that 60111 C nails began to make themselves felt in my old boots which were nearly worn out. I sat down by the roadside and using a flagstone as a file tried to wear the ends my patience away. Ifaving done as much a,s would allow I put some sheets of a newspaper insia,, by way of socks and r ed` march- At the end of seven miles the de- mands of my stomach becairii, nso t insistent that I took out the remainder f and ate it, much more hurriedly iedlyhe bread than I had the other portion. I was becoming for it was getting cler totereinethat my reckless would not allow ul nicht.'and I should have to aeh S—ton that when lbeg. I can say this for mveelf that had money even</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>OTHER HARES</head>
        <p>THE OTHER HARES</p>
        <p>Mr. BONA)/ LAW " Look li Mr. Asciurra "How abot [Mr. Asquith, speaking Rule, referrett . familiar."]</p>
        <p>of Homo we are so</p>
        <p>Gazette."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <p>though I had to earn it very hard, I II; rerusedia copper to a poor man, nor did A ways wait to be asked. .3ut this did not make it any easier for to beg when it came to my own turn. d'sed, at the very worst I have seldom 11 able to bring myself to ask for help dire! though I know there are people who give on the direct appeal that always 01( to disregard hints. When about half Journey was complete, I asked for a driat water at a farmhouse. This was given to hut not too graciously, and a hint as to possibility of the existence of some cold tatoes brought the answer that they had, been given to the pigs, and, moreover, the master was not at home. Later on: tried the same experimerr4—a44 was told t was the fourth tramn who had called t day and that there was nothing for me. "Not even a glass of water ?" I asked "Oh, there is a well half a mile down, road, and you may drink as much as you` there," came the answer. I had to put considerable restraint at, self to keen from saying something of felt, but I remembered that at .on was almost a gentleman and marked: "You are very kind." TELEPHONE 3.55 Y.</p>
