        <p>..1111104.444.4.ftimider a GRA PvENHALL • Street Works.—At the fortnightly- meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, it was stated that the Grappenhall street works were proceeding satis- factorily, Land Bought.—An agreement with Admiral Jones Parry and the Runcorn Council for the sale of land for the sewage disposal works of Grappenhall received the seal of the Council at the meeting at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday. Liberal Social.—A tea and concert will be given to-night under the auspices of the Liberal Association in the Methodist New Assembly Room, Grappenhall. The speakers will be Mr. P. M. Oliver and Alderman J. Middleton, of Oldham. Complaint Removed.—At the meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council on Tuesday, at Dutton Workhouse, it was stated that the alleged nuisance at Mr. Pemberton's property in Ackers-lane, Latchford Without, had been abated. A letter was read, in which it was sug- gested that the roadway was high near the site of the complaint, with the result that the water drained on to the land. THELWALL. A New Vicar.—The patron of the living of Thelwall, which is vacant through the death of the Rev. Pym Williamson, has bestowed the living upon the Rev. Lewis H. Tearks, chaplain to a hospital near Windsor. The new vicar will be inducted after Easter. He was educated at Cambridge, and was ordained by the Bishop of Durham in 1895. and has held livings in Pem- brokeshire, the Shetland Islands and in Lon- don. PADGATE. Wesley Guild.—On Monday evening the meet- ing was of a literary character. Selections, both humorous and otherwise, from various authors, were given by several of the Guild members, the readers being Miss Ella London, Miss Jessie Ward, Mr. G. Brough, and Mr. J. H. Mason. Charles Dickens was to the front for the hum- orous portion; three selections from "Pickwick Papers" being read, and these afforded great amusement. The Rev. E. Morgan presided, and at the close thanked, on behalf of those pre- sent, all who had taken part in providing the evening's entertainment.</p>
